//www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"30\" y=\"8\" width=\"10\" height=\"124\" fill=\"#E3D6C2\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><rect x=\"160\" y=\"8\" width=\"10\" height=\"124\" fill=\"#E3D6C2\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><polygon points=\"40,10 47,15 40,20\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,10 153,15 160,20\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,20 47,25 40,30\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,20 153,25 160,30\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,30 47,35 40,40\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,30 153,35 160,40\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,40 47,45 40,50\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,40 153,45 160,50\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,50 47,55 40,60\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,50 153,55 160,60\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,60 47,65 40,70\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,60 153,65 160,70\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,70 47,75 40,80\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,70 153,75 160,80\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,80 47,85 40,90\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,80 153,85 160,90\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,90 47,95 40,100\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,90 153,95 160,100\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,100 47,105 40,110\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,100 153,105 160,110\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,110 47,115 40,120\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,110 153,115 160,120\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"40,120 47,125 40,130\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><polygon points=\"160,120 153,125 160,130\" fill=\"#CDBDA6\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"0.8\"/><rect x=\"56\" y=\"92\" width=\"88\" height=\"10\" fill=\"#C9B79F\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"60\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"60\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"140\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"140\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"44\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"156\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"2\"/></svg>"],1,"圖片題","第三段：繩子拉緊時彈簧保持彎曲、兩端不碰到導軌的齒；<i>If the rope broke, the spring would snap straight, pushing its ends into the teeth</i>，平台被卡住。繩子斷、彈簧伸直卡進齒中的是 (B)。(A) 繩子未斷的正常狀態；(C) 繩斷但彈簧仍彎、平台下墜；(D) 繩子未斷卻卡住。","(A) 繩未斷、彈簧彎曲　(B) 繩斷、彈簧伸直卡住導軌　(C) 繩斷、彈簧仍彎、平台下墜　(D) 繩未斷、彈簧伸直"],["Why did Otis have the rope cut in front of the crowd?",["To test whether the axe was sharp enough","To show how quickly a platform could fall","To entertain people with a dangerous trick","To prove that his platform would not fall if the rope broke"],[],3,"推論（目的）","當時大眾害怕升降機繩子斷裂（第二段）。Otis 當眾砍斷繩子，平台只掉幾英寸就停住，他宣布 <i>All safe</i>，目的就是證明繩子斷了平台也不會墜落，選 (D)。","(A) 測試斧頭夠不夠利　(B) 展示平台能墜落得多快　(C) 用危險特技娛樂觀眾　(D) 證明繩子斷了平台也不會掉下去"]],["1854 年，在紐約市一場大型博覽會上，一群人聚集觀看一場奇特的表演。一位名叫伊萊沙．奧的斯的男子站在一座開放式平台上，平台被一條繩子吊到半空中。就在觀眾屏息之際，他命令助手用斧頭砍斷繩子。平台往下掉了幾英寸──然後停住了。奧的斯脫帽鞠躬，宣布：「各位先生，一切安全，一切安全。」","升降機器已經存在好幾個世紀，但人們害怕它們是有原因的。工廠和倉庫用來吊運貨物的升降機只靠一條繩子懸吊，繩子一斷，平台和上面的一切都會摔到地上。因此很少人願意搭乘，建築也很少超過五、六層樓──差不多是大多數人願意爬的樓梯數。","奧的斯是一家床架工廠的技工，他在打造一台搬運重型設備的升降機時解決了這個問題。他在平台頂端裝上一片堅固的鋼製彈簧，並把它和繩子連在一起。升降井兩側是有齒的導軌，齒就像鋸齒一樣。只要繩子拉緊，彈簧就會保持彎曲，兩端碰不到齒；一旦繩子斷了，彈簧會瞬間彈直，兩端卡進兩側的齒中，把平台鎖在原處。","這場示範讓奧的斯一舉成名。1857 年，第一部裝有他的安全裝置的載客電梯，安裝在紐約一家五層樓的商店裡。之後數十年，電梯先後改以蒸汽和電力驅動，建築師也開始設計愈來愈高的建築。如果沒有安全的電梯，摩天大樓可能永遠不會出現。","現代電梯雖然先進得多，仍然依靠同樣的基本概念：一旦出了問題，車廂應該停住，而不是墜落。"],[["exhibition","n.","博覽會；展覽"],["hold one's breath","phr.","屏息"],["hoist","n.","升降機；起重機"],["shaft","n.","（電梯）井道"],["demonstration","n.","示範"],["skyscraper","n.","摩天大樓"]],329],["近視與「天天戶外 120」（在地健康政策）",["Walk into any classroom in Taiwan, and you will probably see many students wearing glasses. Myopia, or nearsightedness, is a condition in which faraway objects look blurry. In recent decades, it has become extremely common in East Asia. In some cities, around 80 to 90 percent of young adults are nearsighted, and scientists estimate that by 2050 about half of the world's population could be affected.","Myopia usually develops when the eyeball grows too long during childhood. As a result, light from distant objects focuses in front of the retina, the light-sensitive layer at the back of the eye, instead of directly on it. For years, people blamed reading and screens. Close-up work does seem to play a role, but researchers have found that another factor may be even more important: how much time children spend outdoors.","Several studies have shown that children who spend more time outside are less likely to become nearsighted, even if they also read a lot. One explanation is that bright daylight, which can be many times stronger than indoor light, causes the retina to release a chemical called dopamine, which may slow the growth of the eyeball.","Taiwan was among the first places to turn this research into policy. In 2010, the Ministry of Education launched the \"Tian-Tian Outdoor 120\" program, encouraging schools to give students two hours of outdoor activity every day. Some lessons moved outside, and students were urged to leave classrooms during breaks. After decades of steady increases, the share of elementary school students with poor eyesight began to fall.","Experts also recommend the \"20-20-20 rule\" for anyone who must use screens: every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet (six meters) away for 20 seconds. Regular eye checks are important too, since severe myopia raises the risk of serious eye diseases later in life. The message is simple: sometimes the best medicine for your eyes is a walk in the sun."],[["What is the main purpose of the passage?",["To explain the causes of myopia and how outdoor time may help","To advertise a new kind of glasses for students","To compare the schools of Taiwan and other countries","To argue that students should stop reading books"],[],0,"寫作目的","第一段指出近視普遍，第二段說明成因，第三段說明戶外活動為何有幫助，第四段介紹台灣政策，末段提供建議。目的在說明近視成因與戶外活動的幫助，選 (A)。(D) 文中說愛讀書的孩子只要多到戶外也較不易近視。","(A) 說明近視的成因以及戶外時間如何可能有幫助　(B) 推銷一種新的學生眼鏡　(C) 比較台灣與他國的學校　(D) 主張學生不該再讀書"],["According to the passage, what happens in a nearsighted eye?",["The eyeball becomes too short.","The retina produces too much dopamine.","Light cannot enter the eye at all.","Light from far away focuses in front of the retina."],[],3,"細節","第二段：眼球在成長期變得太長，<i>light from distant objects focuses in front of the retina</i>，選 (D)。(A) 是太長不是太短；(B) 多巴胺反而可能減緩眼球變長。","(A) 眼球變得太短　(B) 視網膜分泌太多多巴胺　(C) 光完全無法進入眼睛　(D) 遠方的光聚焦在視網膜前方"],["What did the \"Tian-Tian Outdoor 120\" program encourage?",["Two hours of reading every day","Eye checks every 120 days","Two hours of outdoor activity every day","Using screens for less than 120 minutes a week"],[],2,"細節","第四段：教育部推動「天天戶外 120」，鼓勵學校 <i>give students two hours of outdoor activity every day</i>，120 指 120 分鐘，選 (C)。","(A) 每天閱讀兩小時　(B) 每 120 天檢查一次眼睛　(C) 每天兩小時戶外活動　(D) 每週使用螢幕少於 120 分鐘"],["Which of the following follows the \"20-20-20 rule\"?",["A student reads 20 pages and then sleeps for 20 minutes.","After 20 minutes on a tablet, a student looks at a tree six meters away for 20 seconds.","A student holds her phone 20 centimeters from her eyes.","A student closes his eyes for 20 minutes every 20 hours."],[],1,"應用推論","末段：<i>every 20 minutes, look at something about 20 feet (six meters) away for 20 seconds</i>。(B) 用平板 20 分鐘後，看六公尺外的樹 20 秒，完全符合。","(A) 讀 20 頁後睡 20 分鐘　(B) 用平板 20 分鐘後，看六公尺外的樹 20 秒　(C) 把手機拿在離眼睛 20 公分處　(D) 每 20 小時閉眼 20 分鐘"]],["走進台灣任何一間教室，你大概都會看到很多戴眼鏡的學生。近視是一種看遠處物體會模糊的狀況。近幾十年來，近視在東亞變得極為普遍。在某些城市，約八到九成的年輕人有近視，科學家估計到了 2050 年，全世界可能有一半的人口受到影響。","近視通常是因為眼球在兒童時期長得太長。結果，來自遠處物體的光線聚焦在視網膜（眼球後方感光的那一層）前方，而不是正好落在上面。多年來，人們把近視歸咎於讀書和螢幕。近距離用眼似乎確實有影響，但研究人員發現，另一個因素可能更重要：孩子待在戶外的時間有多長。","好幾項研究顯示，在戶外待得比較久的孩子比較不容易近視，即使他們也很愛讀書。一種解釋是，戶外的日光比室內光線強上好幾倍，會讓視網膜釋放一種叫多巴胺的化學物質，而多巴胺可能會減緩眼球的生長。","台灣是最早把這項研究化為政策的地方之一。2010 年，教育部推出「天天戶外 120」計畫，鼓勵學校讓學生每天有兩小時的戶外活動。有些課程移到戶外上，下課時間也鼓勵學生離開教室。在持續上升數十年之後，國小學生視力不良的比率開始下降。","專家也建議必須使用螢幕的人遵守「20-20-20 法則」：每 20 分鐘，看約 20 英尺（六公尺）外的東西 20 秒。定期檢查眼睛也很重要，因為高度近視會提高日後罹患嚴重眼疾的風險。訊息很簡單：有時候，對眼睛最好的藥，就是到陽光下走一走。"],[["nearsighted","adj.","近視的"],["blurry","adj.","模糊的"],["retina","n.","視網膜"],["blame","v.","歸咎"],["policy","n.","政策"],["severe","adj.","嚴重的"]],322],["橡皮鴨艦隊與洋流研究（1992 年起的經典海洋故事）",["In January 1992, a cargo ship traveling from Hong Kong to the United States ran into a fierce storm in the North Pacific. Several containers fell overboard. One of them held 28,800 plastic bath toys—yellow ducks, red beavers, blue turtles, and green frogs. When the container broke open, the toys spilled out and began an unexpected journey across the world's oceans.","Ten months later, the first toys started washing up on the coast of Alaska, thousands of kilometers from where they had fallen into the sea. People who found them reported their discoveries, and the story caught the attention of an oceanographer named Curtis Ebbesmeyer. He realized that the toys offered a rare opportunity. Scientists had long studied ocean currents by releasing bottles or cards and waiting for people to find them. But few experiments involved thousands of identical objects released at a single known time and place. Here was one that had happened by accident.","Working with a colleague who had built a computer model of ocean currents, Ebbesmeyer used reports of where and when the toys landed to test and improve the model. The data helped show how floating objects are carried by huge circular currents known as gyres. Some toys circled the North Pacific for years. Others are believed to have drifted north into the Arctic, where they became trapped in ice before slowly moving toward the Atlantic.","Over time, the toys changed. The sun and salt water bleached their bright colors until many of the ducks were almost white. As news of their adventure spread, the toys became collectors' items, and a single one found on a beach could sell for hundreds of dollars.","The story is charming, but it also carries a serious message. Every year, hundreds or even thousands of shipping containers are lost at sea, and much of the plastic they carry never fully breaks down. The same currents that carried the ducks around the world also carry plastic waste, which collects in the gyres and forms huge patches of floating garbage."],[["Which of the following is the best title for the passage?",["How to Clean Plastic Toys","Bath Toys That Helped Scientists Study the Sea","The Dangers of Sailing in Storms","Why Collectors Love Rubber Ducks"],[],1,"標題","文章敘述 1992 年落海的洗澡玩具漂流各地，被海洋學家用來研究、改進洋流模型，最後連結到塑膠汙染。核心是「幫助科學家研究海洋的玩具」，選 (B)。(D) 只是第四段的細節。","(A) 如何清潔塑膠玩具　(B) 幫助科學家研究海洋的洗澡玩具　(C) 在暴風雨中航行的危險　(D) 收藏家為何愛橡皮鴨"],["Why were the toys especially useful to scientists?",["They were made of a material that never changes color.","They carried tiny tracking devices.","They were thousands of identical objects released at a known time and place.","They floated faster than any other object."],[],2,"細節（原因）","第二段：過去很少實驗涉及 <i>thousands of identical objects released at a single known time and place</i>，這批玩具正好符合，選 (C)。(A) 第四段說玩具會被曬到褪色；(B) 未提及。","(A) 它們的材質永遠不會變色　(B) 它們裝有微型追蹤器　(C) 它們是在已知時間地點落海的數千個相同物體　(D) 它們漂得比任何物體都快"],["What does the word \"bleached\" in the fourth paragraph most likely mean?",["made heavier","made more valuable","broke into pieces","made lighter in color"],[],3,"字義推論","上下文：陽光和海水把鮮豔的顏色 bleached，<i>until many of the ducks were almost white</i>，鴨子幾乎變成白色，可知是「使褪色、漂白」，選 (D)。","(A) 使變重　(B) 使更有價值　(C) 碎成片片　(D) 使顏色變淡"],["What is the purpose of the last paragraph?",["To connect the story to the problem of plastic pollution","To explain how bath toys are made in factories","To describe the work of collectors","To suggest that ships should not carry toys"],[],0,"段落功能","末段以 <i>it also carries a serious message</i> 轉折：貨櫃落海、塑膠難以分解，洋流把塑膠垃圾帶進環流形成垃圾帶。這段把有趣的故事連結到塑膠汙染問題，選 (A)。","(A) 把故事連結到塑膠汙染問題　(B) 說明工廠如何製造洗澡玩具　(C) 描述收藏家的工作　(D) 建議船隻不要載運玩具"]],["1992 年 1 月，一艘從香港開往美國的貨輪在北太平洋遇上猛烈的暴風雨，好幾個貨櫃掉進海裡。其中一個貨櫃裝著 28,800 個塑膠洗澡玩具──黃色小鴨、紅色河狸、藍色烏龜和綠色青蛙。貨櫃破裂後，玩具散落出來，展開了一趟橫越世界各大洋、意想不到的旅程。","十個月後，第一批玩具開始被沖上阿拉斯加的海岸，距離它們落海的地方有數千公里遠。撿到的人回報了他們的發現，這件事引起海洋學家柯蒂斯．艾比斯梅爾的注意。他意識到這批玩具提供了難得的機會。長久以來，科學家研究洋流的方式是投放瓶子或卡片，等人撿到；但很少有實驗是在同一個已知的時間和地點，投放數千個一模一樣的物體。而現在，這樣的實驗意外發生了。","艾比斯梅爾和一位建立了洋流電腦模型的同事合作，利用玩具上岸的地點和時間的回報，來檢驗並改進這個模型。這些資料幫助說明了漂浮物如何被稱為「環流」的巨大循環洋流帶著走。有些玩具在北太平洋繞了好幾年；另一些據信往北漂進北極，被困在冰裡，再慢慢移向大西洋。","隨著時間過去，玩具也變了樣。陽光和海水讓它們鮮豔的顏色褪去，許多小鴨幾乎變成白色。隨著它們的冒險故事傳開，這些玩具成了收藏品，在海灘上撿到的一隻就可能賣到數百美元。","這個故事很討喜，卻也帶著嚴肅的訊息。每年有數百、甚至數千個貨櫃在海上遺失，而它們載運的塑膠大多永遠無法完全分解。把小鴨帶到世界各地的洋流，也同樣帶著塑膠垃圾，這些垃圾聚積在環流中，形成巨大的海上垃圾帶。"],[["overboard","adv.","落水；從船上掉入海中"],["oceanographer","n.","海洋學家"],["identical","adj.","完全相同的"],["current","n.","洋流；水流"],["bleach","v.","使褪色；漂白"],["break down","phr.","分解"]],340]]],[11,[["京都櫻花 1,200 年來最早盛開（2021 年氣候新聞）",["In Japan, the blooming of cherry blossoms, or sakura, is more than a beautiful sight. It is a national event. Each spring, weather agencies issue forecasts of when the flowers will open, and millions of people gather under the trees for hanami, or flower-viewing parties. In recent years, however, the trees have been sending a worrying message.","In 2021, the cherry trees in Kyoto reached full bloom on March 26, earlier than in any previous year in more than 1,200 years of records. How do we know? For centuries, emperors, nobles, and monks in the old capital wrote down the dates of cherry-blossom festivals in diaries and official records. By collecting these documents, a Japanese scientist named Yasuyuki Aono built a record of full-bloom dates going back to the year 812, one of the longest records of its kind in the world.","The data reveal a clear pattern. For most of that history, the trees reached full bloom around mid-April, with the date moving back and forth from year to year depending on the weather. Since the 1800s, however, the date has been getting steadily earlier, and the change has sped up in recent decades. Today, the trees in Kyoto usually bloom more than a week earlier than they did for most of those centuries.","The main reason is rising temperatures. Cherry trees respond closely to warmth in February and March: the warmer those months are, the sooner the buds open. Scientists believe two factors are at work in Kyoto. One is global climate change. The other is the \"urban heat island\" effect, in which cities become warmer than the countryside because buildings and roads absorb and release heat.","Earlier blooms may seem harmless, but they can cause problems. Festivals planned months ahead may miss the flowers. Some scientists also worry that if winters become too warm, the trees may not get the cold period they need, leading to weaker blooms. The ancient diaries, written for poetry and celebration, have become an unexpected tool for understanding our changing climate."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How to plan a flower-viewing party in Japan","What Kyoto's cherry-blossom records reveal about a warming climate","Why Japanese emperors wrote diaries","How cherry trees are grown in cities"],[],1,"主旨","第二段寫 2021 年京都櫻花創 1,200 年來最早紀錄，以及古代日記如何構成長期紀錄；第三、四段分析趨勢與原因（氣溫上升）；末段說古代日記成了理解氣候變遷的工具。選 (B)。","(A) 如何在日本規劃賞花派對　(B) 京都櫻花紀錄透露的氣候暖化訊息　(C) 日本天皇為何寫日記　(D) 櫻花樹如何在城市中種植"],["How do scientists know that the 2021 bloom was earlier than any before it in over 1,200 years?",["They studied the rings inside old cherry trees.","They used computer models of ancient weather.","They asked elderly people in Kyoto.","They collected dates from old diaries and official records."],[],3,"細節","第二段：天皇、貴族與僧侶在日記和官方紀錄中寫下賞櫻活動的日期，青野靖之 <i>By collecting these documents</i> 建立了回溯到 812 年的紀錄，選 (D)。","(A) 研究老櫻花樹的年輪　(B) 使用古代天氣的電腦模型　(C) 詢問京都的老人家　(D) 從古代日記與官方紀錄收集日期"],["Which graph best shows the trend in Kyoto's full-bloom dates described in the passage?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 128\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"38\" y1=\"14\" x2=\"38\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"38\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polyline points=\"38,35.32 48,30.39999999999999 58,37.78 68,32.04 78,36.14 88,31.22 98,36.96 108,32.86 118,34.5 128,32.04 138,36.96 148,33.68 158,40.239999999999995 168,50.9 178,64.84 188,84.52\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">later</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">earlier</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">812</text><text x=\"113\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1400</text><text x=\"188\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 128\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"38\" y1=\"14\" x2=\"38\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"38\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polyline points=\"38,18.10000000000001 48,22.200000000000003 58,25.480000000000004 68,28.760000000000005 78,30.39999999999999 88,35.32 98,38.6 108,41.879999999999995 118,45.160000000000004 128,50.08 138,53.36 148,58.28 158,63.199999999999996 168,69.75999999999999 178,75.5 188,81.24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">later</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">earlier</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">812</text><text x=\"113\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1400</text><text x=\"188\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 128\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"38\" y1=\"14\" x2=\"38\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"38\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polyline points=\"38,71.4 48,66.48 58,73.03999999999999 68,68.12 78,71.4 88,67.3 98,72.22 108,68.94 118,70.58 128,68.12 138,63.199999999999996 148,55 158,46.800000000000004 168,36.96 178,27.120000000000005 188,18.10000000000001\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">later</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">earlier</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">812</text><text x=\"113\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1400</text><text x=\"188\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 128\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"38\" y1=\"14\" x2=\"38\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"38\" y1=\"96\" x2=\"188\" y2=\"96\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polyline points=\"38,20.560000000000002 48,27.120000000000005 58,33.68 68,41.879999999999995 78,48.440000000000005 88,55 98,59.92 108,63.199999999999996 118,61.56 128,64.02 138,62.38 148,63.199999999999996 158,64.84 168,62.38 178,64.02 188,63.199999999999996\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"34\" y=\"22\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">later</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">earlier</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">812</text><text x=\"113\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">1400</text><text x=\"188\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2021</text></svg>"],0,"圖片題（折線圖）","第三段：長期以來盛開日在四月中旬上下波動（線段大致持平、有起伏），<i>Since the 1800s ... the date has been getting steadily earlier</i>，近幾十年更快。持平後在末段明顯往「earlier」下降的是 (A)。(B) 一路下降；(C) 變晚；(D) 早期下降後持平，都不符。","(A) 長期持平後近代快速提早　(B) 從 812 年起一路提早　(C) 近代變晚　(D) 早期提早、近代持平"],["What does the last sentence of the passage suggest?",["Ancient poems are more accurate than modern data.","Scientists should learn to write poetry.","Old records written for other purposes can help modern science.","Climate change first began in ancient Japan."],[],2,"推論（句意）","末句：這些為詩歌與慶典而寫的古代日記，<i>have become an unexpected tool for understanding our changing climate</i>。原本為別的目的而寫的紀錄，如今幫助了現代科學，選 (C)。","(A) 古詩比現代資料更準確　(B) 科學家應學寫詩　(C) 為其他目的寫下的舊紀錄也能幫助現代科學　(D) 氣候變遷最早始於古代日本"]],["在日本，櫻花盛開不只是美景，更是全國性的大事。每年春天，氣象單位會發布開花預報，數以百萬計的人聚在樹下「花見」，也就是舉辦賞花派對。然而近年來，櫻花樹一直在傳遞令人憂心的訊息。","2021 年，京都的櫻花在 3 月 26 日滿開，比 1,200 多年紀錄中的任何一年都早。我們怎麼知道？幾百年來，古都的天皇、貴族和僧侶在日記與官方紀錄中寫下賞櫻活動的日期。日本科學家青野靖之蒐集這些文獻，建立了一份可回溯到西元 812 年的滿開日期紀錄，是世界上同類紀錄中最長的之一。","資料呈現出清楚的模式。在這段歷史的大部分時間裡，櫻花大約在四月中旬滿開，日期隨著每年的天氣前後變動。然而自 1800 年代起，滿開日期持續提早，近幾十年更是加速。如今，京都櫻花通常比那幾個世紀的大部分時候提早一週以上盛開。","主要原因是氣溫上升。櫻花樹對二、三月的溫暖非常敏感：這兩個月愈暖和，花苞就愈早綻放。科學家認為京都有兩個因素在起作用：一是全球氣候變遷，二是「都市熱島效應」──建築和道路吸收並釋放熱能，使城市比鄉間更熱。","櫻花提早開似乎無傷大雅，卻可能帶來問題。好幾個月前就規劃好的祭典可能錯過花期。有些科學家也擔心，如果冬天變得太暖，櫻花樹可能得不到所需的低溫期，導致開花變差。這些為了詩歌與慶典而寫下的古代日記，意外成了理解氣候變遷的工具。"],[["bloom","n./v.","開花"],["forecast","n.","預報"],["noble","n.","貴族"],["reveal","v.","顯示；揭露"],["respond to","phr.","對…有反應"],["absorb","v.","吸收"]],339],["幸運籤餅的日本身世（飲食文化經典題材）",["At the end of a meal in a Chinese restaurant in the United States, diners usually receive a small folded cookie with a slip of paper inside. The message might offer advice (\"Patience is a virtue\"), a prediction (\"Good news will come to you soon\"), or a list of \"lucky numbers.\" Many Americans assume fortune cookies are a Chinese tradition. In fact, they are almost unknown in China—and their roots appear to lie in Japan.","Researchers have found that bakeries near Kyoto have long made a similar cookie called tsujiura senbei. It is larger and darker than the American version and flavored with sesame and miso rather than vanilla, and the fortune is tucked into the fold rather than placed inside. A Japanese researcher even discovered a drawing from 1878 showing a baker making these cookies.","The cookies probably arrived in the US with Japanese immigrants in the late 1800s and early 1900s. Several people have claimed to be the inventor, including Makoto Hagiwara, who ran the Japanese Tea Garden in San Francisco and served the cookies there. In 1983, a mock trial in San Francisco even \"ruled\" that the city was the cookie's birthplace, though the decision had no legal power.","How, then, did fortune cookies become linked with Chinese food? During World War II, many Japanese Americans were forced to leave their homes and live in camps. Japanese-owned bakeries closed, and Chinese-American makers took over production. At the same time, Chinese restaurants were growing popular across the country, and the cookies became a cheap, fun way to end a meal.","Today, one company in New York alone produces millions of fortune cookies a day. The messages can be surprisingly powerful. In 2005, 110 people across the US won second prize in a national lottery—an unusually large number. Officials suspected cheating until they learned that the winners had all picked numbers printed in fortune cookies. As for whether the fortunes ever come true, that is another question."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How to bake fortune cookies at home","Why Chinese restaurants are popular in America","The surprising history of the fortune cookie","How the US lottery works"],[],2,"主旨","首段點出一般人以為籤餅來自中國，其實根源在日本；接著寫日本的辻占煎餅、傳入美國、二戰後與中餐連結，最後談現況與趣聞。主旨是籤餅出人意料的歷史，選 (C)。","(A) 如何在家烤籤餅　(B) 中餐館在美國為何受歡迎　(C) 幸運籤餅出人意料的歷史　(D) 美國樂透如何運作"],["Which of the following is true about tsujiura senbei?",["The fortune is placed in the fold of the cookie.","It is flavored mainly with vanilla.","It was first made in San Francisco in 1983.","It is smaller and lighter in color than the American version."],[],0,"細節","第二段：辻占煎餅的籤紙 <i>tucked into the fold rather than placed inside</i>，選 (A)。(B) 是芝麻與味噌口味；(C) 1983 年是舊金山的模擬審判；(D) 它比美國版更大、顏色更深。","(A) 籤紙夾在餅的摺縫中　(B) 主要是香草口味　(C) 1983 年首創於舊金山　(D) 比美國版小、顏色淺"],["According to the passage, how did fortune cookies become linked with Chinese restaurants?",["A Chinese emperor ordered bakers to make them.","Chinese immigrants brought the recipe from China.","A court in San Francisco gave the cookies to Chinese bakers.","Japanese bakeries closed during World War II, and Chinese-American makers took over."],[],3,"細節（因果）","第四段：二戰期間日裔美國人被迫遷入營區，<i>Japanese-owned bakeries closed, and Chinese-American makers took over production</i>，加上中餐館普及，籤餅就和中餐連在一起，選 (D)。","(A) 中國皇帝命令烘焙師製作　(B) 華人移民從中國帶來配方　(C) 舊金山法院把籤餅交給華人烘焙師　(D) 二戰時日裔麵包店關閉，由華裔業者接手"],["Why did lottery officials suspect cheating at first in 2005?",["The winners all lived in New York.","An unusually large number of people won the same prize.","The winning numbers were printed in a newspaper.","The prize money had been stolen."],[],1,"推論","末段：110 人同時贏得二獎，是 <i>an unusually large number</i>，數量異常，官員因而懷疑作弊，後來才發現他們都用了籤餅上的號碼，選 (B)。","(A) 得獎者都住在紐約　(B) 同時贏得同一個獎的人數異常地多　(C) 中獎號碼刊在報紙上　(D) 獎金被偷了"]],["在美國的中餐館用完餐，客人通常會拿到一個摺起來的小餅乾，裡面夾著一張紙條。紙條上可能是建議（「耐心是一種美德」）、預言（「好消息很快就會降臨」），或一串「幸運號碼」。許多美國人以為幸運籤餅是中國傳統。事實上，它在中國幾乎沒人知道──它的根源似乎在日本。","研究人員發現，京都附近的糕餅店很早就在做一種類似的餅乾，叫做「辻占煎餅」。它比美國版更大、顏色更深，用芝麻和味噌而不是香草調味，而且籤紙是夾在摺縫裡，而不是放在餅乾裡面。一位日本研究者甚至找到一幅 1878 年的圖畫，畫著一位師傅正在做這種餅乾。","這種餅乾大概是在 1800 年代末到 1900 年代初，隨著日本移民傳入美國。好幾個人都自稱是發明者，包括在舊金山經營日本茶園、在那裡供應這種餅乾的萩原真。1983 年，舊金山的一場模擬審判甚至「判定」舊金山是籤餅的發源地，不過這項判決並沒有法律效力。","那麼，幸運籤餅是怎麼和中國菜扯上關係的？二次世界大戰期間，許多日裔美國人被迫離開家園，住進收容營。日本人經營的糕餅店關門，華裔美國業者接手了生產。同時，中餐館在全美愈來愈受歡迎，籤餅便成了一種便宜又有趣的餐後點心。","如今，光是紐約的一家公司，每天就生產數百萬個幸運籤餅。紙條上的訊息有時威力驚人。2005 年，全美有 110 人同時贏得一項全國樂透的二獎──人數異常地多。官員起初懷疑有人作弊，後來才發現，這些得獎者全都選了印在幸運籤餅上的號碼。至於籤上的預言會不會成真，那又是另一回事了。"],[["slip","n.","（紙）條"],["virtue","n.","美德"],["assume","v.","以為；假定"],["tuck","v.","塞進；夾入"],["immigrant","n.","移民"],["suspect","v.","懷疑"]],330],["澳洲禁止 16 歲以下使用社群媒體（2025 年 12 月上路）",["On December 10, 2025, Australia became the first country in the world to ban children under 16 from having accounts on major social media platforms. Under the new law, companies such as TikTok, Instagram, Snapchat, Facebook, X, and YouTube must take \"reasonable steps\" to stop younger users from creating or keeping accounts. Companies that fail to do so can be fined up to 49.5 million Australian dollars. Children and their parents, however, are not punished.","The government argued that social media had become harmful to young people. Supporters of the law pointed to cyberbullying, harmful content, and the way apps are designed to keep users scrolling for hours. Many parents welcomed the ban, saying that a rule that applies to everyone makes it much easier for them to say no to their children.","Critics, however, raised several concerns. Some experts warned that teenagers would simply find ways around the rules, such as using a parent's account or lying about their age. Others worried that the ban could cut off young people who rely on online communities for support, especially those who feel lonely in their daily lives. There were also questions about privacy, since checking ages may require users to share personal information, such as photos of their faces.","The first results came quickly. In January 2026, the government reported that about 4.7 million accounts had been removed or restricted in the first month. Yet officials also admitted that many under-16s were still managing to stay online, and that it was too early to say whether the law was working as intended.","Other countries are watching closely, and several governments, including some in Europe, have discussed similar age limits. Whatever the final result, Australia's experiment has raised a question that families everywhere are asking: at what age are young people ready for social media—and who should decide?"],[["Under Australia's new law, who can be fined?",["Social media companies","Children under 16","Parents of young users","Schools that allow phones"],[],0,"細節","第一段：未採取合理措施的 <i>Companies</i> 最高可罰 4,950 萬澳幣，而 <i>Children and their parents ... are not punished</i>，選 (A)。","(A) 社群媒體公司　(B) 16 歲以下的孩子　(C) 年輕使用者的父母　(D) 允許使用手機的學校"],["Which of the following is NOT one of the critics' concerns?",["Teenagers might find ways around the rules.","Some young people could lose sources of support.","Checking ages might threaten users' privacy.","The ban would make smartphones more expensive."],[],3,"細節（NOT）","第三段批評者的擔憂：青少年會設法規避（A）、可能切斷依賴網路社群支持的年輕人（B）、年齡驗證涉及隱私（C）。文中沒提到手機變貴，選 (D)。","(A) 青少年可能設法規避規定　(B) 有些年輕人可能失去支持的來源　(C) 驗證年齡可能威脅隱私　(D) 禁令會讓智慧型手機變貴"],["What can be inferred from the fourth paragraph?",["The law has completely solved the problem.","The success of the law is still uncertain.","Very few accounts were affected by the law.","Children under 16 have stopped using the internet."],[],1,"推論","第四段：雖然第一個月約 470 萬個帳號被移除或限制，但官員也承認許多未滿 16 歲的人仍在線上，<i>it was too early to say whether the law was working</i>。可知成效仍不確定，選 (B)。","(A) 法律已徹底解決問題　(B) 這項法律是否成功仍不確定　(C) 受影響的帳號很少　(D) 16 歲以下的孩子已不再上網"],["What is the passage mainly about?",["How to protect personal information online","Why Australian teenagers prefer TikTok","A new law and the debate surrounding it","How social media companies make money"],[],2,"主旨","第一段介紹澳洲新法，第二段是支持者的理由，第三段是批評者的擔憂，第四段是初步結果，末段談各國關注與留下的問題。全文介紹一項新法及其引發的討論，選 (C)。","(A) 如何在網路上保護個資　(B) 澳洲青少年為何偏愛抖音　(C) 一項新法以及圍繞它的爭論　(D) 社群媒體公司如何賺錢"]],["2025 年 12 月 10 日，澳洲成為全世界第一個禁止 16 歲以下兒童在主要社群媒體平台擁有帳號的國家。根據新法，TikTok、Instagram、Snapchat、Facebook、X 和 YouTube 等公司必須採取「合理措施」，阻止未滿 16 歲的使用者建立或保留帳號。未能做到的公司，最高可被罰款 4,950 萬澳幣。不過，兒童和他們的父母不會受罰。","政府主張，社群媒體已經對年輕人造成傷害。支持者舉出網路霸凌、有害內容，以及應用程式讓使用者一滑就是好幾個小時的設計。許多家長歡迎這項禁令，他們說，一條適用於所有人的規定，讓他們更容易對孩子說「不」。","然而，批評者提出了幾項疑慮。有些專家警告，青少年只會想辦法規避規定，例如使用父母的帳號或謊報年齡。也有人擔心，禁令可能切斷那些仰賴網路社群獲得支持的年輕人，尤其是在日常生活中感到孤單的人。此外還有隱私的問題，因為驗證年齡可能需要使用者提供個人資訊，例如臉部照片。","初步結果很快就出爐。2026 年 1 月，政府公布第一個月約有 470 萬個帳號被移除或限制使用。但官員也承認，許多未滿 16 歲的人仍設法留在網路上，現在要判斷這項法律是否達到預期效果還言之過早。","其他國家正密切關注，包括歐洲在內的好幾個國家政府都討論過類似的年齡限制。無論最後結果如何，澳洲的這項實驗都提出了一個世界各地家庭都在問的問題：年輕人幾歲才準備好使用社群媒體──又該由誰來決定？"],[["platform","n.","平台"],["fine","v.","罰款"],["cyberbullying","n.","網路霸凌"],["lonely","adj.","孤單的"],["privacy","n.","隱私"],["restrict","v.","限制"]],308]]],[12,[["碳板跑鞋與比賽規則（2017–2020 年運動科技新聞）",["In 2017, Nike released a running shoe called the Vaporfly, and the world of distance running has not been the same since. Within a few years, runners wearing it and similar \"super shoes\" had broken almost every major road-racing record. In 2019, Eliud Kipchoge became the first person to run a marathon distance in under two hours, wearing a test version of a newer model.","What makes these shoes so special? Traditional running shoes have a thin layer of foam to cushion the foot. Super shoes, by contrast, combine a thick layer of light, very springy foam with a stiff plate, usually made of carbon fiber, inside the sole. The foam absorbs the force of each step and returns much of that energy, like a trampoline, while the curved plate acts like a lever that helps push the runner forward. Studies have found that such shoes can reduce the energy needed to run at a given speed by about four percent—a huge advantage in a sport where races are often won by seconds.","Not everyone celebrated. Critics argued that the shoes gave an unfair advantage, comparing them to the high-tech swimsuits that were banned in 2010 after swimmers broke dozens of world records. Others pointed out that the shoes were expensive, so athletes without large sponsors might fall behind. Some even asked: if a record depends on the shoe, whose achievement is it—the runner's or the engineer's?","In 2020, World Athletics, the sport's governing body, responded with new rules. For road races, the sole of a shoe may be no thicker than 40 millimeters, and it may contain no more than one stiff plate. In addition, any shoe used in competition must be available for ordinary people to buy, so that no athlete can use secret technology.","Today, super shoes are seen everywhere, from the Olympics to local fun runs. Yet the debate continues over where the line between fair technology and unfair advantage should be drawn."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How super shoes work and the debate they have caused","How Eliud Kipchoge trained for the marathon","Why high-tech swimsuits were banned","How to choose running shoes for beginners"],[],0,"主旨","第一段介紹超級跑鞋帶來的改變，第二段說明原理，第三段是批評，第四段是新規則，末段說爭議仍在。主旨是 (A)。(C) 泳衣只是第三段用來類比的例子。","(A) 超級跑鞋如何運作及其引發的爭議　(B) 基普喬蓋如何為馬拉松訓練　(C) 高科技泳衣為何被禁　(D) 初學者如何挑選跑鞋"],["According to the passage, what does the stiff plate in a super shoe do?",["It keeps the runner's feet warm.","It makes the shoe lighter than traditional shoes.","It works like a lever to help push the runner forward.","It absorbs all of the force from each step."],[],2,"細節","第二段：<i>the curved plate acts like a lever that helps push the runner forward</i>，選 (C)。(D) 吸收衝擊力並回彈的是泡棉，不是碳板。","(A) 讓跑者的腳保暖　(B) 讓鞋子比傳統鞋輕　(C) 像槓桿一樣幫助跑者向前推進　(D) 吸收每一步的全部衝擊力"],["Under the 2020 rules, which of the following shoes would be allowed in a road race?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><path d=\"M22,52 Q30,26 62,22 L96,20 Q120,34 150,38 Q178,42 180,52 Z\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><path d=\"M18,52 L182,52 Q186,87.1 176,87.1 L28,87.1 Q16,87.1 18,52 Z\" fill=\"#F3E3C3\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><path d=\"M26,74.305 Q100,79.305 176,65.305\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"192\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"192\" y2=\"87.1\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"188\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"188\" y1=\"87.1\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"87.1\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"105.1\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">45 mm · 1 plate</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><path d=\"M22,52 Q30,26 62,22 L96,20 Q120,34 150,38 Q178,42 180,52 Z\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><path d=\"M18,52 L182,52 Q186,81.64 176,81.64 L28,81.64 Q16,81.64 18,52 Z\" fill=\"#F3E3C3\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><path d=\"M26,71.302 Q100,76.302 176,62.30200000000001\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"192\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"192\" y2=\"81.64\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"188\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"188\" y1=\"81.64\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"81.64\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"99.64\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">38 mm · 1 plate</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><path d=\"M22,52 Q30,26 62,22 L96,20 Q120,34 150,38 Q178,42 180,52 Z\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><path d=\"M18,52 L182,52 Q186,79.3 176,79.3 L28,79.3 Q16,79.3 18,52 Z\" fill=\"#F3E3C3\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><path d=\"M26,64.555 Q100,69.555 176,55.555\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M26,74.656 Q100,79.656 176,65.656\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"192\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"192\" y2=\"79.3\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"188\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"188\" y1=\"79.3\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"79.3\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"97.3\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">35 mm · 2 plates</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><path d=\"M22,52 Q30,26 62,22 L96,20 Q120,34 150,38 Q178,42 180,52 Z\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><path d=\"M18,52 L182,52 Q186,91 176,91 L28,91 Q16,91 18,52 Z\" fill=\"#F3E3C3\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"192\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"192\" y2=\"91\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"188\" y1=\"52\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"52\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"188\" y1=\"91\" x2=\"196\" y2=\"91\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"109\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"600\">50 mm · no plate</text></svg>"],1,"圖片題（規則應用）","第四段：路跑鞋底 <i>no thicker than 40 millimeters</i>，且 <i>no more than one stiff plate</i>。(B) 38 mm、一片碳板，兩項都符合。(A)(D) 鞋底超過 40 mm；(C) 有兩片碳板。","(A) 45 mm、一片板　(B) 38 mm、一片板　(C) 35 mm、兩片板　(D) 50 mm、沒有板"],["Why must shoes used in competition be available for ordinary people to buy?",["To help shoe companies earn more money","To make sure that shoes are not too expensive","To encourage more people to run marathons","To prevent athletes from using secret technology"],[],3,"細節（目的）","第四段：比賽用鞋必須是一般人買得到的，<i>so that no athlete can use secret technology</i>，選 (D)。","(A) 幫鞋商賺更多錢　(B) 確保鞋子不會太貴　(C) 鼓勵更多人跑馬拉松　(D) 防止選手使用祕密科技"]],["2017 年，Nike 推出一款名為 Vaporfly 的跑鞋，從此長跑世界再也不同以往。短短幾年內，穿著它和其他類似「超級跑鞋」的跑者，幾乎打破了所有重要的路跑紀錄。2019 年，埃利烏德．基普喬蓋穿著一款新型號的測試版跑鞋，成為第一個在兩小時內跑完馬拉松距離的人。","這些鞋子有什麼特別之處？傳統跑鞋只有一層薄薄的泡棉來緩衝腳步。相較之下，超級跑鞋結合了一層又厚又輕、彈性極佳的泡棉，以及鞋底裡面一片堅硬的板子，通常是碳纖維製成。泡棉吸收每一步的衝擊力，再像彈跳床一樣把大部分能量回饋出去；彎曲的碳板則像槓桿，幫助跑者向前推進。研究發現，這種鞋能讓人以相同速度跑步時所需的能量減少約百分之四──在一項常以秒數分勝負的運動中，這是巨大的優勢。","並不是每個人都在慶祝。批評者認為這種鞋帶來不公平的優勢，並把它比作 2010 年被禁用的高科技泳衣──當時泳者打破了數十項世界紀錄。也有人指出鞋子很貴，沒有大型贊助商的選手可能因此落後。甚至有人問：如果紀錄取決於鞋子，那是誰的成就──跑者的，還是工程師的？","2020 年，田徑運動的管理機構「世界田徑總會」以新規則回應：路跑比賽的鞋底厚度不得超過 40 毫米，且最多只能有一片硬板。此外，比賽用鞋必須是一般大眾買得到的，讓任何選手都無法使用祕密科技。","如今，從奧運到地方的路跑活動，到處都看得到超級跑鞋。然而，公平的科技與不公平的優勢之間的界線該畫在哪裡，爭論仍在持續。"],[["cushion","v.","緩衝"],["by contrast","phr.","相較之下"],["stiff","adj.","堅硬的"],["lever","n.","槓桿"],["sponsor","n.","贊助商"],["governing body","n.","管理機構"]],329],["蔡格尼克效應（心理學經典題材）",["In the 1920s, a young psychologist named Bluma Zeigarnik was sitting in a busy restaurant in Berlin with her professor, Kurt Lewin. They noticed something curious about the waiters. A waiter could remember long, complicated orders perfectly—as long as the bill had not yet been paid. Once the customers had paid and left, the waiter seemed to forget the details almost at once.","Zeigarnik decided to test this observation in a laboratory. She gave participants a series of simple tasks, such as solving puzzles and making clay figures. Some tasks they were allowed to finish; others were interrupted before they were completed. Later, when asked to recall the tasks, participants remembered the unfinished ones about twice as well as the finished ones. This tendency to remember interrupted tasks better than completed ones became known as the Zeigarnik effect.","Why does it happen? Psychologists believe that starting a task creates a kind of mental tension. Until the task is complete, the mind keeps it active, like an open window on a computer screen. Once the task is finished, the tension is released, and the mind is free to let it go.","Television writers understand this well. A series that ends each episode with a cliffhanger—a hero hanging from the edge of a cliff, a mysterious knock at the door—leaves viewers with an unfinished story that is hard to stop thinking about. Many of us cannot resist watching \"just one more episode.\"","The effect can also be useful. Students who find it hard to begin studying may benefit from simply starting, even for a few minutes; once a task is under way, the mind tends to want to return to it. On the other hand, the effect can cause stress. A long list of unfinished tasks can keep the mind busy at night, making it hard to relax. Some experts suggest writing down a plan for unfinished work before going to bed. Doing so seems to tell the mind that the task is under control, allowing it to rest."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How waiters in Berlin were trained","Why unfinished tasks tend to stay on our minds","How television series are written","Why students should study late at night"],[],1,"主旨","第一、二段寫蔡格尼克效應的由來與實驗，第三段說明原因，第四、五段說明它在電視劇與生活中的影響。主旨是「未完成的事為何常留在腦中」，選 (B)。","(A) 柏林的服務生如何受訓　(B) 未完成的事為何常留在腦海中　(C) 電視影集如何編寫　(D) 學生為何該熬夜讀書"],["What gave Zeigarnik the idea for her study?",["A puzzle she could not finish","A television series she watched","A book written by her professor","Waiters who remembered orders until they were paid"],[],3,"細節","第一段：她和教授在餐廳注意到服務生在帳單付清前能完整記住點餐，付完錢就幾乎立刻忘記，<i>Zeigarnik decided to test this observation</i>，選 (D)。","(A) 一個她解不開的謎題　(B) 她看的一部電視劇　(C) 她教授寫的一本書　(D) 帳單付清前都記得點餐內容的服務生"],["What does \"cliffhanger\" in the fourth paragraph most likely mean?",["An exciting ending that leaves the story unfinished","A scene filmed in the mountains","A character who is afraid of heights","An episode that explains everything clearly"],[],0,"字義推論","上下文：每集結尾停在主角懸在懸崖邊、門外傳來神祕敲門聲，<i>leaves viewers with an unfinished story</i>，讓人忍不住看下一集。cliffhanger 指「停在緊要關頭、懸而未決的結尾」，選 (A)。","(A) 讓故事懸而未決的刺激結尾　(B) 在山上拍攝的場景　(C) 怕高的角色　(D) 把一切交代清楚的一集"],["According to the passage, which of the following may help a person relax at night?",["Watching one more episode of a series","Starting a new task before bed","Writing down a plan for unfinished work","Trying to remember every task of the day"],[],2,"細節（應用）","末段：未完成的事會讓腦袋晚上停不下來，專家建議 <i>writing down a plan for unfinished work before going to bed</i>，讓大腦覺得一切在掌控中而能休息，選 (C)。(A)(B) 反而會留下更多未完成的事。","(A) 再看一集影集　(B) 睡前開始新的工作　(C) 寫下未完成工作的計畫　(D) 試著記住當天每一件事"]],["1920 年代，年輕的心理學家布魯瑪．蔡格尼克和她的教授庫爾特．勒溫坐在柏林一家忙碌的餐廳裡。他們注意到服務生有個奇怪的現象：只要帳單還沒付，服務生就能完整記住又長又複雜的點餐內容；可是客人一付完錢離開，服務生似乎幾乎立刻就忘了細節。","蔡格尼克決定在實驗室裡驗證這個觀察。她給參與者一連串簡單的任務，例如解謎題、捏黏土人偶。有些任務讓他們做完，有些則在完成前被打斷。之後請他們回想做過的任務時，參與者記得未完成任務的程度，大約是已完成任務的兩倍。這種對被打斷的任務記得比完成的任務更清楚的傾向，就被稱為「蔡格尼克效應」。","為什麼會這樣？心理學家認為，開始一件事會產生一種心理上的緊繃。在任務完成之前，大腦會讓它保持在活躍狀態，就像電腦螢幕上一個開著的視窗。一旦任務完成，緊繃就解除了，大腦也就可以放下它。","電視編劇深諳此道。一部影集若每集都以「懸念結尾」收場──主角懸在懸崖邊、門外傳來神祕的敲門聲──就會讓觀眾帶著一個未完的故事，很難不去想它。我們很多人都忍不住要「再看一集就好」。","這個效應也可以派上用場。很難開始讀書的學生，不妨就先開始，哪怕只有幾分鐘；一旦事情開了頭，大腦往往會想回到它身上。另一方面，這個效應也可能帶來壓力。一長串未完成的事情會讓大腦在夜裡停不下來，難以放鬆。有些專家建議睡前把未完成工作的計畫寫下來。這麼做似乎能告訴大腦「事情都在掌控之中」，讓它得以休息。"],[["observation","n.","觀察"],["interrupt","v.","打斷"],["recall","v.","回想"],["tension","n.","緊張；緊繃"],["cliffhanger","n.","懸念結尾"],["under way","phr.","進行中"]],340],["吐瓦魯面對海平面上升（2021–2025 年氣候新聞）",["Tuvalu is one of the smallest countries on Earth. Its nine islands in the Pacific Ocean have a total land area of only 26 square kilometers and are home to about 11,000 people. Most of the land lies less than three meters above sea level. For Tuvalu, rising seas caused by climate change are not a distant threat but a daily reality. During high tides, seawater can flood roads and homes and seep into the soil, making it harder to grow crops.","In 2021, Tuvalu's foreign minister, Simon Kofe, gave a video speech to a world climate conference while standing knee-deep in the sea in a suit and tie. The image spread around the world, showing leaders what was at stake. A year later, he announced an even more unusual plan: Tuvalu would become the first \"digital nation.\" The government would build a virtual copy of the islands online and store the nation's history, culture, and official records there, so that even if the land were lost, the country could live on in some form.","The government also took legal steps. In 2023, Tuvalu changed its constitution to state that the country will remain a state, with the same borders, even if its land disappears. That year, it also signed a treaty with Australia called the Falepili Union. Under the agreement, up to 280 Tuvaluans each year can move to Australia to live, work, and study. When applications first opened in 2025, more than 8,000 people—including family members—signed up, well over half of the population.","Many Tuvaluans, however, have no wish to leave. For them, the islands are not just land but the home of their ancestors, their language, and their way of life. The government continues to build seawalls and create new, higher land to protect its people.","Tuvalu's story raises difficult questions for the world. What happens to a nation if its land is gone? And what responsibility do larger countries, whose pollution has driven climate change, have toward the small nations that suffer most?"],[["What is the main purpose of the passage?",["To encourage tourists to visit the Pacific","To compare Tuvalu's culture with Australia's","To explain how seawalls are built","To describe how a small nation is responding to rising seas"],[],3,"寫作目的","第一段說明吐瓦魯面臨海平面上升，第二、三段介紹它的因應：泡在海中演講、數位國家、修憲、與澳洲簽約，第四段寫居民不願離開與築海堤，末段提出問題。目的是描述小國如何因應海平面上升，選 (D)。","(A) 鼓勵遊客造訪太平洋　(B) 比較吐瓦魯與澳洲的文化　(C) 說明海堤如何建造　(D) 描述一個小國如何因應海平面上升"],["What is the purpose of Tuvalu's \"digital nation\" plan?",["To attract foreign companies to invest online","To keep the nation's culture and records alive even if the land is lost","To replace the government with computers","To teach young people how to use the internet"],[],1,"細節（目的）","第二段：在網路上建立島嶼的虛擬複本並保存歷史、文化與官方紀錄，<i>so that even if the land were lost, the country could live on in some form</i>，選 (B)。","(A) 吸引外國公司在網路上投資　(B) 即使失去土地，也能保存國家的文化與紀錄　(C) 用電腦取代政府　(D) 教年輕人使用網路"],["Which of the following is true about the Falepili Union?",["It allows all Tuvaluans to move to Australia at once.","It was signed after Tuvalu's land had disappeared.","It allows up to 280 Tuvaluans a year to move to Australia.","Few people in Tuvalu were interested in it."],[],2,"細節","第三段：<i>up to 280 Tuvaluans each year can move to Australia</i>，選 (C)。(A) 每年有名額上限；(B) 土地尚未消失；(D) 2025 年有八千多人登記，遠超過人口的一半。","(A) 讓所有吐瓦魯人一次移居澳洲　(B) 在吐瓦魯土地消失後才簽署　(C) 每年最多讓 280 名吐瓦魯人移居澳洲　(D) 吐瓦魯人對它興趣缺缺"],["What does the author suggest in the last paragraph?",["Larger countries may have a duty to help small nations harmed by climate change.","Tuvalu should give up its land and move to Australia.","Climate change affects all countries equally.","The questions about Tuvalu have already been answered."],[],0,"推論（作者觀點）","末段反問：那些污染造成氣候變遷的大國，<i>what responsibility ... have toward the small nations that suffer most</i>。作者藉問句暗示大國可能有責任幫助受害最深的小國，選 (A)。(C) 與「受害最深的小國」矛盾。","(A) 大國可能有責任幫助受氣候變遷傷害的小國　(B) 吐瓦魯應放棄土地遷往澳洲　(C) 氣候變遷對各國影響相同　(D) 關於吐瓦魯的問題都已有答案"]],["吐瓦魯是地球上最小的國家之一。它位於太平洋的九座島嶼，陸地總面積只有 26 平方公里，住著約一萬一千人。大部分土地的海拔不到三公尺。對吐瓦魯來說，氣候變遷造成的海平面上升不是遙遠的威脅，而是每天的現實。漲潮時，海水可能淹沒道路和房屋，並滲入土壤，讓作物更難生長。","2021 年，吐瓦魯外交部長賽門．柯菲穿著西裝、打著領帶，站在深及膝蓋的海水中，向一場世界氣候會議發表影片演說。這個畫面傳遍全世界，讓各國領袖看見事態有多嚴重。一年後，他宣布了一項更不尋常的計畫：吐瓦魯將成為第一個「數位國家」。政府將在網路上建立島嶼的虛擬複本，把國家的歷史、文化和官方紀錄存放在那裡，這樣即使土地消失，國家也能以某種形式延續下去。","政府也採取了法律行動。2023 年，吐瓦魯修改憲法，明定即使土地消失，國家仍將維持為一個國家，疆界不變。同年，它也和澳洲簽訂了名為「法列皮里聯盟」的條約。根據協議，每年最多可有 280 名吐瓦魯人移居澳洲生活、工作和求學。2025 年首度開放申請時，有八千多人（包含家屬）登記，遠超過全國人口的一半。","然而，許多吐瓦魯人並不想離開。對他們而言，這些島嶼不只是土地，更是祖先、語言和生活方式的家園。政府持續興建海堤，並造出地勢更高的新土地來保護人民。","吐瓦魯的故事向世界提出了艱難的問題：如果一個國家的土地消失了，它會怎麼樣？而那些排放污染、促成氣候變遷的大國，對受害最深的小國又負有什麼責任？"],[["sea level","n.","海平面"],["seep","v.","滲入"],["at stake","phr.","面臨危險；攸關"],["virtual","adj.","虛擬的"],["constitution","n.","憲法"],["treaty","n.","條約"]],339]]],[13,[["孟買便當快遞員 dabbawala（社會與職業經典題材）",["Every weekday in Mumbai, India, around 200,000 home-cooked lunches travel across one of the world's busiest cities and arrive at offices in time for lunch. They are carried by the dabbawalas, a group of about 5,000 delivery workers whose name comes from \"dabba,\" meaning box, and \"wala,\" meaning a person who does a certain job.","The service began in 1890, when a banker in Mumbai wanted a home-cooked meal at work and hired someone to bring it to him. Others soon asked for the same service. At the time, many office workers had long journeys to work and preferred food made at home to restaurant meals. More than a century later, the tradition continues.","Here is how it works. In the late morning, a dabbawala collects lunchboxes from homes in one neighborhood and takes them by bicycle to the nearest train station. There, the boxes are sorted and loaded onto trains. At stations in the city center, other dabbawalas sort them again and deliver them by bicycle or handcart to offices. After lunch, the process runs in reverse, and the empty boxes are back home by late afternoon. A single box may pass through the hands of three or four dabbawalas along the way.","What makes this remarkable is that the system relies not on computers or phones but on a simple code painted on each box. Many dabbawalas have little formal education, so the code uses colors, symbols, and numbers to show where the box comes from, the station where it must be unloaded, and the building and floor where it must go. The dabbawalas are often said to make only one mistake in several million deliveries, though this famous figure has never been officially confirmed.","Their accuracy has drawn attention from business schools around the world, and in 2003 Britain's Prince Charles visited them in Mumbai. Today, the dabbawalas face new challenges, from food delivery apps to changing work habits. Yet their story shows that teamwork, discipline, and a clever system can sometimes do what technology cannot."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["Why office workers in Mumbai prefer restaurant food","A lunch delivery system in Mumbai and why it works so well","How Indian trains are run on time","How food delivery apps are changing India"],[],1,"主旨","第一、二段介紹 dabbawala 與起源，第三段說明運作流程，第四段說明靠簡單編碼達到極高準確度，末段談外界關注與挑戰。主旨是 (B)。(A) 與原文相反：上班族偏好家常菜。","(A) 孟買上班族為何偏好餐廳食物　(B) 孟買的便當遞送系統及其運作良好的原因　(C) 印度火車如何準時　(D) 外送應用程式如何改變印度"],["How is the information in the third paragraph organized?",["The steps of a process are described in order.","Two cities are compared and contrasted.","A problem is described, followed by its solution.","Different opinions about a topic are listed."],[],0,"篇章組織","第三段以 <i>Here is how it works</i> 開頭，依序描述上午收便當、騎車到車站、分類上火車、市中心再分類、送到辦公室、午後送回，屬於「依序說明流程」，選 (A)。","(A) 依序描述一個流程的步驟　(B) 比較兩座城市的異同　(C) 先描述問題再提出解決方法　(D) 列出對某主題的不同意見"],["Why does the code on the lunchboxes use colors and symbols?",["They make the boxes look more attractive.","Computers can read them more easily.","They are required by Indian law.","Many dabbawalas have little formal education."],[],3,"細節（原因）","第四段：<i>Many dabbawalas have little formal education, so the code uses colors, symbols, and numbers</i>，選 (D)。(B) 這套系統正是不靠電腦。","(A) 讓便當盒更好看　(B) 讓電腦更容易讀取　(C) 印度法律規定　(D) 許多 dabbawala 沒受過太多正規教育"],["What does the author suggest about the claim that the dabbawalas make \"only one mistake in several million deliveries\"?",["It was invented by Prince Charles.","It has been proved by business schools.","It is widely repeated but not officially proven.","It shows that the system is getting worse."],[],2,"推論（作者態度）","第四段：他們 <i>are often said to</i> 數百萬次才錯一次，<i>though this famous figure has never been officially confirmed</i>。作者指出這個數字廣為流傳但未經正式證實，選 (C)。","(A) 由查爾斯王子捏造　(B) 已被商學院證實　(C) 廣為流傳但未經正式證實　(D) 顯示系統愈來愈差"]],["在印度孟買，每個工作日都有大約二十萬份家常午餐橫越這座世界最繁忙的城市之一，準時送到辦公室。負責運送的是 dabbawala（便當快遞員），一群約五千名的送餐工作者，他們的名稱來自「dabba」（盒子）和「wala」（從事某種工作的人）。","這項服務始於 1890 年，當時孟買一位銀行家想在上班時吃到家裡煮的飯，便雇人替他送來。很快就有其他人要求同樣的服務。那時許多上班族通勤路程很長，比起餐館的飯菜，他們更喜歡家裡做的食物。一個多世紀後，這項傳統依然延續。","運作方式如下：上午稍晚，一位 dabbawala 到某個社區的住家收集便當盒，騎腳踏車送到最近的火車站。便當盒在那裡分類後裝上火車。到了市中心的車站，其他 dabbawala 再分類一次，用腳踏車或手推車送到各個辦公室。午餐過後，流程反過來進行，空便當盒在傍晚前回到家中。一個便當盒在途中可能會經過三、四位 dabbawala 之手。","了不起的是，這套系統靠的不是電腦或手機，而是漆在每個便當盒上的簡單編碼。許多 dabbawala 沒有受過太多正規教育，所以編碼用顏色、符號和數字來標示便當盒的出發地、要卸下的車站，以及要送到的大樓和樓層。常有人說 dabbawala 數百萬次遞送才出錯一次，不過這個著名的數字從未經過正式證實。","他們的準確度吸引了世界各地商學院的關注，2003 年英國查爾斯王子也曾到孟買拜訪他們。如今，dabbawala 面臨新的挑戰，從外送應用程式到工作型態的改變都是。然而他們的故事說明了：團隊合作、紀律和巧妙的系統，有時能做到科技做不到的事。"],[["home-cooked","adj.","家常的；在家煮的"],["sort","v.","分類"],["in reverse","phr.","反過來；逆向"],["formal education","n.","正規教育"],["confirm","v.","證實"],["discipline","n.","紀律"]],339],["墨西哥鈍口螈（六角恐龍）的保育危機",["With its wide smile, tiny eyes, and feathery pink gills sticking out from the sides of its head, the axolotl looks like a cartoon character. In recent years, this unusual salamander has become a pop-culture star. It appears in video games, as stuffed toys, and even on Mexico's 50-peso banknote. Yet in the wild, the axolotl is close to extinction.","In the wild, axolotls are found almost only in the canals of Xochimilco, on the southern edge of Mexico City. Unlike most salamanders, which lose their gills and move onto land as adults, axolotls keep their young features throughout their lives. They stay in the water, breathing through their gills, even after they are fully grown.","The animals are also famous for their remarkable ability to regenerate. If an axolotl loses a leg, it can grow a complete new one, including bones, muscles, and nerves, without leaving a scar. It can even repair parts of its heart, spinal cord, and brain. Because of this, axolotls are kept in laboratories around the world, where scientists hope they will reveal secrets that could one day help humans heal.","Ironically, while huge numbers of axolotls live in labs and home fish tanks, their wild relatives are disappearing. Surveys found about 6,000 axolotls per square kilometer in Xochimilco in 1998, but only about 35 in 2014. The causes include water pollution, the growth of Mexico City, and fish such as carp and tilapia, which were brought into the canals and eat young axolotls and their food.","Efforts are now under way to save them. Local farmers who grow crops on traditional island gardens called chinampas have created clean-water shelters in the canals, where axolotls can live safely. A university project invites people to \"adopt\" an axolotl online, with the money going to protect its home. Whether these efforts succeed will decide if the axolotl survives as more than a cute pet and a picture on a banknote."],[["Which of the following is the best title for the passage?",["A Famous Animal in Danger","How to Keep Pets in Fish Tanks","The History of Mexican Banknotes","Why Salamanders Live on Land"],[],0,"標題","首段先說鈍口螈在流行文化中很紅，接著轉折 <i>Yet in the wild, the axolotl is close to extinction</i>；後文說明牠的特性、野外數量銳減的原因與保育行動。最佳標題是「一種瀕危的知名動物」，選 (A)。","(A) 一種瀕臨危機的知名動物　(B) 如何在魚缸裡養寵物　(C) 墨西哥紙鈔的歷史　(D) 蠑螈為何住在陸地上"],["How are axolotls different from most salamanders?",["They live only in laboratories.","They are much larger than other salamanders.","They keep their gills and stay in the water as adults.","They lose their legs when they grow up."],[],2,"細節（比較）","第二段：多數蠑螈成年後失去鰓、移居陸地，鈍口螈卻 <i>keep their young features</i>，長大後仍留在水中用鰓呼吸，選 (C)。","(A) 牠們只住在實驗室裡　(B) 牠們比其他蠑螈大得多　(C) 成年後仍保有鰓、留在水中　(D) 長大後會失去腳"],["Why does the author use the word \"Ironically\" at the beginning of the fourth paragraph?",["Axolotls grow back their legs very quickly.","Axolotls are common in captivity but rare in the wild.","Scientists dislike keeping axolotls in labs.","Carp and tilapia are popular pets in Mexico."],[],1,"寫作手法","Ironically（諷刺的是）後面說：大量鈍口螈住在實驗室和家中魚缸，<i>their wild relatives are disappearing</i>。人工環境中很多、野外卻快消失，這種反差就是諷刺之處，選 (B)。","(A) 鈍口螈腳長回來得很快　(B) 鈍口螈在人工飼養下很常見，野外卻很稀少　(C) 科學家不喜歡在實驗室養鈍口螈　(D) 鯉魚和吳郭魚是墨西哥的熱門寵物"],["Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of the axolotl's decline in the wild?",["Water pollution","The growth of Mexico City","Fish that were brought into the canals","People catching axolotls for food"],[],3,"細節（NOT）","第四段列出的原因：水污染（A）、墨西哥城擴張（B）、被引進運河的鯉魚與吳郭魚（C）。文中沒有提到人們捕來食用，選 (D)。","(A) 水污染　(B) 墨西哥城的擴張　(C) 被引進運河的魚類　(D) 人們捕捉鈍口螈來吃"]],["咧著大大的笑臉、眼睛小小的、頭兩側伸出羽毛般的粉紅色鰓──墨西哥鈍口螈（俗稱六角恐龍）看起來就像卡通人物。近年來，這種奇特的蠑螈成了流行文化明星：牠出現在電玩裡、被做成絨毛玩偶，甚至印在墨西哥 50 披索的紙鈔上。然而在野外，鈍口螈已瀕臨滅絕。","野生的鈍口螈幾乎只出現在墨西哥城南緣霍奇米爾科的運河中。大多數蠑螈成年後會失去鰓、移居陸地，鈍口螈卻終生保有幼年的特徵，即使完全長大，也留在水中用鰓呼吸。","這種動物還以驚人的再生能力聞名。鈍口螈如果失去一條腿，可以長出一條完整的新腿，包括骨頭、肌肉和神經，而且不留疤痕。牠甚至能修復部分心臟、脊髓和大腦。因此，世界各地的實驗室都養著鈍口螈，科學家希望牠們能揭開某些祕密，有朝一日幫助人類療傷。","諷刺的是，大量的鈍口螈住在實驗室和家中魚缸裡，牠們在野外的同類卻正在消失。調查發現，1998 年霍奇米爾科每平方公里約有六千隻鈍口螈，到了 2014 年只剩約 35 隻。原因包括水污染、墨西哥城的擴張，以及被引進運河的鯉魚和吳郭魚──牠們會吃掉幼小的鈍口螈和牠們的食物。","拯救行動正在進行中。在名為「奇南帕」的傳統浮田上耕作的當地農民，在運河中打造了乾淨的水域避難所，讓鈍口螈能安全生活。一所大學的計畫邀請民眾在網路上「認養」鈍口螈，款項用來保護牠的家。這些努力能否成功，將決定鈍口螈是否不只是以可愛寵物和紙鈔圖案的身分存在下去。"],[["gill","n.","鰓"],["extinction","n.","滅絕"],["regenerate","v.","再生"],["spinal cord","n.","脊髓"],["ironically","adv.","諷刺的是"],["shelter","n.","避難所；庇護處"]],323],["韋伯太空望遠鏡（2021 年發射、2022 年首批影像）",["On December 25, 2021, a rocket lifted off from French Guiana, in South America, carrying the most powerful space telescope ever built. The James Webb Space Telescope, a project led by NASA together with the European and Canadian space agencies, had taken more than two decades and about ten billion US dollars to build. Its mission: to look farther back in time than ever before.","Light from distant objects takes time to reach us, so looking far into space means looking into the past. The first galaxies formed more than 13 billion years ago, and their light has been stretched during its long journey, shifting it into infrared, a kind of light that human eyes cannot see. Webb was designed to detect this infrared light.","To do so, it needed an enormous mirror. Webb's main mirror is 6.5 meters wide, nearly three times as wide as the mirror on the famous Hubble Space Telescope. But a mirror that large could not fit inside a rocket. The solution was to build it from 18 six-sided pieces, each covered with a thin layer of gold, which reflects infrared light well. Fitted together like a honeycomb, they work as a single large mirror. For launch, the mirror was folded up, then opened in space.","Because infrared light is a form of heat, the telescope must be kept extremely cold—below minus 220 degrees Celsius—so that its own warmth does not drown out faint signals. For this reason, Webb carries a sunshield about the size of a tennis court, made of five thin layers. It also circles the sun at a point about 1.5 million kilometers from Earth, where the sun, Earth, and moon can always stay behind its shield.","In July 2022, the first images were released, showing thousands of galaxies in a tiny patch of sky with amazing clarity. Since then, Webb has found some of the earliest galaxies ever seen and studied the air of planets around other stars. For astronomers, it is like getting a new pair of eyes."],[["Why was Webb designed to detect infrared light?",["Infrared light is easier to photograph with gold.","Infrared light travels faster than other light.","Light from the earliest galaxies has been stretched into infrared.","Human eyes are especially sensitive to infrared light."],[],2,"細節（原因）","第二段：最早星系的光在漫長旅途中被拉長，<i>shifting it into infrared</i>，因此韋伯被設計來偵測紅外線，選 (C)。(D) 原文說紅外線是人眼看不見的光。","(A) 紅外線比較容易用黃金拍攝　(B) 紅外線比其他光跑得快　(C) 最早星系的光已被拉長成紅外線　(D) 人眼對紅外線特別敏感"],["Which picture best shows Webb's main mirror?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"62\" r=\"46\" fill=\"#C9CED6\" stroke=\"#7D8794\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"62\" r=\"8\" fill=\"#FCFAF7\" stroke=\"#7D8794\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><polygon points=\"72.8,39.5 66.9,49.7 55.1,49.7 49.2,39.5 55.1,29.3 66.9,29.3\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"72.8,62.0 66.9,72.2 55.1,72.2 49.2,62.0 55.1,51.8 66.9,51.8\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"72.8,84.5 66.9,94.7 55.1,94.7 49.2,84.5 55.1,74.3 66.9,74.3\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"92.3,28.2 86.4,38.4 74.6,38.4 68.7,28.2 74.6,18.0 86.4,18.0\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"92.3,50.7 86.4,61.0 74.6,61.0 68.7,50.7 74.6,40.5 86.4,40.5\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"92.3,73.3 86.4,83.5 74.6,83.5 68.7,73.3 74.6,63.0 86.4,63.0\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"92.3,95.8 86.4,106.0 74.6,106.0 68.7,95.8 74.6,85.6 86.4,85.6\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"111.8,17.0 105.9,27.2 94.1,27.2 88.2,17.0 94.1,6.7 105.9,6.7\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"111.8,39.5 105.9,49.7 94.1,49.7 88.2,39.5 94.1,29.3 105.9,29.3\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"111.8,84.5 105.9,94.7 94.1,94.7 88.2,84.5 94.1,74.3 105.9,74.3\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"111.8,107.0 105.9,117.3 94.1,117.3 88.2,107.0 94.1,96.8 105.9,96.8\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"131.3,28.2 125.4,38.4 113.6,38.4 107.7,28.2 113.6,18.0 125.4,18.0\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"131.3,50.7 125.4,61.0 113.6,61.0 107.7,50.7 113.6,40.5 125.4,40.5\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"131.3,73.3 125.4,83.5 113.6,83.5 107.7,73.3 113.6,63.0 125.4,63.0\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"131.3,95.8 125.4,106.0 113.6,106.0 107.7,95.8 113.6,85.6 125.4,85.6\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"150.8,39.5 144.9,49.7 133.1,49.7 127.2,39.5 133.1,29.3 144.9,29.3\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"150.8,62.0 144.9,72.2 133.1,72.2 127.2,62.0 133.1,51.8 144.9,51.8\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"150.8,84.5 144.9,94.7 133.1,94.7 127.2,84.5 133.1,74.3 144.9,74.3\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><polygon points=\"90.3,47.3 82.4,61.0 66.6,61.0 58.7,47.3 66.6,33.6 82.4,33.6\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"90.3,76.7 82.4,90.4 66.6,90.4 58.7,76.7 66.6,63.0 82.4,63.0\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"115.8,32.6 107.9,46.2 92.1,46.2 84.2,32.6 92.1,18.9 107.9,18.9\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"115.8,91.4 107.9,105.1 92.1,105.1 84.2,91.4 92.1,77.8 107.9,77.8\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"141.3,47.3 133.4,61.0 117.6,61.0 109.7,47.3 117.6,33.6 133.4,33.6\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"141.3,76.7 133.4,90.4 117.6,90.4 109.7,76.7 117.6,63.0 133.4,63.0\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"46\" y=\"36\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"46\" y=\"54.5\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"46\" y=\"73\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"64.5\" y=\"36\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"64.5\" y=\"54.5\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"64.5\" y=\"73\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"83\" y=\"36\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"83\" y=\"54.5\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"83\" y=\"73\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"101.5\" y=\"36\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"101.5\" y=\"54.5\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"101.5\" y=\"73\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"36\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"54.5\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"73\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"138.5\" y=\"36\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"138.5\" y=\"54.5\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"138.5\" y=\"73\" width=\"17\" height=\"17\" fill=\"#D3A64F\" stroke=\"#A9802F\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg>"],1,"圖片題","第三段：主鏡由 <i>18 six-sided pieces</i> 組成，鍍金，<i>Fitted together like a honeycomb</i>。(B) 是 18 片六角形拼成的蜂巢狀，選 (B)。(A) 單片圓鏡較像哈伯望遠鏡；(C) 只有 6 片；(D) 是 18 片方形。","(A) 單片圓形鏡　(B) 18 片六角形蜂巢狀　(C) 6 片六角形　(D) 18 片方形"],["What is the main purpose of Webb's sunshield?",["To keep the telescope extremely cold","To protect astronauts working on the telescope","To collect energy from the sun","To make the telescope easier to see from Earth"],[],0,"細節（目的）","第四段：紅外線是一種熱，望遠鏡必須保持極低溫，<i>For this reason, Webb carries a sunshield</i>。遮陽罩的目的是讓望遠鏡維持極冷，選 (A)。","(A) 讓望遠鏡保持極低溫　(B) 保護在望遠鏡上工作的太空人　(C) 收集太陽能　(D) 讓人從地球更容易看到望遠鏡"],["What does the author mean by saying \"it is like getting a new pair of eyes\"?",["Astronomers need new glasses to study Webb's images.","Webb was built to study human eyes.","Webb's images are too bright to look at directly.","Webb allows astronomers to see things they could not see before."],[],3,"推論（比喻）","末段：韋伯拍出清晰的深空影像、找到最早期的星系、研究系外行星的大氣，對天文學家而言，<i>like getting a new pair of eyes</i>，比喻能看見以前看不到的東西，選 (D)。","(A) 天文學家需要新眼鏡才能研究韋伯的影像　(B) 韋伯是為研究人眼而建　(C) 韋伯的影像太亮，無法直視　(D) 韋伯讓天文學家看見以前看不到的東西"]],["2021 年 12 月 25 日，一枚火箭從南美洲的法屬圭亞那升空，載著有史以來最強大的太空望遠鏡。詹姆斯．韋伯太空望遠鏡由美國太空總署主導，並與歐洲及加拿大太空機構合作，耗時二十多年、花費約一百億美元打造。它的任務是：比以往更深入地回顧過去。","遠方天體的光需要時間才能到達我們這裡，所以望向宇宙深處，就等於望向過去。最早的星系在一百三十多億年前形成，它們的光在漫長旅程中被拉長，偏移成紅外線──一種人眼看不見的光。韋伯就是為了偵測這種紅外線而設計的。","為了做到這一點，它需要一面巨大的鏡子。韋伯的主鏡寬 6.5 公尺，將近著名的哈伯太空望遠鏡鏡面的三倍寬。但這麼大的鏡子放不進火箭裡。解決辦法是用 18 片六角形鏡片組成主鏡，每一片都鍍上一層薄薄的黃金，因為黃金能有效反射紅外線。這些鏡片像蜂巢般拼在一起，就能當成一面大鏡子使用。發射時鏡面摺疊起來，到了太空再展開。","由於紅外線是一種熱，望遠鏡必須保持極低溫──低於攝氏零下 220 度──才不會讓自身的熱掩蓋微弱的訊號。因此，韋伯帶著一面大約網球場大小、由五層薄膜組成的遮陽罩。它還在距離地球約 150 萬公里的一個位置繞著太陽運行，在那裡，太陽、地球和月球都能一直位於遮陽罩後方。","2022 年 7 月，首批影像公開，以驚人的清晰度呈現一小片天空中的數千個星系。從那時起，韋伯發現了一些人類所見過最早期的星系，也研究了其他恆星周圍行星的大氣。對天文學家來說，這就像得到一雙新的眼睛。"],[["galaxy","n.","星系"],["infrared","adj./n.","紅外線（的）"],["enormous","adj.","巨大的"],["reflect","v.","反射"],["drown out","phr.","淹沒（聲音、訊號）"],["clarity","n.","清晰"]],340]]],[14,[["塞納河重新開放游泳（2024 巴黎奧運與 2025 年開放）",["For a hundred years, swimming in the Seine, the river that flows through the heart of Paris, was against the law. Since 1923, the water had been considered too dirty, and the river too dangerous because of boat traffic. Sewage from old pipes often poured into the river after heavy rain, carrying bacteria that could make people sick.","That began to change when Paris was chosen to host the 2024 Olympic Games. City leaders promised that the Seine would be clean enough for open-water swimming events and that, afterward, ordinary people would be able to swim in it too. To keep that promise, France spent about 1.4 billion euros on a major clean-up project. Workers built a huge underground tank to store rainwater and sewage during storms, improved water treatment plants, and connected thousands of homes and houseboats to the sewer system instead of letting waste flow into the river.","The project faced serious doubts. In the weeks before the Games, heavy rain pushed bacteria levels above safe limits, and several training sessions were canceled. To prove the water was safe, Paris Mayor Anne Hidalgo jumped into the river in July 2024 and swam in front of cameras. In the end, the Olympic triathlon and marathon swimming events went ahead, though one was delayed, and a few athletes reported feeling ill afterward.","On July 5, 2025, the city kept its second promise. Three public swimming areas opened along the Seine, including one near the Eiffel Tower. Swimming was free, lifeguards were on duty, and flags showed each day whether the water was clean enough. Despite some closures due to bad weather, tens of thousands of people took a dip that summer.","For many Parisians, swimming in the Seine was more than a way to cool off. It was a sign that a polluted city river could be brought back to life. Other cities, from Berlin to London, are now studying whether they can do the same."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How Paris cleaned up the Seine so that people could swim in it","Why the Olympic triathlon was canceled","How to become a lifeguard in France","Why boats are no longer allowed on the Seine"],[],0,"主旨","第一段交代禁泳百年的原因，第二段寫為奧運投入的整治工程，第三段寫賽前的疑慮，第四段寫 2025 年開放民眾游泳，末段說明意義。主旨是 (A)。(B) 比賽其實照常舉行。","(A) 巴黎如何整治塞納河讓人能下水游泳　(B) 奧運鐵人三項為何取消　(C) 如何在法國當救生員　(D) 塞納河為何禁止行船"],["Why was swimming in the Seine banned in 1923?",["The river was too cold for most of the year.","The city wanted to protect the fish in the river.","The water was dirty, and boat traffic made it dangerous.","Too many tourists were swimming near the Eiffel Tower."],[],2,"細節（原因）","第一段：1923 年起，河水 <i>considered too dirty</i>，河道也 <i>too dangerous because of boat traffic</i>，選 (C)。","(A) 河水一年中大部分時間太冷　(B) 市府想保護河中的魚　(C) 河水很髒，船隻往來又危險　(D) 太多遊客在艾菲爾鐵塔附近游泳"],["Which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the clean-up project?",["Building an underground tank for storm water","Improving water treatment plants","Connecting homes and houseboats to the sewer system","Moving factories away from the river"],[],3,"細節（NOT）","第二段列出的工程：興建地下儲水槽（A）、改善汙水處理廠（B）、讓住家與船屋接上下水道（C）。文中沒有提到把工廠遷離河岸，選 (D)。","(A) 興建儲存暴雨的地下水槽　(B) 改善汙水處理廠　(C) 讓住家與船屋接上下水道　(D) 把工廠遷離河邊"],["Why did Mayor Anne Hidalgo swim in the Seine in July 2024?",["She was training for the Olympic triathlon.","She wanted to show the public that the water was safe.","She was protesting against the clean-up project.","She had lost a bet with an athlete."],[],1,"推論（目的）","第三段：賽前大雨讓細菌超標、訓練取消，外界有疑慮；<i>To prove the water was safe</i>，市長在鏡頭前跳進河裡游泳，選 (B)。","(A) 她在為奧運鐵人三項受訓　(B) 她想向大眾證明河水安全　(C) 她在抗議整治計畫　(D) 她和一位選手打賭輸了"]],["一百年來，在流經巴黎市中心的塞納河游泳是違法的。自 1923 年起，河水被認為太髒，河道又因船隻往來而太危險。大雨過後，老舊管線中的汙水常常流進河裡，帶來可能讓人生病的細菌。","巴黎被選為 2024 年奧運主辦城市後，情況開始改變。市府領導人承諾，塞納河會乾淨到足以舉辦公開水域游泳項目，而且賽後一般民眾也能在河裡游泳。為了兌現承諾，法國花了約 14 億歐元進行大規模整治工程：工人興建了一座巨大的地下水槽，在暴雨時儲存雨水和汙水；改善汙水處理廠；並讓數千戶住家和船屋接上下水道系統，不再讓廢水流進河裡。","這項計畫遭受嚴重質疑。奧運前幾週，大雨使細菌濃度超過安全標準，好幾場訓練被迫取消。為了證明河水安全，巴黎市長安娜．伊達戈在 2024 年 7 月跳進河裡，在鏡頭前游泳。最後，奧運鐵人三項和馬拉松游泳都照常舉行，不過其中一項延後，也有少數選手事後表示身體不適。","2025 年 7 月 5 日，巴黎兌現了第二個承諾。塞納河沿岸開放了三處公共游泳區，其中一處就在艾菲爾鐵塔附近。游泳免費，有救生員值勤，每天還會以旗幟標示水質是否乾淨到可以下水。儘管有幾次因天候不佳而關閉，那年夏天仍有數萬人下水消暑。","對許多巴黎人來說，在塞納河游泳不只是消暑的方式，更象徵著一條遭受汙染的城市河流能夠重獲新生。從柏林到倫敦，其他城市正在研究自己能否做到同樣的事。"],[["sewage","n.","汙水"],["bacteria","n.","細菌"],["treatment plant","n.","處理廠"],["doubt","n.","懷疑"],["on duty","phr.","值勤"],["take a dip","phr.","下水游一下"]],326],["新幹線「7 分鐘奇蹟」清潔團隊（職場經典題材）",["When a Shinkansen bullet train pulls into Tokyo Station, people on the platform witness a remarkable scene. A team of cleaners in bright uniforms lines up beside the train, and as it stops, they bow to the arriving passengers. Then, the moment the last passenger steps off, they rush aboard. Seven minutes later, the entire train is spotless and ready for its next journey.","The cleaners work for a company called Tessei. A Shinkansen usually stays at Tokyo Station for only about 12 minutes before heading out again. After passengers get off and before new ones get on, the cleaners have just seven minutes. In that time, each worker takes care of one car of about 100 seats. They collect trash, wipe every table, check under seats for lost items, change headrest covers, and sweep the floor.","They also turn the seats around. Shinkansen seats can rotate so that passengers always face the direction in which the train is moving. Because the train will leave in the opposite direction, every row must be turned. With the press of a pedal, a whole row can be spun at once. When they finish, the team lines up on the platform again and bows before the next group of passengers boards.","The work was not always admired. In the past, cleaning jobs were seen as low in status, and many workers felt little pride in what they did. In the 2000s, new managers changed the company's culture. They encouraged workers to see themselves as part of the travelers' experience—offering hospitality, not just cleaning. Workers were invited to suggest improvements, and good ideas were praised and put into practice. Even the uniforms were redesigned to look more cheerful.","The results were striking. Complaints dropped, and the team's speed and politeness drew attention from around the world. Harvard Business School wrote a case study about them, and visitors began filming the \"seven-minute miracle.\" The story shows that how people feel about their work can make a great difference in how well they do it."],[["Which of the following is the best title for the passage?",["How Bullet Trains Reach High Speeds","Tokyo Station's Busiest Platforms","A Seven-Minute Miracle on the Rails","Why Japanese Trains Are Always Late"],[],2,"標題","文章描述新幹線清潔團隊在七分鐘內完成清潔、轉座椅，介紹公司文化如何改變，以及外界稱之為 <i>seven-minute miracle</i>。最佳標題是 (C)。","(A) 子彈列車如何達到高速　(B) 東京車站最繁忙的月台　(C) 鐵道上的七分鐘奇蹟　(D) 日本火車為何總是誤點"],["A train arrived at Tokyo Station, and on its next trip it will travel in the direction of the arrow. Which picture shows the seats after the cleaners finish?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 108\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"40\" width=\"184\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"124.4\" y2=\"22\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"130,22 122.79642318117858,25.479724272889843 122.79642318117858,18.520275727110157\" fill=\"#9A7C82\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"15\" fill=\"#9A7C82\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">next trip</text><rect x=\"37\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"22\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"65\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"50\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"93\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"78\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"121\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"106\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"149\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"134\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"177\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"162\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 108\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"40\" width=\"184\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"124.4\" y2=\"22\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"130,22 122.79642318117858,25.479724272889843 122.79642318117858,18.520275727110157\" fill=\"#9A7C82\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"15\" fill=\"#9A7C82\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">next trip</text><rect x=\"22\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"50\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"55\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"78\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"83\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"106\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"111\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"134\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"139\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"162\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"167\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 108\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"40\" width=\"184\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"124.4\" y2=\"22\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"130,22 122.79642318117858,25.479724272889843 122.79642318117858,18.520275727110157\" fill=\"#9A7C82\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"15\" fill=\"#9A7C82\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">next trip</text><rect x=\"22\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"27\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"65\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"50\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"78\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"83\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"121\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"106\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"134\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"139\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"177\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"162\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 108\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"40\" width=\"184\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"22\" x2=\"124.4\" y2=\"22\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"130,22 122.79642318117858,25.479724272889843 122.79642318117858,18.520275727110157\" fill=\"#9A7C82\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"15\" fill=\"#9A7C82\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">next trip</text><rect x=\"37\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"22\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"65\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"50\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"93\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"78\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"106\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"111\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"134\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"139\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"162\" y=\"52\" width=\"5\" height=\"34\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><rect x=\"167\" y=\"76\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" rx=\"2\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/></svg>"],1,"圖片題","第三段：座椅會轉向，讓乘客 <i>always face the direction in which the train is moving</i>；列車將反方向開出，所以每一排都要轉。清潔完成後，所有座椅都應面向下一趟的行進方向（箭頭所指），選 (B)。(A) 仍面向進站方向；(C)(D) 方向不一致。","(A) 全部面向左（未轉）　(B) 全部面向行進方向　(C) 兩兩相對　(D) 一半向左、一半向右"],["Which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the cleaners' work?",["Collecting trash","Checking for lost items","Changing headrest covers","Selling snacks to passengers"],[],3,"細節（NOT）","第二段列出的工作：收垃圾（A）、檢查座位下的遺失物（B）、更換頭枕套（C）、擦桌子、掃地。文中沒有提到向乘客販賣點心，選 (D)。","(A) 收垃圾　(B) 檢查遺失物　(C) 更換頭枕套　(D) 向乘客販賣點心"],["What does the last sentence of the passage suggest?",["Workers who feel valued may do their jobs better.","Cleaning jobs will soon be done by machines.","Only well-paid workers can do their jobs well.","Train companies should hire fewer cleaners."],[],0,"推論","第四段寫新主管讓員工把工作視為服務的一部分、採納建議並給予讚美；末句 <i>how people feel about their work can make a great difference in how well they do it</i>。可推知感到受重視的員工可能表現更好，選 (A)。","(A) 感到受重視的員工可能把工作做得更好　(B) 清潔工作很快就會由機器取代　(C) 只有高薪員工才能把工作做好　(D) 鐵路公司應少雇用清潔人員"]],["新幹線子彈列車駛進東京車站時，月台上的人會看到驚人的一幕：一群穿著鮮豔制服的清潔人員在列車旁排成一列，列車一停下，他們就向下車的乘客鞠躬。最後一位乘客一踏出車廂，他們便衝上車。七分鐘後，整列車一塵不染，準備好下一趟旅程。","這些清潔人員任職於一家名叫 Tessei 的公司。新幹線在東京車站通常只停留約 12 分鐘就再度出發。從乘客下車到新乘客上車之間，清潔人員只有七分鐘。在這段時間裡，每位員工負責一節約 100 個座位的車廂：收垃圾、擦拭每一張餐桌、檢查座位底下有沒有遺失物、更換頭枕套，還要掃地。","他們也要把座椅轉向。新幹線的座椅可以旋轉，讓乘客永遠面向列車行進的方向。由於列車將朝反方向開出，每一排座椅都必須轉過來。只要踩一下踏板，一整排座椅就能一次轉向。完成後，團隊再度在月台上排好，在下一批乘客上車前鞠躬。","這份工作並非一直受到敬重。過去，清潔工作被視為地位低下，許多員工對自己的工作沒什麼自豪感。2000 年代，新的管理階層改變了公司文化，鼓勵員工把自己視為旅客體驗的一部分──提供的是待客服務，而不只是清潔。公司邀請員工提出改善建議，好點子會受到表揚並付諸實行，連制服都重新設計，看起來更活潑。","成效十分顯著。客訴減少了，團隊的速度與禮貌吸引了全世界的目光。哈佛商學院以他們為題撰寫了個案研究，外國遊客也開始拍攝這段「七分鐘奇蹟」。這個故事說明了：人們對自己工作的感受，會大大影響他們把工作做得多好。"],[["witness","v.","目睹"],["spotless","adj.","一塵不染的"],["rotate","v.","旋轉"],["status","n.","地位"],["hospitality","n.","待客之道；款待"],["striking","adj.","顯著的；引人注目的"]],340],["富蘭克林效應（人際心理經典題材）",["If you want someone to like you, what should you do? Most people would say: do something nice for them. But Benjamin Franklin, one of America's founding fathers, discovered that the opposite can also work. Sometimes the best way to win someone over is to ask them to do you a favor.","In his autobiography, Franklin described a problem he faced as a young politician in Pennsylvania. A fellow lawmaker had given a long speech against him, and Franklin worried that this rival could harm his career. Instead of trying to please the man with gifts or praise, Franklin took a different approach. He had heard that the man owned a rare and valuable book, so he wrote a polite note asking to borrow it. The man sent it right away. About a week later, Franklin returned the book with another note, warmly thanking him. The next time they met, the man spoke to Franklin for the first time—and with great politeness. They went on to become lifelong friends.","Franklin summed up the lesson with an old saying: \"He that has once done you a kindness will be more ready to do you another, than he whom you yourself have obliged.\" In other words, people who have helped you are more likely to help you again than people you have helped.","Psychologists call this the \"Ben Franklin effect,\" and studies have supported it. In one experiment from 1969, people who were asked to return prize money as a personal favor to the researcher later rated him more highly than those who were not asked. One explanation is that people want their actions and feelings to match. If we do a favor for someone, our minds may conclude: \"I helped this person, so I must like them.\"","Of course, the trick has limits. A small, polite request is likely to work; a large or demanding one may only cause annoyance. But the next time you want to build a relationship—with a new classmate or even a rival—consider asking for a little help. It may be the start of a friendship."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How Franklin became a successful politician","Why people enjoy reading rare books","Why we should never ask others for help","Why asking someone for a favor can make them like you"],[],3,"主旨","首段提出觀點：請別人幫忙反而能贏得好感；第二段以富蘭克林借書為例，第三段引用他的格言，第四段以實驗與解釋支持，末段提醒限制與應用。主旨是 (D)。","(A) 富蘭克林如何成為成功的政治人物　(B) 人們為何喜歡讀珍本書　(C) 我們為何永遠不該請別人幫忙　(D) 為何請別人幫個忙能讓對方喜歡你"],["What did Franklin do to win over his rival?",["He asked to borrow a rare book from him.","He gave him an expensive gift.","He praised him in a long speech.","He invited him to dinner at his home."],[],0,"細節","第二段：富蘭克林沒有送禮或讚美，而是聽說對方有本珍貴的書，<i>wrote a polite note asking to borrow it</i>，選 (A)。(B)(C) 正是他沒採用的方法。","(A) 向他借一本珍貴的書　(B) 送他昂貴的禮物　(C) 在長篇演說中稱讚他　(D) 邀他到家裡吃晚餐"],["In Franklin's saying, who does \"he whom you yourself have obliged\" refer to?",["A person who has done you a kindness","A person you have done a favor for","A person who refuses to help you","A person who owns many books"],[],1,"字義推論（指涉）","oblige 在此指「施恩於某人、幫某人忙」。下一句 <i>In other words</i> 換句話說：幫過你的人，比你幫過的人（<i>people you have helped</i>）更可能再幫你。可知 he whom you yourself have obliged 指「你幫過忙的人」，選 (B)。(A) 是格言前半的「he that has once done you a kindness」。","(A) 曾經幫過你的人　(B) 你曾經幫過忙的人　(C) 拒絕幫你的人　(D) 擁有很多書的人"],["According to the last paragraph, which kind of request is most likely to work?",["A large favor that takes a lot of time","A request made in an angry way","A small and polite request","A demand made in front of others"],[],2,"細節","末段：<i>A small, polite request is likely to work; a large or demanding one may only cause annoyance</i>，選 (C)。","(A) 很花時間的大忙　(B) 以生氣的方式提出的請求　(C) 小而有禮貌的請求　(D) 當眾提出的強硬要求"]],["如果你想讓某人喜歡你，該怎麼做？大部分人會說：對他好一點。但美國開國元勳之一的班傑明．富蘭克林發現，反過來做也行得通。有時候，要贏得某人的好感，最好的辦法是請他幫你一個忙。","富蘭克林在自傳中描述了他年輕時在賓夕法尼亞從政所遇到的難題。一位同僚議員曾發表長篇演說反對他，富蘭克林擔心這位對手會危及自己的前途。他沒有用禮物或讚美去討好對方，而是另闢蹊徑。他聽說這個人擁有一本珍貴罕見的書，就寫了一張客氣的便條，請求借閱。對方立刻把書送來。大約一週後，富蘭克林還書時附上另一張便條，熱情地向他道謝。兩人下一次見面時，這個人第一次主動和富蘭克林說話──而且非常有禮貌。後來他們成了一輩子的朋友。","富蘭克林用一句古老的諺語總結這個教訓：「曾經幫過你一次的人，會比你幫過的人更願意再幫你。」換句話說，幫過你的人，比你幫過的人更可能再次幫你。","心理學家把這稱為「富蘭克林效應」，也有研究支持這個說法。在 1969 年的一項實驗中，被要求「當作幫研究者個人一個忙」而退還獎金的人，事後對研究者的評價，比沒被這樣要求的人更高。一種解釋是，人們希望自己的行為和感受一致。如果我們幫了某人的忙，大腦可能會下結論：「我幫了這個人，所以我一定是喜歡他的。」","當然，這招有它的限度。小而有禮貌的請求可能奏效；太大或強人所難的請求，可能只會讓人厭煩。但下次你想建立一段關係時──不論是和新同學，甚至是對手──不妨請對方幫個小忙。這也許就是一段友誼的開始。"],[["win sb over","phr.","贏得某人的支持或好感"],["favor","n.","幫忙；恩惠"],["autobiography","n.","自傳"],["rival","n.","對手"],["oblige","v.","幫…的忙；施恩於"],["annoyance","n.","惱怒；厭煩"]],352]]],[15,[["阿拉斯加「胖熊週」（線上票選活動與生態）",["Every autumn, people around the world vote in an unusual online contest: choosing the fattest bear. The event, called Fat Bear Week, is held by Katmai National Park in Alaska. It began in 2014 as a one-day event and has grown into a week-long competition that draws more than a million votes.","The contestants are brown bears that gather along the Brooks River each summer. The river has one of the world's largest runs of sockeye salmon, which swim upstream to lay their eggs. As the fish leap up a small waterfall, the bears wait at the top, sometimes catching them right out of the air. A large bear can eat dozens of salmon in a single day.","The bears are not simply being greedy. In winter, they will sleep in their dens for up to six months without eating or drinking. During that time, they survive entirely on stored fat. A bear that has not gained enough weight may not live until spring. A pregnant female needs even more fat, since she will give birth during the winter and feed her cubs milk. For these animals, getting fat is a matter of life and death.","Fans follow the bears through live webcams placed along the river. Park staff post \"before\" and \"after\" photos showing each bear in early summer and again in the fall, and voters choose the one that best shows a successful season. Over the years, some bears have become stars. An older male called Otis, known for patiently waiting for fish to come to him, has won the title four times.","Park officials say the contest is about more than fun. By watching the bears, people learn how healthy ecosystems work: the bears depend on the salmon, and the salmon depend on clean rivers and healthy oceans. If the salmon runs fail, the bears suffer too. In this way, a silly contest helps millions of people understand the connections in nature—and why protecting them matters."],[["What is the main idea of the passage?",["Brown bears are dangerous to people in Alaska.","Salmon are the fastest fish in Alaska's rivers.","A contest about fat bears teaches people about nature.","Park staff choose the winner of Fat Bear Week."],[],2,"主旨","文章介紹胖熊週的由來、熊為何要增胖、粉絲如何投票，末段點出活動的意義：<i>a silly contest helps millions of people understand the connections in nature</i>。選 (C)。(D) 由網友投票，不是工作人員選出。","(A) 棕熊對阿拉斯加居民很危險　(B) 鮭魚是阿拉斯加河中最快的魚　(C) 一場胖熊比賽讓人們認識大自然　(D) 公園人員選出胖熊週冠軍"],["Why do the bears need to gain so much weight?",["They must live on stored fat while they sleep through the winter.","Heavier bears can catch fish more easily.","Fat helps them swim in the cold river.","They want to win the online contest."],[],0,"細節（原因）","第三段：冬天熊在巢穴裡睡長達六個月、不吃不喝，<i>they survive entirely on stored fat</i>，體重不夠可能活不到春天，選 (A)。(D) 熊不知道比賽，是人類舉辦的。","(A) 冬眠期間必須靠儲存的脂肪過活　(B) 較重的熊比較容易抓魚　(C) 脂肪幫助牠們在冷河中游泳　(D) 牠們想贏得網路比賽"],["How do voters decide which bear to vote for?",["By measuring each bear's weight on a scale","By counting how many fish each bear catches","By listening to the park rangers' advice","By comparing photos of each bear from early summer and fall"],[],3,"細節","第四段：公園人員張貼每隻熊初夏與秋天的「前後對照照片」，投票者 <i>choose the one that best shows a successful season</i>，選 (D)。","(A) 用磅秤量每隻熊的體重　(B) 計算每隻熊抓了幾條魚　(C) 聽從巡山員的建議　(D) 比較每隻熊初夏與秋天的照片"],["Based on the last paragraph, what would most likely happen if the salmon runs failed?",["The bears would move to the ocean.","The bears would have trouble gaining enough weight.","The contest would become more popular.","The river would become cleaner."],[],1,"推論","末段：熊依賴鮭魚，<i>If the salmon runs fail, the bears suffer too</i>。結合第三段，熊需要大量進食才能過冬，鮭魚減少就很難增胖，選 (B)。","(A) 熊會移居到海洋　(B) 熊難以增加足夠的體重　(C) 比賽會更受歡迎　(D) 河水會變得更乾淨"]],["每年秋天，世界各地的人都會在一場特別的網路比賽中投票：選出最胖的熊。這項名為「胖熊週」的活動由阿拉斯加的卡特麥國家公園舉辦。它在 2014 年以單日活動起家，如今已發展成為期一週、吸引上百萬票的比賽。","參賽者是每年夏天聚集在布魯克斯河畔的棕熊。這條河有全世界規模最大的紅鮭洄游之一，紅鮭會逆流而上產卵。當魚躍上一道小瀑布時，熊就守在瀑布頂端，有時甚至直接從半空中把魚咬住。一隻大熊一天能吃下幾十條鮭魚。","熊並不只是貪吃。冬天時，牠們會在巢穴裡睡上長達六個月，不吃也不喝，完全靠儲存的脂肪存活。體重增加得不夠的熊，可能活不到春天。懷孕的母熊需要更多脂肪，因為她會在冬天產下幼熊並哺乳。對這些動物來說，變胖是攸關生死的事。","粉絲透過架設在河邊的即時網路攝影機追蹤這些熊。公園人員會貼出每隻熊初夏與秋天的「前後對照」照片，投票者選出最能展現這一季成果的熊。這些年來，有些熊成了明星。一頭名叫奧提斯、以耐心等魚上門聞名的老公熊，已經四度奪冠。","公園官員說，這個比賽不只是好玩而已。看著這些熊，人們能了解健康的生態系如何運作：熊依賴鮭魚，鮭魚依賴乾淨的河流和健康的海洋。如果鮭魚洄游的數量銳減，熊也會受害。如此一來，一場看似無厘頭的比賽，讓數百萬人了解大自然中環環相扣的關係──以及保護這些關係的重要。"],[["contestant","n.","參賽者"],["upstream","adv.","逆流而上"],["greedy","adj.","貪吃的；貪心的"],["den","n.","（野獸的）巢穴"],["cub","n.","幼獸"],["ecosystem","n.","生態系"]],330],["縮水式通膨 shrinkflation（2022–2024 年消費新聞）",["Have you ever opened a bag of potato chips and felt that it held more air than chips? You may have been right. Around the world, many companies have been quietly shrinking their products while keeping the price the same. This practice is known as \"shrinkflation,\" a combination of \"shrink\" and \"inflation.\"","Shrinkflation is a way for companies to deal with rising costs. When the price of ingredients, packaging, or transportation goes up, a company can either raise its prices or give customers less for the same money. Many choose the second option, because shoppers tend to notice a higher price far more quickly than a smaller package. A chocolate bar might lose a few grams, a roll of toilet paper a few sheets, or a box of cereal a little height. Often, the package itself looks almost exactly the same.","One famous case involved Toblerone, the Swiss chocolate bar known for its row of triangular peaks. In 2016, the company reduced the weight of two of its bars sold in the UK. Instead of making the bars shorter, it widened the gaps between the peaks, so each bar stayed the same length but contained less chocolate. Fans were furious, and the change made headlines. Two years later, the company went back to the original shape.","Shrinkflation became especially common during the period of high prices that followed the COVID-19 pandemic. In response, some governments and stores have taken action. In 2023, a French supermarket chain began putting warning labels on products that had shrunk without becoming cheaper. In 2024, France went further, requiring large stores to display a notice when a product's quantity had gone down but its price had not.","What can shoppers do? Experts recommend checking the \"unit price,\" which shows the cost per gram, per liter, or per sheet. This makes it easier to compare products and to notice when you are paying more for less. After all, the size of the package is not always the size of the deal."],[["According to the passage, what is \"shrinkflation\"?",["Raising prices because products have become bigger","Giving customers less of a product for the same price","Making packages smaller to protect the environment","Selling old products at lower prices"],[],1,"細節（定義）","第一段：公司 <i>quietly shrinking their products while keeping the price the same</i>，價格不變、份量變少，選 (B)。","(A) 因產品變大而漲價　(B) 以相同價格給顧客較少的產品　(C) 為了環保縮小包裝　(D) 低價出售舊產品"],["Why do many companies choose to shrink products instead of raising prices?",["Smaller products are cheaper to advertise.","Governments require them to do so.","Customers prefer smaller packages.","Shoppers notice price increases more quickly than smaller sizes."],[],3,"細節（原因）","第二段：<i>shoppers tend to notice a higher price far more quickly than a smaller package</i>，所以許多公司選擇縮小份量，選 (D)。(B) 政府的規定反而是要求揭露縮水。","(A) 較小的產品廣告較便宜　(B) 政府要求他們這麼做　(C) 顧客偏好較小的包裝　(D) 消費者對漲價的察覺比對份量變小快得多"],["Which picture best shows the change made to the chocolate bar in 2016?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"16\" y=\"46\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">before</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"46\" width=\"158\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"34,46 43,26 52,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"54,46 63,26 72,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"74,46 83,26 92,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"94,46 103,26 112,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"114,46 123,26 132,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"134,46 143,26 152,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"154,46 163,26 172,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"174,46 183,26 192,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"16\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">after</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"108\" width=\"158\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"34,108 43,88 52,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"62,108 71,88 80,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"90,108 99,88 108,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"118,108 127,88 136,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"146,108 155,88 164,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"174,108 183,88 192,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"16\" y=\"46\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">before</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"46\" width=\"158\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"34,46 43,26 52,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"54,46 63,26 72,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"74,46 83,26 92,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"94,46 103,26 112,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"114,46 123,26 132,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"134,46 143,26 152,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"154,46 163,26 172,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"174,46 183,26 192,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"16\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">after</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"108\" width=\"118\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"34,108 43,88 52,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"54,108 63,88 72,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"74,108 83,88 92,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"94,108 103,88 112,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"114,108 123,88 132,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"134,108 143,88 152,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"16\" y=\"46\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">before</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"46\" width=\"158\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"34,46 43,26 52,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"54,46 63,26 72,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"74,46 83,26 92,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"94,46 103,26 112,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"114,46 123,26 132,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"134,46 143,26 152,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"154,46 163,26 172,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"174,46 183,26 192,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"16\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">after</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"108\" width=\"158\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"34,108 43,97 52,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"54,108 63,97 72,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"74,108 83,97 92,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"94,108 103,97 112,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"114,108 123,97 132,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"134,108 143,97 152,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"154,108 163,97 172,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"174,108 183,97 192,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><text x=\"16\" y=\"46\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">before</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"46\" width=\"158\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"34,46 43,26 52,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"54,46 63,26 72,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"74,46 83,26 92,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"94,46 103,26 112,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"114,46 123,26 132,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"134,46 143,26 152,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"154,46 163,26 172,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"174,46 183,26 192,46\" fill=\"#D9C2A8\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"16\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">after</text><rect x=\"34\" y=\"108\" width=\"149\" height=\"6\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"34,108 41,90 48,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"49,108 56,90 63,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"64,108 71,90 78,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"79,108 86,90 93,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"94,108 101,90 108,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"109,108 116,90 123,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"124,108 131,90 138,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"139,108 146,90 153,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"154,108 161,90 168,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><polygon points=\"169,108 176,90 183,108\" fill=\"#B88A63\" stroke=\"#6E5243\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg>"],0,"圖片題","第三段：公司沒有把巧克力變短，而是 <i>widened the gaps between the peaks</i>，長度不變、巧克力變少。(A) 長度相同、峰與峰之間的空隙變大，選 (A)。(B) 變短；(C) 峰變矮；(D) 峰變多變窄。","(A) 長度不變、間隔變寬　(B) 間隔不變、整條變短　(C) 峰變矮　(D) 峰變多、變窄"],["Which shopper is following the experts' advice in the last paragraph?",["Tom always buys the product in the biggest package.","Mia only buys products that are on sale.","Ken compares the price per gram of two bags of chips.","Amy chooses the product with the most colorful package."],[],2,"應用推論","末段：專家建議看「單位價格」，也就是 <i>the cost per gram, per liter, or per sheet</i>。(C) 比較兩包洋芋片每公克的價格，正符合建議。(A) 末句提醒包裝大小不等於划算。","(A) Tom 總是買包裝最大的　(B) Mia 只買特價品　(C) Ken 比較兩包洋芋片每公克的價格　(D) Amy 選包裝最鮮豔的"]],["你有沒有打開一包洋芋片，覺得裡面的空氣比洋芋片還多？你可能是對的。世界各地有許多公司在價格不變的情況下，悄悄縮小產品的份量。這種做法被稱為「縮水式通膨」（shrinkflation），由「縮水」（shrink）和「通貨膨脹」（inflation）兩字組合而成。","縮水式通膨是公司因應成本上漲的一種方式。當原料、包裝或運輸的價格上漲時，公司可以漲價，或是以同樣的價錢給顧客較少的東西。許多公司選擇後者，因為消費者察覺漲價的速度遠比察覺包裝變小快得多。一條巧克力可能少了幾公克，一捲衛生紙少了幾張，一盒穀片矮了一點。包裝本身往往看起來幾乎一模一樣。","一個著名的例子是瑞士的三角巧克力（Toblerone），它以一整排三角形山峰造型聞名。2016 年，該公司減少了兩款在英國販售的巧克力重量。它沒有把巧克力做短，而是加寬山峰之間的間隔，讓每條巧克力長度不變，巧克力卻變少了。粉絲大為憤怒，這項改變還登上新聞頭條。兩年後，公司恢復了原本的造型。","在新冠肺炎疫情之後的高物價時期，縮水式通膨變得特別普遍。對此，有些政府和商店採取了行動。2023 年，法國一家連鎖超市開始在份量縮水卻沒降價的商品上貼警示標籤。2024 年，法國更進一步，要求大型商店在商品份量減少但價格未降時張貼告示。","消費者能怎麼做？專家建議查看「單位價格」，也就是每公克、每公升或每張的價格。這樣比較容易比較不同產品，也比較容易察覺自己花更多錢買到更少的東西。畢竟，包裝的大小不一定代表划不划算。"],[["shrink","v.","縮小"],["inflation","n.","通貨膨脹"],["ingredient","n.","原料；材料"],["furious","adj.","憤怒的"],["quantity","n.","數量；份量"],["unit price","n.","單位價格"]],335],["麟洋配奧運羽球雙連霸（2021 東京、2024 巴黎）",["On August 4, 2024, people across Taiwan gathered in front of their screens as Lee Yang and Wang Chi-lin stepped onto the badminton court in Paris. Three years earlier, at the Tokyo Olympics, the pair had won Taiwan's first-ever Olympic gold medal in badminton. Now they were attempting something no men's doubles team had ever done: winning the Olympic title twice in a row.","Doubles badminton is often described as one of the fastest racket sports in the world. A smash can send the shuttlecock flying at well over 300 kilometers per hour, and points are decided in a split second. Partners must constantly switch between attacking from the back of the court and controlling the net at the front, often with no time to speak. Success depends not only on skill but on trust—each player must know where the other will move.","Lee and Wang, nicknamed \"Lin-Yang\" by fans, built that trust over years of training and competition. In Tokyo, they beat a strong pair from China in straight games in the final. Defending a title, however, is famously difficult, because every opponent studies the champions closely. In Paris, the pair had to fight their way through a series of tough matches.","In the final, they again faced a strong pair from China. After winning the first game, they lost the second, and the match went to a deciding third game. With the score close near the end, Lee and Wang stayed calm and won the final points. When the final point was won, the two partners shouted with joy and embraced. They had made history.","Soon after the victory, Lee retired from international competition, ending his career at its highest point. For many in Taiwan, the pair's story was about more than medals. It showed the value of trust, teamwork, and the courage to keep going when success is far from certain."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How to play doubles badminton","Why badminton is popular in China","How the Paris Olympics were organized","A Taiwanese pair's historic win and what it meant"],[],3,"主旨","首段點出麟洋配挑戰史上首次男雙連霸，第二段說明雙打需要默契，第三、四段寫衛冕之路與決賽，末段寫李洋退役與這段故事的意義。選 (D)。(A) 只是第二段的背景說明。","(A) 如何打羽球雙打　(B) 羽球為何在中國受歡迎　(C) 巴黎奧運如何籌辦　(D) 一對台灣組合的歷史性勝利及其意義"],["What made the 2024 victory historic?",["It was Taiwan's first Olympic medal of any kind.","It was the first time Taiwan sent badminton players to the Olympics.","No men's doubles team had won the Olympic title twice in a row before.","It was the fastest badminton match ever played."],[],2,"細節","第一段：他們嘗試 <i>something no men's doubles team had ever done: winning the Olympic title twice in a row</i>，選 (C)。(A) 東京那面才是台灣羽球首金，而且台灣過去在其他項目早有獎牌。","(A) 那是台灣第一面奧運獎牌　(B) 那是台灣第一次派羽球選手參加奧運　(C) 從來沒有男雙組合連續兩屆奪得奧運金牌　(D) 那是史上最快的羽球比賽"],["According to the second paragraph, what does success in doubles depend on?",["Having the tallest players on the court","Trust between the two partners","Talking loudly during each point","Using the most expensive rackets"],[],1,"細節","第二段：雙打 <i>Success depends not only on skill but on trust</i>，每位球員都必須知道搭檔會往哪裡移動，選 (B)。(C) 文中說常常 <i>with no time to speak</i>。","(A) 場上球員身高最高　(B) 兩位搭檔之間的信任　(C) 每一分都大聲交談　(D) 使用最昂貴的球拍"],["Which of the following is true about the final in Paris?",["Lee and Wang won after losing the second game.","Lee and Wang won in straight games.","Lee and Wang played against a team from Japan.","Lee and Wang lost the first game but won the next two."],[],0,"細節","第四段：他們 <i>After winning the first game, they lost the second</i>，第三局決勝才贏下，選 (A)。(B) 直落二是東京決賽；(C) 對手來自中國；(D) 順序相反。","(A) 麟洋配在輸掉第二局後獲勝　(B) 麟洋配直落二獲勝　(C) 對手是日本組合　(D) 輸掉第一局後連贏兩局"]],["2024 年 8 月 4 日，當李洋和王齊麟踏上巴黎的羽球場時，全台各地的人都守在螢幕前。三年前在東京奧運，這對搭檔替台灣拿下史上第一面奧運羽球金牌。現在，他們要挑戰一件從來沒有男子雙打組合做到的事：連續兩屆奪得奧運金牌。","羽球雙打常被形容為世界上速度最快的拍類運動之一。一記殺球能讓羽球以時速遠超過 300 公里的速度飛出，勝負往往在一瞬間決定。搭檔必須不斷在後場進攻與前場控網之間換位，常常連開口說話的時間都沒有。成功不只靠技術，更靠信任──每位球員都必須知道搭檔會往哪裡移動。","被球迷暱稱為「麟洋配」的李洋和王齊麟，經過多年的訓練與比賽建立了這份信任。在東京奧運決賽，他們直落二擊敗一對實力堅強的中國組合。然而，衛冕出了名地困難，因為每個對手都會仔細研究冠軍。在巴黎，兩人必須在一連串艱苦的比賽中殺出重圍。","決賽中，他們再度遇上一對強勁的中國組合。他們先拿下第一局，卻輸掉第二局，比賽進入決勝的第三局。比賽尾聲比分咬得很緊，李洋和王齊麟保持冷靜，拿下最後幾分。拿下最後一分時，兩人興奮吶喊、相擁慶祝。他們創造了歷史。","勝利後不久，李洋便從國際賽場退役，在生涯最高峰畫下句點。對許多台灣人來說，這對搭檔的故事不只是獎牌而已，它展現了信任、團隊合作，以及在成功毫無把握時仍堅持下去的勇氣。"],[["attempt","v.","嘗試；企圖"],["in a row","phr.","連續"],["split second","n.","一瞬間"],["nickname","v./n.","給…取綽號；綽號"],["defend a title","phr.","衛冕"],["retire","v.","退休；退役"]],315]]],[16,[["「每天一萬步」的由來與研究（健康經典題材＋2019、2022 年研究）",["For many people, 10,000 steps a day has become the golden rule of fitness. Smartwatches buzz with congratulations when we reach it, and countless health campaigns repeat it. But where did this number come from? Surprisingly, it did not begin with science. It began with marketing.","In 1965, shortly after the Tokyo Olympics had started a fitness boom in Japan, a company began selling a pedometer called the \"manpo-kei,\" which means \"10,000-step meter.\" The number was catchy and easy to remember, and the Japanese character for 10,000 even looks a little like a person walking. The product was a success, and the goal of 10,000 steps spread around the world.","For decades, few people questioned the number. Then researchers began to test it. In 2019, a study led by Dr. I-Min Lee of Harvard followed about 16,000 older women for several years. Those who walked about 4,400 steps a day had a much lower risk of dying during the study than those who walked about 2,700. The benefits increased with more steps, but only up to about 7,500 steps a day; beyond that, there was little additional gain.","Later research painted a similar picture. A 2022 analysis that combined data from several studies found that for adults over 60, the benefits leveled off at around 6,000 to 8,000 steps a day. For younger adults, the benefits continued up to about 8,000 to 10,000 steps.","Does this mean that walking 10,000 steps is a waste of time? Not at all. Walking more is still good for the heart, the mind, and one's mood. The real message is encouraging: you do not need to hit a magic number to benefit. For people who are not very active, adding even a few thousand steps a day can make a real difference. In the end, the best number of steps may simply be \"more than you take now.\""],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How to choose the best smartwatch","Why the Tokyo Olympics were important to Japan","How older women can avoid heart disease","Where the 10,000-step goal came from and what research says about it"],[],3,"主旨","第一、二段說明「一萬步」源自 1965 年計步器的行銷，第三、四段介紹研究結果，末段給出結論。主旨是一萬步目標的由來與研究發現，選 (D)。","(A) 如何挑選最好的智慧手錶　(B) 東京奧運對日本為何重要　(C) 年長女性如何避免心臟病　(D) 一萬步目標的由來及研究對它的看法"],["According to the passage, how did the 10,000-step goal begin?",["It was a catchy number used to sell a pedometer.","It was recommended by doctors at the Tokyo Olympics.","It came from a Harvard study of older women.","It was the average number of steps taken by athletes."],[],0,"細節","第一、二段：這個數字 <i>did not begin with science. It began with marketing</i>，1965 年一款名為「萬步計」的計步器因數字好記而熱賣，選 (A)。(C) 哈佛研究是後來對這個數字的檢驗。","(A) 它是用來推銷計步器的好記數字　(B) 由東京奧運的醫生建議　(C) 來自哈佛對年長女性的研究　(D) 是運動員平均每天走的步數"],["Which graph best shows the results of the 2019 study?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 132\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"44\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"44\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polyline points=\"44,90.4 62.25,80.8 80.5,71.2 98.75,61.6 117,52 135.25,42.4 153.5,32.8 171.75,23.200000000000003 190,14.400000000000006\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"20\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">more</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">less</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">steps per day</text><text x=\"44\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2k</text><text x=\"80.5\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4k</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6k</text><text x=\"153.5\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8k</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">10k</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"56\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 14 56)\">benefit</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 132\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"44\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"44\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polyline points=\"44,90.4 62.25,66.4 80.5,48 98.75,34.400000000000006 117,24.799999999999997 135.25,19.200000000000003 153.5,16.80000000000001 171.75,16 190,16\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"20\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">more</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">less</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">steps per day</text><text x=\"44\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2k</text><text x=\"80.5\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4k</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6k</text><text x=\"153.5\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8k</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">10k</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"56\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 14 56)\">benefit</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 132\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"44\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"44\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polyline points=\"44,90.4 62.25,66.4 80.5,48 98.75,34.400000000000006 117,24.799999999999997 135.25,21.599999999999994 153.5,36 171.75,52 190,66.4\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"20\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">more</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">less</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">steps per day</text><text x=\"44\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2k</text><text x=\"80.5\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4k</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6k</text><text x=\"153.5\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8k</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">10k</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"56\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 14 56)\">benefit</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 132\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"44\" y1=\"12\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><line x1=\"44\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"92\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><polyline points=\"44,88 62.25,88 80.5,87.2 98.75,87.2 117,86.4 135.25,85.6 153.5,84 171.75,66.4 190,16\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#9A7C82\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-linecap=\"round\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"20\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">more</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"end\">less</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\">steps per day</text><text x=\"44\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">2k</text><text x=\"80.5\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">4k</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">6k</text><text x=\"153.5\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">8k</text><text x=\"190\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">10k</text><text x=\"14\" y=\"56\" fill=\"#8A7F76\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"rotate(-90 14 56)\">benefit</text></svg>"],1,"圖片題（折線圖）","第三段：步數愈多益處愈大，<i>but only up to about 7,500 steps a day; beyond that, there was little additional gain</i>。曲線先上升、到約 7,500 步後趨於平緩的是 (B)。(A) 一路直線上升；(C) 超過後反而下降；(D) 到一萬步才突然上升。","(A) 直線上升　(B) 上升後在約 7,500 步趨平　(C) 上升後下降　(D) 接近一萬步才陡升"],["What is the \"encouraging\" message in the last paragraph?",["Walking is less useful than people once believed.","Only people who walk 10,000 steps will stay healthy.","Even small increases in walking can help less active people.","Smartwatches are the key to a healthy life."],[],2,"推論","末段：不需要達到神奇數字也能受益，<i>adding even a few thousand steps a day can make a real difference</i>，對不常活動的人來說，多走一點就有幫助，選 (C)。(A) 作者明說多走路仍有益；(B) 與原文相反。","(A) 走路沒有人們以為的那麼有用　(B) 只有走滿一萬步的人才會健康　(C) 即使只多走一點，也能幫助不常活動的人　(D) 智慧手錶是健康生活的關鍵"]],["對很多人來說，「每天一萬步」已經成了健身的黃金法則。達標時，智慧手錶會震動祝賀，無數健康宣導活動也不斷重複這個數字。但這個數字是從哪裡來的？出人意料的是，它不是始於科學，而是始於行銷。","1965 年，東京奧運帶動日本健身熱潮後不久，一家公司開始販售一款名為「萬步計」的計步器，意思就是「一萬步計量器」。這個數字響亮好記，而日文的「万」字看起來甚至有點像一個正在走路的人。這項產品大獲成功，一萬步的目標也傳遍全世界。","數十年來，很少有人質疑這個數字。後來研究人員開始檢驗它。2019 年，哈佛的 I-Min Lee 博士主持一項研究，追蹤約一萬六千名年長女性長達數年。每天走約 4,400 步的人，在研究期間的死亡風險明顯低於每天只走約 2,700 步的人。步數愈多，益處愈大，但只到每天約 7,500 步為止；超過之後，額外的好處就很少了。","後來的研究也得出類似的結果。2022 年一項綜合多項研究資料的分析發現，60 歲以上的成人，益處在每天約 6,000 到 8,000 步時趨於平穩；較年輕的成人，益處則持續到約 8,000 到 10,000 步。","這是否表示走一萬步是浪費時間？完全不是。多走路對心臟、大腦和心情依然有益。真正的訊息是令人振奮的：你不必達到某個神奇數字才能受益。對不太活動的人來說，每天即使只多走幾千步，也能帶來真正的改變。說到底，最好的步數也許就是「比你現在走的多一點」。"],[["campaign","n.","宣導活動"],["marketing","n.","行銷"],["pedometer","n.","計步器"],["catchy","adj.","好記的；吸引人的"],["level off","phr.","趨於平穩"],["additional","adj.","額外的"]],314],["《牛津英語詞典》收錄 26 個韓語詞（2021 年語言新聞）",["In September 2021, the Oxford English Dictionary, one of the most respected records of the English language, announced that it had added 26 new words of Korean origin. The list included food words such as \"banchan,\" the small side dishes served with Korean meals, and \"bulgogi,\" thin slices of grilled beef. It also included \"mukbang,\" an online video in which a host eats large amounts of food while talking to viewers, and \"hallyu,\" the Korean word for the wave of interest in Korean culture around the world.","Why would an English dictionary add Korean words? The answer lies in how dictionaries work. Editors do not decide which words people should use. Instead, they study huge collections of real writing, from newspapers to social media, and record the words that English speakers are actually using. When a borrowed word appears often enough, over a long enough period, it earns a place in the dictionary. The 2021 additions reflected the global popularity of K-pop, Korean films and dramas, and Korean food.","English has always been a great borrower. \"Ketchup\" may come from a southern Chinese language, \"tycoon\" from Japanese, \"shampoo\" from Hindi, and \"chocolate\" from a language of Mexico. Through trade, travel, and cultural exchange, English has taken in words from hundreds of languages. Today, the internet speeds up this process, allowing a word to travel around the world in days rather than centuries.","Interestingly, some of the new entries changed meaning on their journey. \"Skinship,\" for example, was created in Japan from two English words and later became popular in Korea, where it refers to physical closeness, such as holding hands or hugging, between family members, friends, or couples. \"Fighting!\"—often spelled \"hwaiting!\"—is used in Korea to cheer someone on, quite different from its usual meaning in English.","Language experts say the 2021 update marked a shift. In the past, English mainly lent its words to other languages. Now, more and more words are flowing in the other direction, showing that English is shaped by its speakers everywhere, not only those in Britain or the United States."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["Why an English dictionary added Korean words and what this shows","How to cook traditional Korean dishes","Why K-pop is popular among teenagers","How dictionary editors learn foreign languages"],[],0,"主旨","首段介紹 2021 年 OED 收錄 26 個韓語詞，第二段說明字典收詞的方式，第三段說英語一向大量借字，第四段舉詞義改變的例子，末段點出英語受世界各地使用者影響。選 (A)。","(A) 英語詞典為何收錄韓語詞，以及這說明了什麼　(B) 如何烹調傳統韓國菜　(C) 韓國流行音樂為何受青少年歡迎　(D) 詞典編輯如何學外語"],["According to the passage, how do dictionary editors decide which words to include?",["They choose words that they personally like.","They ask governments to approve new words.","They include every word used on social media.","They record words that appear often in real writing over time."],[],3,"細節","第二段：編輯研究大量真實文字，記錄人們實際使用的字；借來的字 <i>appears often enough, over a long enough period</i> 才會被收錄，選 (D)。(C) 並非出現過就收，需要夠頻繁、夠長久。","(A) 選自己喜歡的字　(B) 請政府核准新字　(C) 收錄社群媒體上用過的每個字　(D) 記錄長期經常出現在真實文字中的字"],["In Korea, what does \"Fighting!\" usually express?",["Anger toward an enemy","An invitation to a sports match","Encouragement for someone","A warning of danger"],[],2,"細節（詞義）","第四段：Fighting!（hwaiting!）在韓國 <i>is used ... to cheer someone on</i>，用來替人加油打氣，選 (C)。這和英文原本「打鬥」的意思很不同。","(A) 對敵人的憤怒　(B) 邀請參加運動比賽　(C) 為某人加油打氣　(D) 危險警告"],["What can be inferred from the last paragraph?",["English will soon stop borrowing words from other languages.","English is increasingly shaped by speakers around the world.","Only British and American speakers can change English.","Korean will replace English as a global language."],[],1,"推論","末段：過去多是英語把字借給其他語言，現在愈來愈多字反向流入，<i>English is shaped by its speakers everywhere</i>，選 (B)。(A)(C) 與原文相反；(D) 過度推論。","(A) 英語很快就不再向其他語言借字　(B) 英語愈來愈受世界各地使用者影響　(C) 只有英美人士能改變英語　(D) 韓語將取代英語成為國際語言"]],["2021 年 9 月，記錄英語最具權威的辭典之一《牛津英語詞典》宣布新增 26 個源自韓語的詞。名單中有食物相關的字，例如「banchan」（韓式餐點附上的小菜）和「bulgogi」（烤牛肉薄片）；也有「mukbang」（吃播，主持人一邊大量進食一邊和觀眾聊天的網路影片），以及「hallyu」（韓流，指全球對韓國文化的熱潮）。","英語詞典為什麼要收錄韓語詞？答案在於詞典的運作方式。編輯並不決定人們該用哪些字，而是研究大量真實的文字資料，從報紙到社群媒體，記錄英語使用者實際在用的字。當一個外來字出現得夠頻繁、持續得夠久，就能在詞典中占有一席之地。2021 年新增的這些詞，反映了韓國流行音樂、韓國電影與影集，以及韓國美食在全球受歡迎的程度。","英語向來很會「借字」。「ketchup」（番茄醬）可能源自華南的一種語言，「tycoon」（大亨）來自日語，「shampoo」（洗髮精）來自印地語，「chocolate」（巧克力）則來自墨西哥的一種語言。透過貿易、旅行和文化交流，英語吸收了數百種語言的字詞。如今網路更加快了這個過程，讓一個字在幾天內──而不是幾個世紀──就傳遍全世界。","有趣的是，有些新收錄的詞在傳播途中改變了意思。例如「skinship」是日本用兩個英文字創造的詞，後來在韓國流行起來，指家人、朋友或情侶之間牽手、擁抱等肢體上的親近。「Fighting!」（常拼作「hwaiting!」）在韓國是用來替人加油打氣，和它在英文中的一般意思相當不同。","語言專家說，2021 年的這次更新代表一種轉變。過去，主要是英語把字借給其他語言；現在，愈來愈多的字反向流入，顯示英語是由世界各地的使用者共同塑造的，不只是英國或美國的人。"],[["origin","n.","起源"],["side dish","n.","小菜；配菜"],["reflect","v.","反映"],["borrower","n.","借用者"],["entry","n.","（詞典的）詞條"],["cheer sb on","phr.","為某人加油"]],347],["可可價格創新高（2024 年國際經濟新聞）",["Chocolate lovers around the world received unwelcome news in 2024. The price of cocoa, the main ingredient in chocolate, rose to the highest level ever recorded. In early 2023, a ton of cocoa beans sold for about 2,500 US dollars. By December 2024, the price had climbed above 12,000 dollars—nearly five times as much.","The main reason lies in West Africa. More than half of the world's cocoa is grown in just two countries, Côte d'Ivoire (Ivory Coast) and Ghana. In 2023 and 2024, farms in the region suffered from unusual weather: first heavy rains that helped plant diseases spread, then hot, dry conditions that damaged the crop. At the same time, a disease called swollen shoot virus, spread by tiny insects, continued to destroy cocoa trees. Many trees were also old and less productive, and farmers, who often earn very little, could not afford to plant new ones.","With cocoa so expensive, chocolate makers faced difficult choices. Some raised prices. Others made their products smaller, a practice known as \"shrinkflation,\" or changed their recipes, adding more nuts, wafers, or caramel and using less chocolate. Shoppers in many countries noticed that their favorite treats were becoming more expensive—or quietly changing.","The crisis also drew attention to a long-standing problem: the people who grow cocoa rarely benefit from the money chocolate brings in. Many cocoa farmers live in poverty, earning only a small share of the final price of a chocolate bar. Experts warn that unless farmers are paid enough to care for their trees and plant new ones, supply problems will continue.","Prices have since fallen from their peak, but they remain higher than in the past. Scientists are working on cocoa plants that can resist disease, and some companies are investing in new farms in other parts of the world. For now, the next time you unwrap a chocolate bar, you might remember the long journey—and the many challenges—behind it."],[["According to the passage, how did the price of cocoa change between early 2023 and December 2024?",["It doubled.","It rose to nearly five times its earlier level.","It fell by about half.","It stayed about the same."],[],1,"細節（數據）","第一段：2023 年初每噸約 2,500 美元，2024 年 12 月超過 12,000 美元，<i>nearly five times as much</i>，選 (B)。","(A) 漲了一倍　(B) 漲到原本的將近五倍　(C) 跌了大約一半　(D) 大致不變"],["Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of the cocoa shortage?",["Unusual weather in West Africa","A disease spread by insects","Old trees that produce less","A shortage of chocolate factories"],[],3,"細節（NOT）","第二段列出的原因：西非異常天氣（A）、由小昆蟲傳播的腫枝病毒（B）、老樹產量低（C）。文中沒有提到巧克力工廠不足，選 (D)。","(A) 西非的異常天氣　(B) 由昆蟲傳播的疾病　(C) 產量較低的老樹　(D) 巧克力工廠不足"],["Which of the following was one way chocolate makers responded to high cocoa prices?",["Using less chocolate in their recipes","Buying cocoa farms in Ghana","Stopping all chocolate production","Lowering the prices of their products"],[],0,"細節","第三段：有些廠商漲價、縮小份量，或 <i>changed their recipes, adding more nuts, wafers, or caramel and using less chocolate</i>，選 (A)。(D) 與漲價相反。","(A) 在配方中使用較少巧克力　(B) 買下迦納的可可農場　(C) 停止生產所有巧克力　(D) 調降產品價格"],["What does the fourth paragraph suggest?",["Cocoa farmers are becoming rich because of high prices.","Chocolate makers should move their factories to Africa.","Paying farmers fairly may help solve the supply problem.","People should stop buying chocolate."],[],2,"推論","第四段：可可農很少從巧克力賺的錢中受益，專家警告 <i>unless farmers are paid enough to care for their trees and plant new ones, supply problems will continue</i>。反過來說，讓農民得到合理收入有助於解決供應問題，選 (C)。(A) 與「生活貧困」相反。","(A) 可可農因價格高漲而致富　(B) 巧克力廠商應把工廠搬到非洲　(C) 給農民合理報酬可能有助解決供應問題　(D) 人們應停止購買巧克力"]],["2024 年，世界各地的巧克力愛好者收到一個不受歡迎的消息：巧克力的主要原料可可，價格漲到有紀錄以來的最高點。2023 年初，一噸可可豆約賣 2,500 美元；到了 2024 年 12 月，價格已漲破 12,000 美元──將近原本的五倍。","主要原因在西非。全世界一半以上的可可產自象牙海岸和迦納這兩個國家。2023 和 2024 年，當地農場遭遇異常天氣：先是大雨助長植物疾病蔓延，接著又是炎熱乾燥的天氣傷害作物。同時，一種由小昆蟲傳播、名為「腫枝病毒」的疾病持續摧毀可可樹。許多可可樹也已老化、產量下降，而收入往往微薄的農民又無力種植新樹。","可可這麼貴，巧克力廠商面臨艱難的抉擇。有些廠商漲價；有些把產品縮小（也就是所謂的「縮水式通膨」），或改變配方，加入更多堅果、威化餅或焦糖，減少巧克力的用量。許多國家的消費者都注意到，自己最愛的零食變貴了──或者悄悄變了樣。","這場危機也讓人注意到一個長久存在的問題：種植可可的人很少從巧克力帶來的收益中受惠。許多可可農生活貧困，只拿到一條巧克力最終售價中的一小部分。專家警告，除非農民得到足夠的收入來照顧可可樹、種植新樹，否則供應問題將持續下去。","此後價格已從高峰回落，但仍比過去高。科學家正在研發能抵抗疾病的可可品種，有些公司也投資在世界其他地區開闢新農場。下次你拆開一條巧克力時，或許會想起它背後漫長的旅程──以及重重的挑戰。"],[["ingredient","n.","原料"],["productive","adj.","多產的"],["afford","v.","負擔得起"],["recipe","n.","配方；食譜"],["long-standing","adj.","長期存在的"],["poverty","n.","貧窮"]],326]]],[17,[["平溪天燈的傳統與環保爭議（在地文化）",["Every year during the Lantern Festival, the small mountain town of Pingxi in New Taipei City is filled with crowds. People write wishes on large paper lanterns—for good health, success in exams, or love—then light a small fuel pad inside. As the hot air fills the lantern, it rises slowly into the night sky. When hundreds are released together, the sight is so beautiful that it has appeared in travel guides and films, attracting visitors from around the world.","The custom is said to date back more than a century. According to local stories, Pingxi was a remote area where bandits sometimes attacked villages. When villagers fled into the mountains to hide, those who stayed behind would release lanterns to signal that the danger had passed and it was safe to come home. Over time, the lanterns became a symbol of peace and hope, and releasing them became a way to send wishes to the heavens.","However, the beauty of the lanterns comes at a cost. What goes up must come down. When the fuel burns out, the lanterns fall into forests, rivers, and farmland. Their paper, wire frames, and leftover fuel create waste in the hills, and sometimes lanterns that are still burning can start fires. Local farmers have complained of lantern frames tangled in their crops, and animals may be harmed by the wire.","To deal with the problem, the local government and community groups have tried several solutions. Residents have been encouraged to collect fallen lanterns, and a recycling program has offered rewards for those who bring them back. Some shops now sell lanterns made with materials that break down more easily. In recent years, the government has also held lantern events at fixed times and places, which makes it easier to clean up afterward.","The debate over Pingxi's lanterns reflects a question faced by many places: how can we keep a beloved tradition alive while reducing its harm to the environment? For now, Pingxi is trying to do both—letting wishes rise into the sky while making sure they do not leave a mess behind."],[["According to local stories, why did villagers first release lanterns?",["To send wishes to their ancestors","To attract visitors to the town","To celebrate a good harvest","To tell people hiding in the mountains that it was safe to return"],[],3,"細節（起源）","第二段：盜匪來襲時村民躲進山裡，留守的人放天燈 <i>to signal that the danger had passed and it was safe to come home</i>，選 (D)。(A) 向天祈願是後來演變出的意義。","(A) 向祖先傳達願望　(B) 吸引遊客到小鎮　(C) 慶祝豐收　(D) 通知躲在山裡的人可以安全返家"],["What does \"What goes up must come down\" in the third paragraph refer to?",["Lanterns that rise into the sky eventually fall back to the ground.","Prices of lanterns rise and fall.","Visitors who climb the mountains must return home.","Wishes written on lanterns may not come true."],[],0,"推論（句意）","這句話後面接著說 <i>When the fuel burns out, the lanterns fall into forests, rivers, and farmland</i>，指升空的天燈終究會掉回地面、造成垃圾，選 (A)。","(A) 升上天空的天燈終究會掉回地面　(B) 天燈價格漲漲跌跌　(C) 爬山的遊客終究要回家　(D) 寫在天燈上的願望不一定會實現"],["Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way to deal with the problem?",["Rewarding people who bring back fallen lanterns","Selling lanterns that break down more easily","Holding lantern events at fixed times and places","Banning tourists from releasing lanterns"],[],3,"細節（NOT）","第四段提到：回收撿回的天燈給獎勵（A）、販售較易分解的天燈（B）、在固定時間地點舉辦活動（C）。文中沒有說禁止遊客放天燈，選 (D)。","(A) 獎勵撿回天燈的人　(B) 販售較易分解的天燈　(C) 在固定時間地點舉辦放天燈活動　(D) 禁止遊客放天燈"],["What is the author's attitude toward the sky lantern tradition?",["Strongly against it","Uninterested in its future","Completely supportive of it, without any concerns","Balanced, hoping to keep it while reducing harm"],[],3,"作者態度","作者先描寫天燈之美與由來，再說明環境代價與因應措施，末段說平溪 <i>is trying to do both</i>──保留傳統並減少危害。態度持平，選 (D)。","(A) 強烈反對　(B) 不關心它的未來　(C) 完全支持，毫無疑慮　(D) 持平，希望保留傳統並減少危害"]],["每年元宵節期間，新北市的山城平溪總是擠滿人潮。人們在大大的紙燈籠上寫下願望──祈求健康、考試順利或愛情美滿──再點燃燈裡的小塊燃料。熱空氣充滿燈身後，天燈便緩緩升上夜空。數百盞天燈同時施放時，景象美得令人屏息，不但登上旅遊指南和電影，也吸引了世界各地的遊客。","據說這項習俗已有一百多年歷史。根據地方傳說，平溪過去是偏遠地區，時常有盜匪侵擾村莊。村民逃進山裡躲避時，留守的人會施放天燈，通知大家危險已經解除、可以平安回家。久而久之，天燈成了和平與希望的象徵，施放天燈也成為向上天傳達願望的方式。","然而，天燈的美是有代價的。上升的東西終究會落下。燃料燒完後，天燈掉進森林、河流和農田。紙張、鐵絲骨架和殘留的燃料成了山間的垃圾，有時仍在燃燒的天燈甚至可能引發火災。當地農民抱怨天燈骨架纏在作物上，鐵絲也可能傷害動物。","為了處理這個問題，地方政府和社區團體嘗試了幾種辦法。居民被鼓勵撿拾掉落的天燈，回收計畫也提供獎勵給把天燈帶回來的人。有些店家現在販售用較易分解的材料製成的天燈。近年來，政府也在固定的時間和地點舉辦放天燈活動，讓事後清理更容易。","平溪天燈的爭議，反映了許多地方都面臨的問題：如何讓深受喜愛的傳統延續下去，同時減少它對環境的傷害？目前，平溪正努力兩者兼顧──讓願望升上天空，同時確保不留下一團混亂。"],[["release","v.","施放；釋放"],["bandit","n.","盜匪"],["signal","v.","發出信號"],["tangled","adj.","纏繞的"],["reward","n.","獎勵"],["beloved","adj.","深受喜愛的"]],351],["「閏秒」將走入歷史（2022 年國際度量衡大會決議）",["Most people have never noticed it, but some years have been one second longer than others. Since 1972, scientists have added a \"leap second\" to the world's official clocks 27 times. On those occasions, the clock did something unusual: instead of moving from 23:59:59 straight to 00:00:00, it displayed 23:59:60 first. Now, however, the leap second is on its way out.","Why was it needed in the first place? Today, official time is kept by atomic clocks, which measure time with amazing accuracy by counting the vibrations of atoms. But the length of a day depends on Earth's rotation, and Earth does not spin at a perfectly steady speed. The pull of the moon, movements of the ocean, and changes deep inside the planet can speed it up or slow it down slightly. Over time, the difference between atomic time and the time based on Earth's rotation adds up. Whenever the gap approached nine-tenths of a second, a leap second was added to keep the two in step.","For most people, an extra second made no difference. For computers, it was a headache. Many systems are not designed to handle a minute with 61 seconds, and leap seconds have caused websites to crash and computer networks to fail. To avoid such problems, some technology companies began \"smearing\" the extra second, slowing their clocks very slightly over many hours so that the second was spread out instead of added all at once. However, this meant that different systems were briefly showing slightly different times.","In November 2022, representatives from countries around the world voted to stop adding leap seconds by 2035. Instead, the difference between atomic time and Earth's rotation will be allowed to grow larger before any correction is made—perhaps once in a century, using a \"leap minute\" or something similar.","The decision shows how much modern life depends on precise timekeeping. Satellite navigation, banking, and the internet all need clocks that agree with one another down to tiny fractions of a second. In a world run by computers, even a single second matters."],[["Why were leap seconds added to official clocks?",["To make the new year begin at exactly midnight","To keep atomic time in step with Earth's rotation","To give computers extra time to update","To make atomic clocks run faster"],[],1,"細節（原因）","第二段：地球自轉速度不穩定，原子時與依地球自轉計算的時間會逐漸產生差距，差距接近 0.9 秒時就加一個閏秒 <i>to keep the two in step</i>，選 (B)。","(A) 讓新年剛好在午夜開始　(B) 讓原子時與地球自轉保持一致　(C) 給電腦更多更新時間　(D) 讓原子鐘走得更快"],["Which picture shows how an official clock displayed a leap second?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"34\" y=\"8\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">23:59:59</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"37\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"41.5\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" /><polygon points=\"100,45 97.82517232944384,40.49776448823661 102.17482767055616,40.49776448823661\" fill=\"#8A7F76\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"46\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"66\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">00:00:00</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"79.5\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" /><polygon points=\"100,83 97.82517232944384,78.49776448823661 102.17482767055616,78.49776448823661\" fill=\"#8A7F76\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"84\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">00:00:01</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"34\" y=\"8\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">23:59:59</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"37\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"41.5\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" /><polygon points=\"100,45 97.82517232944384,40.49776448823661 102.17482767055616,40.49776448823661\" fill=\"#8A7F76\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"46\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"66\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">23:59:60</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"79.5\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" /><polygon points=\"100,83 97.82517232944384,78.49776448823661 102.17482767055616,78.49776448823661\" fill=\"#8A7F76\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"84\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">00:00:00</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"34\" y=\"8\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">23:59:58</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"37\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"41.5\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" /><polygon points=\"100,45 97.82517232944384,40.49776448823661 102.17482767055616,40.49776448823661\" fill=\"#8A7F76\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"46\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"66\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">23:59:59</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"79.5\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" /><polygon points=\"100,83 97.82517232944384,78.49776448823661 102.17482767055616,78.49776448823661\" fill=\"#8A7F76\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"84\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">23:59:59</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 124\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"34\" y=\"8\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"28\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">23:59:59</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"37\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"41.5\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" /><polygon points=\"100,45 97.82517232944384,40.49776448823661 102.17482767055616,40.49776448823661\" fill=\"#8A7F76\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"46\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"66\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">00:00:00</text><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"75\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"79.5\" stroke=\"#8A7F76\" stroke-width=\"1.4\" /><polygon points=\"100,83 97.82517232944384,78.49776448823661 102.17482767055616,78.49776448823661\" fill=\"#8A7F76\"/><rect x=\"34\" y=\"84\" width=\"132\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#EDE6DC\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#3F3833\" font-size=\"17\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"700\" font-family=\"Menlo, Courier New, monospace\" letter-spacing=\"1\">00:00:00</text></svg>"],1,"圖片題","第一段：加閏秒時，時鐘不是從 23:59:59 直接跳到 00:00:00，而是 <i>displayed 23:59:60 first</i>。順序 23:59:59 → 23:59:60 → 00:00:00，選 (B)。(A) 是一般情形；(C)(D) 都是重複某一秒，不是文中描述的顯示方式。","(A) 正常跳到隔天　(B) 23:59:59 → 23:59:60 → 00:00:00　(C) 23:59:59 重複一次　(D) 00:00:00 重複一次"],["What does \"smearing\" the extra second mean in the third paragraph?",["Spreading the second out over many hours","Deleting the second completely","Adding two seconds instead of one","Announcing the second on websites"],[],0,"字義推論","上下文：科技公司讓時鐘在好幾個小時內極輕微地變慢，<i>so that the second was spread out instead of added all at once</i>，smear 指「把一秒攤開分散到長時間裡」，選 (A)。","(A) 把這一秒分散到好幾個小時中　(B) 完全刪除這一秒　(C) 加兩秒而不是一秒　(D) 在網站上公告這一秒"],["According to the passage, what was decided in November 2022?",["Atomic clocks will no longer be used.","Leap seconds will be added more often.","Leap seconds will stop being added by 2035.","Earth's rotation will be measured only once a century."],[],2,"細節","第四段：各國代表 <i>voted to stop adding leap seconds by 2035</i>，選 (C)。(D) 文中說的是或許一世紀才修正一次差距，不是一世紀才測量一次自轉。","(A) 不再使用原子鐘　(B) 更常加閏秒　(C) 2035 年前停止加閏秒　(D) 每世紀只測量一次地球自轉"]],["大多數人從未察覺，但有些年份比其他年份多了一秒。自 1972 年以來，科學家已經在全球官方時鐘上加了 27 次「閏秒」。每逢這種時候，時鐘會出現不尋常的情況：它不是從 23:59:59 直接跳到 00:00:00，而是先顯示 23:59:60。然而現在，閏秒即將走入歷史。","一開始為什麼需要閏秒？如今的官方時間由原子鐘維持，原子鐘藉由計算原子的振動來測量時間，準確得驚人。但一天的長度取決於地球自轉，而地球的自轉速度並不完全穩定。月球的引力、海洋的運動，以及地球深處的變化，都可能讓自轉稍微加快或變慢。日子一久，原子時與依地球自轉計算的時間之間的差距就會累積。每當差距接近十分之九秒時，就會加一個閏秒，讓兩者保持一致。","對大多數人來說，多一秒沒什麼差別；但對電腦而言，這卻是個頭痛的問題。許多系統並沒有設計成能處理一分鐘有 61 秒的情況，閏秒曾導致網站當機、電腦網路故障。為了避免這些問題，有些科技公司開始把這一秒「抹平」：在好幾個小時內讓時鐘極輕微地變慢，把這一秒分散開來，而不是一次加上去。不過，這也表示不同系統在短時間內會顯示略微不同的時間。","2022 年 11 月，世界各國代表投票決定在 2035 年前停止增加閏秒。今後將容許原子時與地球自轉之間的差距累積得更大，才進行修正──也許一百年一次，改用「閏分」之類的方式。","這項決定顯示現代生活多麼仰賴精確的計時。衛星導航、銀行和網路都需要彼此一致、精確到極小幾分之一秒的時鐘。在一個由電腦運轉的世界裡，即使只是一秒也很重要。"],[["leap second","n.","閏秒"],["accuracy","n.","準確度"],["rotation","n.","自轉；旋轉"],["in step","phr.","一致；同步"],["crash","v.","（電腦）當機"],["precise","adj.","精確的"]],351],["日本 900 萬間空屋（2023 年住宅調查）",["In Japan, there is a word for an empty house: akiya. According to a government survey released in 2024, there were about nine million akiya across the country in 2023—a record high. That means nearly one in every seven homes in Japan was standing empty.","The main reason is Japan's shrinking and aging population. For years, more people have died than have been born, and many young people have moved from the countryside to big cities such as Tokyo and Osaka in search of jobs. When elderly parents in rural towns pass away, their children, who now live and work elsewhere, often have no use for the family home. Some cannot afford to repair it; others find it too troublesome to sell.","Culture also plays a part. In Japan, houses traditionally lose value quickly. Many buyers prefer new homes, partly because building standards for earthquakes have become much stricter over the years, and older houses may not meet them. As a result, an old house in the countryside may be worth little or nothing. Tearing it down costs money, and in some cases, the tax on land with a house on it is lower than on empty land, which gives owners a reason to leave old houses standing.","Empty homes can cause problems for neighborhoods. Without care, they fall apart, become hiding places for animals or pests, and can collapse during typhoons or earthquakes. Rows of abandoned houses can also make a town feel lonely and unsafe, encouraging even more residents to leave.","Some communities are trying to turn the problem into an opportunity. Many local governments run \"akiya banks,\" websites that list empty houses for sale or rent, sometimes at extremely low prices. Young families, artists, and even foreign buyers have bought old homes and turned them into cafés, guesthouses, or workshops. These efforts cannot solve the problem alone, but they show that an empty house does not have to be the end of a story. It can also be the start of a new one."],[["What is the main reason for the large number of empty houses in Japan?",["Japan's population is shrinking and aging.","Many houses were destroyed by earthquakes.","Foreign buyers have bought most of the homes.","The government has banned new buildings."],[],0,"細節（主因）","第二段第一句：<i>The main reason is Japan's shrinking and aging population</i>，死亡多於出生、年輕人移往大城市，選 (A)。(C) 外國買家是末段提到的轉機之一。","(A) 日本人口減少且老化　(B) 許多房屋毀於地震　(C) 外國買家買下大部分房屋　(D) 政府禁止興建新建築"],["According to the third paragraph, why do many Japanese buyers prefer new homes?",["New homes are always cheaper than old ones.","Older homes may not meet stricter earthquake standards.","The government pays people to buy new homes.","Old houses are too large for modern families."],[],1,"細節（原因）","第三段：買家偏好新屋，部分原因是 <i>building standards for earthquakes have become much stricter ... older houses may not meet them</i>，選 (B)。","(A) 新屋一定比舊屋便宜　(B) 舊屋可能不符合更嚴格的耐震標準　(C) 政府付錢給買新屋的人　(D) 舊房子對現代家庭來說太大"],["What is the passage mainly about?",["How to build earthquake-proof houses in Japan","Why young Japanese people prefer big cities","Why Japan has so many empty houses and how some people are using them","How the Japanese government collects land taxes"],[],2,"主旨","第一段點出空屋數量創新高，第二、三段分析原因（人口老化減少、房屋貶值快、拆除成本與稅制），第四段說明空屋帶來的問題，末段介紹再利用的做法。主旨是 (C)。(B)(D) 都只是原因中的細節。","(A) 如何在日本蓋耐震房屋　(B) 日本年輕人為何偏好大城市　(C) 日本為何有這麼多空屋，以及有些人如何利用它們　(D) 日本政府如何徵收土地稅"],["What is the purpose of the last paragraph?",["To warn readers not to buy houses in Japan","To explain how houses are built in Japan","To show how some people are finding new uses for empty houses","To compare Japan with other aging countries"],[],2,"段落功能","末段開頭 <i>Some communities are trying to turn the problem into an opportunity</i>，接著介紹空屋銀行、把老屋改成咖啡館與民宿，說明人們如何為空屋找到新用途，選 (C)。","(A) 警告讀者別在日本買房　(B) 說明日本房屋如何建造　(C) 說明有些人如何為空屋找到新用途　(D) 比較日本與其他高齡化國家"]],["日文裡有個詞專指空屋：akiya（空き家）。根據政府在 2024 年公布的調查，2023 年全日本約有 900 萬間空屋，創下歷史新高。也就是說，日本將近每七間房屋就有一間空著。","主要原因是日本人口減少且老化。多年來，死亡人數一直多於出生人數，許多年輕人也從鄉下搬到東京、大阪等大城市找工作。鄉下小鎮的年邁父母過世後，在外地生活工作的子女往往用不到老家。有些人負擔不起修繕費用，有些人則嫌出售太麻煩。","文化也是原因之一。在日本，房屋傳統上貶值得很快。許多買家偏好新屋，部分原因是耐震建築標準多年來變得嚴格許多，老房子可能不符合。結果，鄉下的一間老房子可能幾乎一文不值。拆除要花錢，而在某些情況下，有房子的土地稅金比空地還低，這也讓屋主有理由讓老房子繼續立在那裡。","空屋會給社區帶來問題。乏人照顧的空屋會逐漸損壞，成為動物或害蟲的藏身處，颱風或地震時還可能倒塌。一整排荒廢的房屋也會讓小鎮顯得冷清又不安全，促使更多居民離開。","有些社區正努力把問題轉為機會。許多地方政府經營「空屋銀行」，在網站上刊登待售或出租的空屋，有時價格低得驚人。年輕家庭、藝術家，甚至外國買家都買下老房子，把它們改成咖啡館、民宿或工作室。這些努力無法單獨解決問題，但它們說明了：一間空屋不一定是故事的結局，也可以是一個新故事的開始。"],[["survey","n.","調查"],["rural","adj.","鄉村的"],["troublesome","adj.","麻煩的"],["strict","adj.","嚴格的"],["abandoned","adj.","廢棄的"],["opportunity","n.","機會"]],339]]],[18,[["死亡谷「會走路的石頭」之謎（自然科學經典題材＋2014 年研究）",["In a dry lakebed called Racetrack Playa, in California's Death Valley National Park, lie hundreds of rocks with a strange secret. Behind each one is a long trail carved into the cracked mud, showing that the rock has moved—sometimes hundreds of meters. Some trails run straight; others curve or zigzag. Yet for decades, no one had ever seen the rocks move.","The \"sailing stones\" puzzled scientists for most of a century. Some weigh as much as 300 kilograms, far too heavy to be pushed by a person without leaving footprints. Over the years, people offered many explanations: strong winds, slippery plants, magnetic forces, and even jokers or aliens. None could be proven, partly because the stones moved so rarely and partly because the area is extremely remote.","In 2011, two cousins, Richard and James Norris, decided to solve the mystery. With the park's permission, they placed several rocks fitted with GPS devices on the playa and set up a weather station and cameras nearby. Then they waited. For two years, nothing happened. Finally, in December 2013, they found the playa covered by a shallow pond with a thin layer of ice on top. As the sun rose and the ice began to melt, it broke into large, thin sheets. A light wind pushed the floating ice sheets across the pond, and the ice pushed the rocks along the soft mud beneath, leaving trails behind. The rocks moved slowly—only a few meters per minute.","The conditions needed are surprisingly specific: enough rain to form a shallow pond, nights cold enough to freeze its surface, sunny days to break the ice, and a gentle wind. Because all of these must happen together, the stones may not move for years. And when the water dries up, the trails remain, but the ice that caused them has disappeared, which explains why the mystery lasted so long.","The researchers published their findings in 2014. Their discovery showed that patience can solve even the strangest puzzles—and that sometimes the answer simply melts away before anyone notices."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["Why Death Valley is the hottest place on Earth","How a long-standing natural mystery was solved","How GPS devices are made","Why tourists should not visit Racetrack Playa"],[],1,"主旨","第一、二段介紹石頭會移動、近百年無人能解的謎，第三段寫研究者如何觀察到真相，第四段說明條件與謎團為何難解，末段總結。主旨是 (B)。","(A) 死亡谷為何是地球最熱的地方　(B) 一個長久的自然之謎如何被解開　(C) GPS 裝置如何製造　(D) 遊客為何不該造訪賽道乾湖"],["According to the passage, why did the mystery last so long?",["Scientists were not allowed to enter the park.","The rocks were too heavy to be studied.","The rocks were moved by visitors at night.","The stones rarely moved, and the ice disappeared afterward."],[],3,"細節（原因）","第二段：石頭 <i>moved so rarely</i>，地點又偏遠；第四段：水乾後只留下痕跡，<i>the ice that caused them has disappeared</i>。兩者合起來就是 (D)。(A) 研究者是經公園許可進行研究的。","(A) 科學家不准進入公園　(B) 石頭太重無法研究　(C) 石頭在夜間被遊客移動　(D) 石頭很少移動，冰事後又消失了"],["Which picture best shows how the sailing stones move?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 130\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"108\" width=\"200\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#C9B08F\"/><text x=\"186\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#6E5243\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"end\">mud</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"94\" width=\"200\" height=\"14\" fill=\"#D5E2E6\"/><text x=\"186\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#5E7F86\" font-size=\"8.5\" text-anchor=\"end\">water</text><rect x=\"46\" y=\"90\" width=\"72\" height=\"5\" fill=\"#F4F8FA\" stroke=\"#9DB6BE\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#5E7F86\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"middle\">thin ice</text><ellipse cx=\"136\" cy=\"96\" rx=\"17\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#8F8780\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"111\" x2=\"118\" y2=\"111\" stroke=\"#7A6046\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\"/><line x1=\"8\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"41.1\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#8D9DB4\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"46,40 39.69687028353126,43.04475873877861 39.69687028353126,36.95524126122139\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><text x=\"27\" y=\"34\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">wind</text><line x1=\"8\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"41.1\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#8D9DB4\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"46,62 39.69687028353126,65.04475873877861 39.69687028353126,58.95524126122139\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><text x=\"27\" y=\"56\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">wind</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 130\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"108\" width=\"200\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#C9B08F\"/><text x=\"186\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#6E5243\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"end\">mud</text><ellipse cx=\"120\" cy=\"96\" rx=\"17\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#8F8780\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"56\" y1=\"111\" x2=\"102\" y2=\"111\" stroke=\"#7A6046\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\"/><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"82\" x2=\"63.1\" y2=\"82\" stroke=\"#8D9DB4\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"68,82 61.69687028353126,85.04475873877861 61.69687028353126,78.95524126122139\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><text x=\"49\" y=\"76\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">wind</text><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"62\" x2=\"63.1\" y2=\"62\" stroke=\"#8D9DB4\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"68,62 61.69687028353126,65.04475873877861 61.69687028353126,58.95524126122139\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><text x=\"49\" y=\"56\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">wind</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 130\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"108\" width=\"200\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#C9B08F\"/><text x=\"186\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#6E5243\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"end\">mud</text><rect x=\"40\" y=\"78\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#E8F0F3\" stroke=\"#9DB6BE\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><text x=\"100\" y=\"98\" fill=\"#5E7F86\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">thick ice</text><ellipse cx=\"118\" cy=\"66\" rx=\"17\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#8F8780\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"14\" y1=\"40\" x2=\"47.1\" y2=\"40\" stroke=\"#8D9DB4\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"52,40 45.69687028353126,43.04475873877861 45.69687028353126,36.95524126122139\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\"/><text x=\"33\" y=\"34\" fill=\"#8D9DB4\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">wind</text></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 130\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><rect x=\"0\" y=\"108\" width=\"200\" height=\"22\" fill=\"#C9B08F\"/><text x=\"186\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#6E5243\" font-size=\"9\" text-anchor=\"end\">mud</text><rect x=\"0\" y=\"80\" width=\"200\" height=\"28\" fill=\"#D5E2E6\"/><text x=\"30\" y=\"98\" fill=\"#5E7F86\" font-size=\"8.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">water</text><ellipse cx=\"136\" cy=\"96\" rx=\"17\" ry=\"13\" fill=\"#8F8780\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.2\"/><line x1=\"70\" y1=\"111\" x2=\"118\" y2=\"111\" stroke=\"#7A6046\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\"/><line x1=\"50\" y1=\"88\" x2=\"85.1\" y2=\"88\" stroke=\"#5E7F86\" stroke-width=\"2.4\" /><polygon points=\"90,88 83.69687028353127,91.04475873877861 83.69687028353127,84.95524126122139\" fill=\"#5E7F86\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"76\" fill=\"#5E7F86\" font-size=\"9.5\" text-anchor=\"middle\">flood</text></svg>"],0,"圖片題","第三段：乾湖積了一層淺水，水面結薄冰；冰融化碎成大片薄冰後，<i>A light wind pushed the floating ice sheets</i>，冰片再推動泥地上的石頭並留下痕跡。風吹薄冰、薄冰推石頭的是 (A)。(B) 沒有水和冰；(C) 石頭在厚冰上；(D) 是洪水沖刷。","(A) 風推動水面薄冰，薄冰推石頭　(B) 風直接吹動乾地上的石頭　(C) 石頭在厚冰上滑動　(D) 洪水沖動石頭"],["Which of the following is NOT one of the conditions needed for the stones to move?",["Enough rain to form a shallow pond","Nights cold enough to freeze the water","Strong storms with powerful winds","Sunny days to break up the ice"],[],2,"細節（NOT）","第四段列出的條件：足夠的雨形成淺池（A）、夜間夠冷能結冰（B）、晴天讓冰碎裂（D），以及 <i>a gentle wind</i>（和緩的風）。強烈暴風不在條件中，選 (C)。","(A) 足以形成淺池的雨量　(B) 冷到能讓水結冰的夜晚　(C) 挾帶強風的暴風雨　(D) 讓冰碎裂的晴天"]],["在加州死亡谷國家公園一處名為「賽道乾湖」的乾涸湖床上，散布著數百顆藏著奇怪祕密的石頭。每顆石頭後方都有一道刻在龜裂泥地上的長長痕跡，顯示石頭曾經移動過──有時長達數百公尺。有些痕跡筆直，有些彎曲或呈之字形。然而數十年來，從來沒有人親眼看過石頭移動。","這些「會航行的石頭」讓科學家困惑了將近一個世紀。有些石頭重達 300 公斤，人不可能推動它卻不留下腳印。多年來，人們提出各種解釋：強風、滑溜的植物、磁力，甚至是惡作劇者或外星人。但沒有一種說法能被證實，一方面是因為石頭很少移動，另一方面是因為這個地方極為偏遠。","2011 年，理查．諾里斯和詹姆斯．諾里斯這對堂兄弟決定解開這個謎。經過公園同意，他們在乾湖上放了幾顆裝有 GPS 裝置的石頭，並在附近架設氣象站和攝影機。然後開始等待。兩年過去了，什麼事也沒發生。終於在 2013 年 12 月，他們發現乾湖積了一層淺淺的水，水面結著一層薄冰。太陽升起、冰開始融化時，冰層碎裂成一大片一大片的薄冰。微風推著漂浮的冰片在水面移動，冰片再推著底下軟泥上的石頭前進，留下痕跡。石頭移動得很慢──每分鐘只有幾公尺。","所需的條件出奇地特定：要有足夠的雨形成淺水池、夜晚要冷到讓水面結冰、要有晴天讓冰碎裂，還要有和緩的風。由於這些條件必須同時發生，石頭可能好幾年都不會動。等到水乾了，痕跡留下來，造成痕跡的冰卻已消失無蹤，這也說明了謎團為何持續這麼久。","研究人員在 2014 年發表了他們的發現。這項發現說明了：耐心能解開最奇怪的謎題──而有時候，答案就在任何人注意到之前悄悄融化了。"],[["lakebed","n.","湖床"],["trail","n.","痕跡；小徑"],["puzzle","v./n.","使困惑；謎"],["remote","adj.","偏遠的"],["permission","n.","許可"],["specific","adj.","特定的"]],343],["全球「遺失錢包」誠實度實驗（2019 年《Science》研究）",["Imagine you work at a hotel front desk, and a stranger hands you a wallet, saying he found it on the street. Inside are a key, a shopping list, and three business cards with the owner's email address. Would you contact the owner? Would your answer change if the wallet contained money?","In 2019, a team of economists set out to answer these questions on a huge scale. Over two years, research assistants \"turned in\" more than 17,000 wallets in 355 cities across 40 countries. The wallets were handed to workers at banks, hotels, post offices, museums, and police stations. Some held no money; others held about 13 US dollars in local currency. The researchers then waited to see how many people would email the owner.","Before revealing the results, the team asked hundreds of experts, as well as ordinary people, to guess what would happen. Most predicted that wallets with money would be returned less often, since people would be tempted to keep the cash. They were wrong. In 38 of the 40 countries, wallets containing money were more likely to be returned than empty ones. Overall, about 51 percent of the wallets with money were reported, compared with about 40 percent of those without. In a smaller follow-up test in three countries, wallets containing nearly 100 dollars were returned even more often.","Why would more money make people more honest? The researchers believe that most people care about seeing themselves as good. Keeping a wallet with a little money might feel like a minor wrong, but keeping one with a lot of money feels like stealing—and people do not want to think of themselves as thieves. Many also imagined how worried the owner would be.","Return rates varied from country to country, with Switzerland and Norway among the highest. But the overall pattern held almost everywhere. The study offers a hopeful message: people may be more honest than we expect. Perhaps, the researchers suggest, we should not be so quick to assume the worst about others."],[["What did the study mainly find?",["Most lost wallets are never returned.","People in big cities are less honest than others.","Wallets with money were more likely to be returned than empty ones.","Hotel workers are more honest than bank workers."],[],2,"主旨（研究發現）","第三段是研究的核心發現：與專家預測相反，<i>wallets containing money were more likely to be returned than empty ones</i>，而且在 40 國中的 38 國都成立，選 (C)。(A) 有錢的錢包約一半被通報；(B)(D) 文中沒有比較。","(A) 大多數遺失的錢包從未被歸還　(B) 大城市的人比較不誠實　(C) 有錢的錢包比空錢包更可能被歸還　(D) 飯店員工比銀行員工誠實"],["What did most experts predict before the results were revealed?",["Wallets with money would be returned less often.","No wallets would be returned at all.","Wallets would be returned only in rich countries.","Money would make no difference."],[],0,"細節","第三段：多數人預測 <i>wallets with money would be returned less often</i>，因為人們會想把錢留下，選 (A)。結果正好相反。","(A) 有錢的錢包較少被歸還　(B) 完全沒有錢包會被歸還　(C) 只有富國的錢包會被歸還　(D) 有沒有錢都沒差別"],["According to the study, about what percentage of wallets were reported to their owners?",["40% of wallets with money; 51% of empty wallets","72% of wallets with money; 13% of empty wallets","About 51% in both groups","51% of wallets with money; 40% of empty wallets"],[],3,"細節（數據）","第三段：<i>about 51 percent of the wallets with money were reported, compared with about 40 percent of those without</i>，選 (D)。(A) 把數字對調了。","(A) 有錢 40%、沒錢 51%　(B) 有錢 72%、沒錢 13%　(C) 兩組都約 51%　(D) 有錢 51%、沒錢 40%"],["According to the researchers, why did more money lead to more returns?",["People hoped to receive a reward.","People did not want to see themselves as thieves.","People were afraid of being caught by the police.","People did not notice the money inside."],[],1,"推論（原因）","第四段：大多數人在意自己是好人，留下有很多錢的錢包 <i>feels like stealing—and people do not want to think of themselves as thieves</i>，選 (B)。(A)(C) 文中未提及。","(A) 人們希望得到報酬　(B) 人們不想把自己看成小偷　(C) 人們怕被警察抓　(D) 人們沒注意到裡面有錢"]],["想像你在飯店櫃台工作，一個陌生人遞給你一個錢包，說是他在街上撿到的。錢包裡有一把鑰匙、一張購物清單，以及三張印有失主電子郵件地址的名片。你會聯絡失主嗎？如果錢包裡有錢，你的答案會不會不同？","2019 年，一組經濟學家著手以極大的規模回答這些問題。兩年間，研究助理在 40 個國家、355 座城市「交出」了一萬七千多個錢包。這些錢包被交給銀行、飯店、郵局、博物館和警察局的工作人員。有些錢包裡沒有錢，有些則裝了約合 13 美元的當地貨幣。研究人員接著等待，看看有多少人會寫電子郵件給失主。","在公布結果之前，團隊請了數百位專家和一般民眾猜猜會發生什麼事。多數人預測，有錢的錢包被歸還的比例會比較低，因為人們會想把錢留下來。他們錯了。在 40 個國家中的 38 個，有錢的錢包比沒錢的錢包更可能被歸還。整體而言，約 51% 有錢的錢包被通報給失主，沒錢的錢包則約為 40%。在三個國家進行的一項較小規模後續測試中，裝了將近 100 美元的錢包被歸還的比例甚至更高。","為什麼錢愈多，人反而愈誠實？研究人員認為，大多數人在意自己是不是好人。留下只有一點錢的錢包，也許只像是個小過錯；但留下有很多錢的錢包就像是偷竊──而人們不想把自己看成小偷。許多人也會想像失主有多著急。","歸還率因國家而異，瑞士和挪威名列前茅。但整體模式幾乎在每個地方都成立。這項研究帶來了一個充滿希望的訊息：人們也許比我們預期的更誠實。研究人員建議，或許我們不該那麼快就把別人往壞處想。"],[["scale","n.","規模"],["currency","n.","貨幣"],["predict","v.","預測"],["tempt","v.","引誘"],["minor","adj.","輕微的"],["assume the worst","phr.","往最壞處想"]],339],["雅加達地層下陷與遷都（2019 年起的國際新聞）",["Jakarta, the capital of Indonesia, is home to more than ten million people—and it is sinking. Some parts of the city, especially along the northern coast, have been dropping by as much as 25 centimeters a year, and around 40 percent of the city now lies below sea level. Experts have warned that large parts of North Jakarta could be underwater by 2050 if nothing changes.","The main cause is not rising seas but what lies beneath the city. Jakarta is built on soft, wet ground near the coast. Because the city's piped water supply cannot meet demand, many households and businesses pump water from underground wells. As water is removed, the soil beneath the city is squeezed and sinks, like a sponge that has been pressed. The weight of countless buildings adds to the problem. At the same time, rising sea levels and heavier rains linked to climate change make flooding even worse.","The government has tried several solutions. A giant sea wall has been built along parts of the coast to keep out the ocean. Officials have also tried to reduce the use of groundwater by expanding the water supply and limiting wells in some buildings. But many experts say a wall alone cannot save a city that keeps sinking behind it.","In 2019, Indonesia's president announced a dramatic plan: to move the capital to a new city called Nusantara, to be built on the island of Borneo, more than 1,000 kilometers away. Supporters say the move will reduce pressure on Jakarta and spread development more evenly across the country. Critics point out that building a capital from scratch is extremely expensive and could harm Borneo's rainforests, home to rare animals such as the orangutan. Construction has continued, but progress has been slower than planned.","Moving government offices, however, will not move Jakarta's people. Millions will remain in the city, which will continue to be Indonesia's center of business. For them, the real solution lies in slowing the sinking—by providing clean piped water so that people no longer need to pump the ground dry."],[["According to the passage, what is the main cause of Jakarta's sinking?",["Pumping water from underground wells","Rising sea levels","Frequent earthquakes","The building of a sea wall"],[],0,"細節（主因）","第二段：<i>The main cause is not rising seas</i>，而是自來水不足，許多家庭和企業抽取地下水，土壤被擠壓而下沉，選 (A)。(B) 海平面上升只會讓淹水更嚴重，不是下陷主因。","(A) 從地下水井抽水　(B) 海平面上升　(C) 頻繁的地震　(D) 興建海堤"],["Why does the author compare the soil under Jakarta to \"a sponge that has been pressed\"?",["To show that the soil is very soft and comfortable","To explain why the city needs more water","To describe the shape of the coastline","To explain how removing water makes the ground sink"],[],3,"寫作手法（比喻）","前句：<i>As water is removed, the soil beneath the city is squeezed and sinks</i>。就像被壓扁的海綿，水被抽走後土壤體積縮小下沉，作者用比喻說明下陷原理，選 (D)。","(A) 表示土壤很軟很舒服　(B) 解釋城市為何需要更多水　(C) 描述海岸線的形狀　(D) 說明抽走水分如何使地面下沉"],["Which of the following is a concern raised by critics of the plan to move the capital?",["The new capital is too close to Jakarta.","The plan is costly and could damage rainforests.","Nusantara has too many people already.","Government workers refuse to move to Borneo."],[],1,"細節","第四段：批評者指出從零打造首都 <i>extremely expensive and could harm Borneo's rainforests</i>，選 (B)。(A) 新首都在一千多公里外。","(A) 新首都離雅加達太近　(B) 計畫耗資龐大且可能破壞雨林　(C) 努山塔拉人口已經太多　(D) 公務員拒絕搬到婆羅洲"],["What does the last paragraph suggest?",["Jakarta will soon be completely empty.","The sea wall has solved the problem.","Moving the capital alone will not solve Jakarta's problem.","Businesses will move to Nusantara first."],[],2,"推論","末段：遷移政府機關 <i>will not move Jakarta's people</i>，數百萬人仍住在雅加達，真正的解方是提供自來水、減緩下陷。可見光遷都無法解決雅加達的問題，選 (C)。","(A) 雅加達很快會完全淨空　(B) 海堤已解決問題　(C) 只靠遷都無法解決雅加達的問題　(D) 企業會先搬到努山塔拉"]],["印尼首都雅加達住著一千多萬人──而它正在下沉。城市的部分地區，尤其是北部沿海一帶，每年下陷多達 25 公分，如今全市約四成的土地低於海平面。專家警告，如果情況不變，北雅加達的大片地區到 2050 年可能會沒入水中。","主要原因不是海平面上升，而是城市底下的狀況。雅加達建在沿海又軟又濕的土地上。由於城市的自來水供應不足，許多家庭和企業從地下水井抽水。地下水被抽走後，城市底下的土壤受到擠壓而下沉，就像被壓扁的海綿。無數建築物的重量更讓問題雪上加霜。同時，與氣候變遷有關的海平面上升和更強的降雨，也讓淹水更加嚴重。","政府嘗試了幾種解決辦法。沿海部分地段已建起巨大的海堤來阻擋海水。官員也試著擴大自來水供應、限制部分建築使用水井，以減少地下水的使用。但許多專家表示，一道堤防救不了一座在堤防後方持續下沉的城市。","2019 年，印尼總統宣布一項大膽的計畫：把首都遷到一座名為努山塔拉的新城市，地點在一千多公里外的婆羅洲島上。支持者說，遷都能減輕雅加達的壓力，讓全國的發展更均衡。批評者則指出，從零開始打造首都極為昂貴，還可能破壞婆羅洲的雨林──那裡是紅毛猩猩等珍稀動物的家。工程持續進行，但進度比原先規劃的慢。","然而，遷移政府機關並不會讓雅加達的居民跟著搬走。數百萬人將留在這座城市，而它仍將是印尼的商業中心。對他們來說，真正的解方在於減緩下陷──提供乾淨的自來水，讓人們不再需要把地底抽乾。"],[["sink","v.","下沉"],["pump","v.","抽（水）"],["squeeze","v.","擠壓"],["dramatic","adj.","大膽的；戲劇性的"],["from scratch","phr.","從零開始"],["rainforest","n.","雨林"]],348]]],[19,[["魔術方塊 50 週年（日常事物起源＋2024 年時事）",["In 1974, a young Hungarian professor of architecture named Ernő Rubik made a small wooden cube in his apartment in Budapest. He was not trying to create a toy. He wanted a model to help his students understand how objects move in three-dimensional space. The challenge was how to let the pieces turn in different directions without the whole cube falling apart. After solving that problem, he painted each side a different color, gave the cube a few twists—and then found he could not put it back in order. It took him about a month to solve his own puzzle.","The puzzle, which Rubik called the \"Magic Cube,\" went on sale in Hungary in 1977. In 1980, a toy company began selling it around the world under a new name: the Rubik's Cube. It became a sensation. Within a few years, around 100 million cubes had been sold, and books explaining how to solve it became bestsellers.","What makes the cube so difficult? A standard cube has six faces, each with nine colored squares. It is made of 26 small pieces: 6 center pieces with one color each, 12 edge pieces with two colors, and 8 corner pieces with three colors. The center pieces never change places, so they decide the color of each face. The number of possible arrangements is enormous—more than 43 quintillion, or 43 followed by 18 zeros. Yet in 2010, researchers used powerful computers to prove that any mixed-up cube can be solved in 20 moves or fewer.","After the first craze faded, the cube made a comeback in the 2000s, thanks partly to online videos that taught solving methods. Speed-solving competitions now attract players of all ages, and the fastest solvers can finish in just a few seconds.","In 2024, the Rubik's Cube celebrated its 50th birthday. Rubik himself has said that the cube's appeal lies in its simplicity: it needs no instructions, no batteries, and no language. Anyone can pick it up—and almost anyone can get hooked trying to solve it."],[["Which of the following is the best title for the passage?",["How to Solve a Puzzle in Twenty Moves","The History of Toys in Hungary","A Classroom Model That Became a World Favorite","Why Speed-Solving Is Good for the Brain"],[],2,"標題","文章寫魔術方塊最初是建築系教授為教學做的立體模型，後來風靡全球、再度翻紅、迎來 50 週年。最佳標題是「從教學模型變成全球熱門玩具」，選 (C)。(A) 二十步只是第三段的細節。","(A) 如何用二十步解開謎題　(B) 匈牙利玩具史　(C) 從教學模型變成全球最愛　(D) 速解為何有益大腦"],["Why did Ernő Rubik first make the cube?",["To help his students understand movement in three dimensions","To win a toy-design contest","To sell it to a large toy company","To teach children their colors"],[],0,"細節（目的）","第一段：他不是要做玩具，而是 <i>wanted a model to help his students understand how objects move in three-dimensional space</i>，選 (A)。","(A) 幫助學生理解物體在三維空間中的移動　(B) 贏得玩具設計比賽　(C) 賣給大型玩具公司　(D) 教小孩認識顏色"],["Each picture shows a single piece from a standard cube, with its inside surfaces shown in dark gray. Which picture shows an edge piece?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><polygon points=\"100,24 134.8,44 100,64 65.2,44\" fill=\"#E2C04F\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"65.2,44 100,64 100,104 65.2,84\" fill=\"#6F8FB8\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"100,64 134.8,44 134.8,84 100,104\" fill=\"#6E9F6A\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><polygon points=\"100,24 134.8,44 100,64 65.2,44\" fill=\"#C0675B\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"65.2,44 100,64 100,104 65.2,84\" fill=\"#6F8FB8\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"100,64 134.8,44 134.8,84 100,104\" fill=\"#4A4440\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><polygon points=\"100,24 134.8,44 100,64 65.2,44\" fill=\"#6E9F6A\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"65.2,44 100,64 100,104 65.2,84\" fill=\"#4A4440\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"100,64 134.8,44 134.8,84 100,104\" fill=\"#4A4440\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 120\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><polygon points=\"100,24 134.8,44 100,64 65.2,44\" fill=\"#C0675B\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"65.2,44 100,64 100,104 65.2,84\" fill=\"#C0675B\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><polygon points=\"100,64 134.8,44 134.8,84 100,104\" fill=\"#4A4440\" stroke=\"#2E2925\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/></svg>"],1,"圖片題","第三段：中心塊一種顏色、<i>12 edge pieces with two colors</i>、角塊三種顏色。(B) 有兩個不同顏色的面，是邊塊。(A) 三色是角塊；(C) 一色是中心塊；(D) 兩面同色，標準方塊上每一塊的各面顏色都不同，不會出現。","(A) 三色（角塊）　(B) 兩種顏色（邊塊）　(C) 一色（中心塊）　(D) 兩面同色（不存在）"],["What did researchers prove in 2010?",["The cube has exactly 43 possible arrangements.","Computers can solve the cube faster than humans.","The center pieces can be moved to other faces.","Any mixed-up cube can be solved in 20 moves or fewer."],[],3,"細節","第三段：2010 年研究人員用電腦證明 <i>any mixed-up cube can be solved in 20 moves or fewer</i>，選 (D)。(A) 是 43 quintillion（43 後面加 18 個零）；(C) 中心塊不會換位。","(A) 方塊剛好有 43 種排列　(B) 電腦解方塊比人快　(C) 中心塊可以移到其他面　(D) 任何打亂的方塊都能在 20 步以內解開"]],["1974 年，匈牙利一位年輕的建築系教授厄爾諾．魯比克在布達佩斯的公寓裡做了一個小木頭方塊。他並不是想做玩具，而是想要一個模型，幫助學生理解物體如何在三維空間中移動。難題在於如何讓各個小塊朝不同方向轉動，整個方塊卻不會散開。解決這個問題後，他把每一面漆上不同顏色，轉了幾下──然後發現自己轉不回來了。他花了大約一個月才解開自己的謎題。","這個魯比克稱為「魔術方塊」的益智玩具，1977 年在匈牙利上市。1980 年，一家玩具公司以新名字「魯比克方塊」把它賣到全世界，造成轟動。短短幾年內就賣出約一億個，教人解方塊的書也成了暢銷書。","方塊為什麼這麼難？標準方塊有六個面，每面有九個色塊。它由 26 個小塊組成：6 個單色的中心塊、12 個雙色的邊塊，以及 8 個三色的角塊。中心塊永遠不會換位置，所以它們決定了每一面的顏色。可能的排列方式多得驚人──超過 4,300 京種，也就是 43 後面加 18 個零。然而在 2010 年，研究人員用強大的電腦證明，任何打亂的方塊都能在 20 步以內解開。","第一波熱潮退去之後，方塊在 2000 年代捲土重來，部分要歸功於教人解法的網路影片。速解比賽如今吸引各年齡層的玩家，最快的高手只要短短幾秒就能完成。","2024 年，魔術方塊迎來 50 歲生日。魯比克本人曾說，方塊的魅力在於它的簡單：不需要說明書、不需要電池，也不需要語言。任何人都能拿起來玩──而且幾乎每個人都會在試著解開它的過程中上癮。"],[["architecture","n.","建築學"],["three-dimensional","adj.","三維的；立體的"],["twist","n./v.","轉動；扭轉"],["sensation","n.","轟動的事物"],["arrangement","n.","排列"],["get hooked","phr.","上癮；著迷"]],340],["2024 年 5 月強烈地磁暴與極光（太空天氣新聞）",["On the night of May 10, 2024, people in places that almost never see the northern lights looked up and saw the sky glowing pink, purple, and green. The colorful lights appeared as far south as Florida and Mexico in North America, and as far as northern China and Japan in Asia. Many people captured them with their phones, whose cameras can pick up colors more clearly than the human eye. It was the strongest geomagnetic storm to hit Earth in more than 20 years.","Auroras are caused by the sun. Besides light and heat, the sun constantly sends out a stream of charged particles known as the solar wind. Sometimes, it also throws out huge clouds of these particles in explosions. When such a cloud reaches Earth, usually one to three days later, it disturbs the planet's magnetic field. Particles pour into the upper atmosphere near the poles and crash into the gases there, making them glow—oxygen gives off green and red light, while nitrogen produces blue and purple. Normally, auroras are seen only in places near the Arctic and Antarctic, but a very strong storm can push them much farther from the poles. The May 2024 storm came during the most active stage of the sun's roughly 11-year cycle.","Beautiful as they are, solar storms can also cause serious trouble. In 1859, a powerful storm sent electric currents through telegraph lines, giving shocks to operators and even starting fires. In 1989, a storm caused a blackout across the Canadian province of Quebec, leaving millions of people without power for about nine hours. Today, our dependence on technology makes us even more vulnerable. Storms can damage satellites, disrupt GPS signals, and interfere with radio communication. In February 2022, a smaller storm caused about 40 newly launched satellites to fall out of orbit.","For this reason, scientists watch the sun closely and give warnings when a storm is coming. Such warnings allow power companies and satellite operators to protect their equipment. For the rest of us, they offer something more enjoyable: a chance to step outside and look up."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How to take photos of the night sky","Why Florida has good weather","The history of the telegraph","What causes auroras and how solar storms can affect Earth"],[],3,"主旨","第一段寫 2024 年 5 月的強烈地磁暴與極光，第二段說明極光的成因，第三段說明太陽風暴造成的災害，末段談預警。主旨是 (D)。","(A) 如何拍攝夜空　(B) 佛羅里達為何天氣好　(C) 電報的歷史　(D) 極光的成因以及太陽風暴如何影響地球"],["According to the passage, which gas gives off green light in an aurora?",["Oxygen","Nitrogen","Carbon dioxide","Hydrogen"],[],0,"細節","第二段：<i>oxygen gives off green and red light, while nitrogen produces blue and purple</i>，綠光來自氧，選 (A)。","(A) 氧　(B) 氮　(C) 二氧化碳　(D) 氫"],["Why could auroras be seen so far from the poles in May 2024?",["The sun was in its least active stage.","People used better cameras than before.","The geomagnetic storm was unusually strong.","The northern lights moved south permanently."],[],2,"細節（原因）","第一段說這是二十多年來最強的地磁暴；第二段：<i>a very strong storm can push them much farther from the poles</i>，選 (C)。(A) 當時正值太陽最活躍的階段；(B) 手機只是讓顏色更容易拍到。","(A) 太陽處於最不活躍的階段　(B) 人們的相機比以前好　(C) 這次地磁暴異常強烈　(D) 北極光永久南移"],["Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a problem caused by solar storms?",["Power blackouts","Earthquakes","Damage to satellites","Shocks to telegraph operators"],[],1,"細節（NOT）","第三段提到：1989 年魁北克大停電（A）、損壞衛星（C）、1859 年電報員遭電擊（D），以及干擾 GPS 與無線電。文中沒有提到地震，選 (B)。","(A) 大停電　(B) 地震　(C) 衛星受損　(D) 電報員遭電擊"]],["2024 年 5 月 10 日夜裡，住在幾乎從來看不到北極光地方的人抬頭一看，發現天空泛著粉紅、紫色和綠色的光芒。在北美洲，絢麗的光一路出現在南至佛羅里達和墨西哥的地方；在亞洲，則遠及中國北部和日本。許多人用手機拍下這一幕，因為手機相機捕捉顏色的能力比肉眼更清楚。這是二十多年來襲擊地球最強烈的一次地磁暴。","極光是太陽造成的。除了光和熱，太陽還不斷送出一股稱為「太陽風」的帶電粒子流。有時，太陽也會在爆發中拋出巨大的粒子雲。這樣的粒子雲通常在一到三天後抵達地球，擾動地球的磁場。粒子湧入兩極附近的高層大氣，撞擊那裡的氣體，使它們發光──氧發出綠光和紅光，氮則產生藍光和紫光。一般來說，只有北極和南極附近的地方才看得到極光，但非常強烈的風暴能把極光推到離兩極遠得多的地方。2024 年 5 月的這場風暴，正好發生在太陽約 11 年週期中最活躍的階段。","太陽風暴雖然美麗，卻也可能造成嚴重的麻煩。1859 年，一場強烈的風暴讓電流竄過電報線路，電擊電報員，甚至引發火災。1989 年，一場風暴造成加拿大魁北克省大停電，數百萬人停電約九小時。如今我們對科技的依賴，讓我們更加脆弱。風暴可能損壞衛星、干擾 GPS 訊號和無線電通訊。2022 年 2 月，一場規模較小的風暴就讓約 40 顆剛發射的衛星脫離軌道墜落。","因此，科學家密切觀察太陽，並在風暴來臨前發出警報。這些警報讓電力公司和衛星業者能保護他們的設備。對我們其他人來說，警報帶來的是更愉快的事：一個走出戶外、抬頭仰望的機會。"],[["capture","v.","捕捉；拍下"],["particle","n.","粒子"],["disturb","v.","擾亂"],["blackout","n.","大停電"],["vulnerable","adj.","脆弱的；易受傷害的"],["interfere with","phr.","干擾"]],350],["斯瓦爾巴全球種子庫（糧食安全與氣候）",["On a frozen mountainside on Spitsbergen, an island in the Arctic Ocean about 1,300 kilometers from the North Pole, a narrow concrete entrance leads deep into the rock. Behind its doors lies one of the world's most important storerooms: the Svalbard Global Seed Vault. Opened in 2008 by the Norwegian government, it holds more than a million samples of seeds from crops such as rice, wheat, beans, and potatoes, sent from almost every country in the world.","The vault serves as a backup for the world's food supply. Around the globe, there are more than 1,700 seed banks that store the seeds of crops and their wild relatives. These collections are extremely important, because old and local varieties may carry genes that help plants survive drought, disease, or heat. But seed banks can be damaged by war, natural disasters, or simple accidents like a power failure. The Svalbard vault keeps extra copies, so that if a seed bank loses its collection, the seeds can be returned.","The location was chosen with great care. The island is remote and free from earthquakes and volcanoes, and the vault sits about 130 meters above sea level, high enough to stay dry even if the polar ice melts. Inside, the seeds are kept at minus 18 degrees Celsius. Even if the cooling system failed, the surrounding permafrost—ground that stays frozen all year—would keep the seeds cold for many years.","The vault has already proved its worth. In 2015, scientists from a research center that had been forced to leave Aleppo, Syria, because of war became the first to withdraw seeds from it. They used the seeds to rebuild their collection in Lebanon and Morocco, and later sent new copies back to Svalbard.","Yet the vault is not immune to climate change. In 2016, unusually warm temperatures caused melting water to leak into the entrance tunnel. No seeds were harmed, but the incident led to major improvements. It was a reminder that even a \"doomsday vault\" needs care—and that protecting the world's food supply is a task that never ends."],[["What is the main purpose of the Svalbard Global Seed Vault?",["To grow crops in the Arctic","To sell seeds to farmers around the world","To keep backup copies of seeds in case seed banks lose them","To study plants that grow in frozen ground"],[],2,"細節（目的）","第二段：種子庫是 <i>a backup for the world's food supply</i>，保存備份，萬一某個種子銀行失去收藏，種子可以送回去，選 (C)。","(A) 在北極種植作物　(B) 把種子賣給世界各地農民　(C) 保存種子備份，以防種子銀行失去收藏　(D) 研究生長在凍土中的植物"],["Which of the following is NOT given as a reason for choosing the vault's location?",["The island is remote.","It is free from earthquakes and volcanoes.","It is high enough to stay dry if polar ice melts.","It is close to many large farms."],[],3,"細節（NOT）","第三段列出選址理由：偏遠（A）、沒有地震與火山（B）、海拔夠高不怕冰融淹水（C），還有永凍土能保持低溫。文中沒有說靠近大型農場，選 (D)。","(A) 島嶼地處偏遠　(B) 沒有地震和火山　(C) 地勢夠高，極地冰融也不會淹水　(D) 靠近許多大型農場"],["What does the event in 2015 show?",["The vault can help restore collections that have been lost.","Seeds stored in the vault cannot be taken out.","The vault was built mainly for Syria.","War has damaged the vault itself."],[],0,"推論","第四段：因戰爭撤離阿勒坡的研究中心首度從種子庫取回種子，在黎巴嫩和摩洛哥重建收藏，並把新備份送回。這證明種子庫能幫助恢復失去的收藏，即 <i>proved its worth</i>，選 (A)。(B) 與「取出種子」矛盾。","(A) 種子庫能幫助恢復已失去的收藏　(B) 種子庫裡的種子無法取出　(C) 種子庫主要為敘利亞而建　(D) 戰爭損壞了種子庫本身"],["What does the phrase \"not immune to\" in the last paragraph most likely mean?",["completely safe from","not completely protected from","responsible for causing","unaware of"],[],1,"字義推論","後文：2016 年異常高溫讓融水滲入入口隧道。可見種子庫也會受氣候變遷影響，not immune to 指「無法完全免於」，選 (B)。(A) 意思相反。","(A) 完全不受…影響　(B) 無法完全免於…　(C) 造成…的原因　(D) 沒有察覺…"]],["在北冰洋的斯匹茲卑爾根島上，一座距離北極點約 1,300 公里的冰封山坡上，一道狹窄的水泥入口通往岩石深處。門後是世界上最重要的儲藏室之一：斯瓦爾巴全球種子庫。這座由挪威政府在 2008 年啟用的種子庫，保存了一百多萬份種子樣本，包括稻米、小麥、豆類和馬鈴薯等作物，來自世界上幾乎每一個國家。","種子庫是全球糧食供應的備份。全世界有一千七百多座種子銀行，保存作物及其野生近親的種子。這些收藏極為重要，因為古老的地方品種可能帶有幫助植物撐過乾旱、疾病或高溫的基因。但種子銀行可能因戰爭、天災，或停電之類的意外而受損。斯瓦爾巴種子庫保存額外的備份，萬一某座種子銀行失去收藏，種子就能送回去。","種子庫的地點經過精心挑選。這座島地處偏遠，沒有地震和火山；種子庫位於海拔約 130 公尺處，即使極地冰層融化也不會淹水。庫內的種子保存在攝氏零下 18 度。就算冷卻系統故障，周圍的永凍土──終年結凍的土地──也能讓種子維持低溫許多年。","種子庫已經證明了它的價值。2015 年，一個因戰爭被迫撤離敘利亞阿勒坡的研究中心的科學家，成為第一批從種子庫取回種子的人。他們用這些種子在黎巴嫩和摩洛哥重建收藏，之後再把新的備份送回斯瓦爾巴。","然而，種子庫也無法完全免於氣候變遷的影響。2016 年，異常的高溫讓融水滲進入口隧道。雖然沒有種子受損，這起事件卻促成了大規模的改善工程。它提醒我們，即使是「末日種子庫」也需要照顧──而保護全世界的糧食供應，是一項永無止境的任務。"],[["vault","n.","（地下）儲藏庫；金庫"],["backup","n.","備份"],["variety","n.","品種"],["copy","n.","副本；備份"],["permafrost","n.","永凍土"],["immune","adj.","免疫的；不受影響的"]],348]]],[20,[["撞擊測試假人與性別差異（2019、2022 年交通安全研究）",["Every new car sold today has been tested in crashes—not with real people, but with crash test dummies. These human-shaped models are packed with sensors that measure the forces on the head, neck, chest, and legs during a collision. The data helps engineers design safer seat belts, airbags, and car bodies. But for decades, there has been a problem with these dummies: most of them are based on the body of an average man.","The most widely used dummy was developed in the 1970s to represent a man about 175 centimeters tall and weighing about 78 kilograms. For years, it was the main dummy used in crash tests around the world. When a \"female\" dummy was introduced, it was mostly a scaled-down version of the male dummy, representing a very small woman about 150 centimeters tall. In many tests, it was placed only in the passenger seat, not the driver's seat.","Women's bodies, however, are not just smaller versions of men's. On average, they have different neck strength, different hip shapes, and a different spread of weight. These differences can affect how a body moves in a crash. A 2019 study by researchers at the University of Virginia found that in similar frontal crashes, women wearing seat belts were 73 percent more likely than men to be seriously injured. Part of the difference may come from the types of cars women tend to drive, but many experts believe the dummies also play a role.","In 2022, a team of Swedish engineers led by Astrid Linder introduced the world's first dummy designed to represent an average woman's body, rather than a smaller version of a man's. Linder argues that safety tests should use dummies of both sexes and of different sizes, so that cars protect everyone equally.","Change has been slow, partly because the rules for official crash tests take years to update. But the issue has drawn growing attention. As Linder and others point out, when safety is designed around only one type of body, everyone else is left at greater risk."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How airbags were invented","Why crash tests may not protect women as well as men","How to choose a safe car","Why Sweden makes the safest cars"],[],1,"主旨","第一段指出假人多以男性體型為準，第二段說明「女性」假人只是縮小版，第三段說明女性身體差異與受傷風險較高，第四、五段寫新的女性假人與改變緩慢。主旨是撞擊測試可能無法同等保護女性，選 (B)。","(A) 安全氣囊如何發明　(B) 撞擊測試為何可能無法像保護男性一樣保護女性　(C) 如何挑選安全的車　(D) 瑞典為何能造出最安全的車"],["Which of the following is true about the earlier \"female\" crash test dummy?",["It was designed after studying many women's bodies.","It was usually placed in the driver's seat.","It represented an average-sized woman.","It was mostly a smaller version of the male dummy."],[],3,"細節","第二段：早期的女性假人 <i>was mostly a scaled-down version of the male dummy</i>，代表身高約 150 公分的嬌小女性，而且常只放在副駕駛座，選 (D)。(C) 代表平均女性體型的是 2022 年的新假人。","(A) 研究許多女性身體後設計　(B) 通常放在駕駛座　(C) 代表平均體型的女性　(D) 大致是男性假人的縮小版"],["According to the 2019 study, women wearing seat belts in similar frontal crashes were 73 percent more likely than men to ____.",["be seriously injured","drive smaller cars","cause an accident","forget their seat belts"],[],0,"細節（數據）","第三段：<i>women wearing seat belts were 73 percent more likely than men to be seriously injured</i>，選 (A)。(B) 開車型的差異只是可能的部分原因，不是這個數據的內容。","(A) 受重傷　(B) 開較小的車　(C) 造成事故　(D) 忘記繫安全帶"],["What does Astrid Linder suggest?",["Women should drive only large cars.","Crash tests should no longer use dummies.","Crash tests should use dummies of both sexes and different sizes.","Seat belts should be designed only for women."],[],2,"細節（觀點）","第四段：Linder 主張 <i>safety tests should use dummies of both sexes and of different sizes, so that cars protect everyone equally</i>，選 (C)。(D) 她的目標是保護所有人，不是只為女性設計。","(A) 女性應該只開大車　(B) 撞擊測試不該再用假人　(C) 撞擊測試應使用不同性別與體型的假人　(D) 安全帶應只為女性設計"]],["如今售出的每一輛新車都經過撞擊測試──測試用的不是真人，而是撞擊測試假人。這些人形模型裝滿感應器，能測量碰撞時頭部、頸部、胸部和腿部承受的力量。這些數據幫助工程師設計更安全的安全帶、安全氣囊和車身。但數十年來，這些假人一直有個問題：大多數是依照一般男性的體型打造的。","使用最廣泛的假人是在 1970 年代開發的，代表一位身高約 175 公分、體重約 78 公斤的男性。多年來，它一直是世界各地撞擊測試的主要假人。後來推出的「女性」假人，大致只是男性假人的縮小版，代表一位身高約 150 公分的非常嬌小的女性。在許多測試中，它只被放在副駕駛座，而不是駕駛座。","然而，女性的身體並不只是縮小版的男性身體。平均而言，女性的頸部力量、臀部形狀和體重分布都不同，這些差異會影響身體在撞擊中的移動方式。維吉尼亞大學的研究人員在 2019 年的一項研究發現，在類似的正面撞擊中，繫著安全帶的女性受重傷的可能性比男性高出 73%。部分差異可能來自女性傾向駕駛的車型，但許多專家認為假人也是原因之一。","2022 年，一支由阿絲特麗．林德帶領的瑞典工程師團隊推出全世界第一個以平均女性身體為設計基準、而非男性縮小版的假人。林德主張，安全測試應該使用兩種性別、不同體型的假人，讓汽車平等地保護每一個人。","改變的腳步很慢，部分原因是官方撞擊測試的規定需要好幾年才能更新。但這個議題正受到愈來愈多關注。正如林德等人所指出的：當安全只以一種體型為設計中心，其他所有人都會暴露在更大的風險中。"],[["dummy","n.","假人"],["sensor","n.","感應器"],["collision","n.","碰撞"],["represent","v.","代表"],["scaled-down","adj.","按比例縮小的"],["frontal","adj.","正面的"]],343],["台灣便利商店的密度與服務（在地生活）",["For many visitors to Taiwan, one of the most surprising sights is not a temple or a night market but a convenience store. In some city neighborhoods, you can stand at one store and see another across the street. Taiwan has more than 13,000 convenience stores—about one for every 1,700 people, one of the highest rates in the world.","What makes these stores remarkable is not just their number but what they offer. Of course, they sell snacks, drinks, and ready-made meals, often heated on the spot. But they also work as small service centers. Customers can pay electricity, water, and phone bills; pay parking fees and traffic tickets; send and pick up packages; print documents and photos; buy train and concert tickets from a machine; and take out cash. Many stores have seating areas where students study or office workers eat lunch, and some offer free Wi-Fi and restrooms.","Several factors help explain this success. Taiwan's cities are crowded, and many people live in small apartments, so a nearby store is truly convenient. Many people work long hours or study late at night, and the stores are open 24 hours a day. In addition, the stores compete fiercely, so they keep adding new services to attract customers. Paying bills and picking up packages bring people through the door, and once inside, many end up buying something else.","The stores also play a role during emergencies. During typhoons, when many shops close, convenience stores often stay open, providing food, water, and a place to charge phones. Some also serve as safe places where people who feel threatened can ask for help.","Not everything about the stores is positive. Workers often face long shifts and must learn a huge range of tasks, from making coffee to handling complicated payments. Some critics also worry that the stores' convenience encourages a throwaway lifestyle, with single-use cups and packaging. Still, for millions of people in Taiwan, the convenience store is more than a shop. It is part of daily life, open whenever it is needed."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How to open a convenience store in Taiwan","Why night markets are losing customers","How typhoons affect businesses in Taiwan","Why convenience stores in Taiwan are so common and useful"],[],3,"主旨","第一段點出台灣便利商店密度極高，第二段介紹多元服務，第三段分析成功原因，第四段寫緊急時的角色，末段談缺點與總結。主旨是 (D)。","(A) 如何在台灣開便利商店　(B) 夜市為何流失顧客　(C) 颱風如何影響台灣的商家　(D) 台灣便利商店為何如此普遍又實用"],["Which of the following services is NOT mentioned in the passage?",["Paying electricity bills","Seeing a doctor","Picking up packages","Buying train tickets"],[],1,"細節（NOT）","第二段列出：繳電費等帳單（A）、寄取包裹（C）、用機器買火車票和演唱會門票（D），還有列印、領錢等。文中沒有提到看醫生，選 (B)。","(A) 繳電費　(B) 看醫生　(C) 取包裹　(D) 買火車票"],["According to the third paragraph, which of the following helps explain the stores' success?",["The government owns most of the stores.","Prices are lower than in supermarkets.","The stores are open all day and night for people with long hours.","Most people in Taiwan do not cook at home."],[],2,"細節（原因）","第三段：許多人工作或讀書到很晚，<i>the stores are open 24 hours a day</i>，選 (C)。其他原因還有城市擁擠、住處小、彼此競爭不斷推出新服務。(A)(B)(D) 文中未提及。","(A) 政府擁有大部分店家　(B) 價格比超市低　(C) 商店全天候營業，適合長時間工作的人　(D) 大多數台灣人不在家煮飯"],["What is the purpose of the last paragraph?",["To point out some drawbacks before giving a final view","To describe how to make coffee","To argue that convenience stores should close at night","To compare Taiwan with other countries"],[],0,"段落功能","末段以 <i>Not everything about the stores is positive</i> 開頭，提出店員工時長、助長拋棄式生活等缺點，接著以 <i>Still</i> 轉折總結便利商店已是生活的一部分。先指出缺點、再給出結論，選 (A)。","(A) 先指出一些缺點，再提出最後的看法　(B) 說明如何煮咖啡　(C) 主張便利商店夜間應關門　(D) 比較台灣與其他國家"]],["對許多來台灣的旅客來說，最令人驚訝的景象之一不是廟宇或夜市，而是便利商店。在某些城市街區，你站在一家店門口就能看到對街的另一家。台灣有一萬三千多家便利商店，大約每一千七百人就有一家，是全世界密度最高的地方之一。","這些商店了不起的地方不只是數量，還有它們提供的東西。當然，它們賣零食、飲料和現成餐點，常常還能當場加熱。但它們也像小型的服務中心：顧客可以繳電費、水費和電話費；繳停車費和交通罰單；寄送和領取包裹；列印文件和照片；用機器購買火車票和演唱會門票；還能提領現金。許多店有用餐區，學生在那裡讀書，上班族在那裡吃午餐；有些還提供免費 Wi-Fi 和廁所。","有幾個因素有助於解釋這樣的成功。台灣的城市人口稠密，許多人住在小公寓裡，所以附近有一家店真的很方便。許多人工作時間長或讀書到深夜，而便利商店 24 小時營業。此外，各家店競爭激烈，因此不斷增加新服務來吸引顧客。繳帳單和取包裹把人帶進店裡，而一進門，許多人最後還會順手買點別的東西。","便利商店在緊急時刻也扮演一定的角色。颱風來襲、許多商店關門時，便利商店往往照常營業，提供食物、飲水和替手機充電的地方。有些店也是讓感到受威脅的人可以求助的安全據點。","便利商店並非樣樣都好。店員常常要輪很長的班，還得學會從煮咖啡到處理複雜繳費等一大堆工作。也有批評者擔心，便利商店的方便助長了拋棄式的生活方式，例如一次性的杯子和包裝。儘管如此，對台灣數百萬人來說，便利商店不只是一家店，而是日常生活的一部分，隨時都在需要的時候開著門。"],[["convenience store","n.","便利商店"],["on the spot","phr.","當場"],["take out cash","phr.","提領現金"],["fiercely","adv.","激烈地"],["shift","n.","輪班"],["single-use","adj.","一次性的"]],342],["火星直升機「機智號」（2021–2024 年太空任務）",["On April 19, 2021, a tiny helicopter rose about three meters into the thin air of Mars, hovered for a few seconds, and landed again. The flight lasted just 39 seconds, but it made history. NASA's Ingenuity had become the first aircraft to make a powered, controlled flight on another planet.","Flying on Mars is extremely difficult. The planet's air is only about one percent as thick as Earth's, so there is very little for a helicopter's blades to push against. To lift off, Ingenuity had to be very light—about 1.8 kilograms—and its blades had to spin very fast, about 2,400 times per minute, several times faster than those of a helicopter on Earth. It had two sets of blades, each about 1.2 meters from tip to tip, stacked one above the other on a central pole and turning in opposite directions. On top of the pole sat a small solar panel that charged its batteries, and four thin legs allowed it to land on rocky ground.","Because radio signals take several minutes to travel between Earth and Mars, Ingenuity could not be flown with a joystick. Instead, engineers sent instructions in advance, and the helicopter flew on its own, using cameras and sensors to find its way.","Ingenuity was designed to make up to five test flights over about a month. Instead, it flew 72 times over nearly three years, traveling a total of about 17 kilometers and helping the Perseverance rover find safe routes and interesting places to study. Its mission ended in January 2024, when one of its blades was damaged during a landing.","To honor the past, the helicopter carried a small piece of cloth from the Wright brothers' airplane, which made the first powered flight on Earth in 1903. Ingenuity's success has opened the door to future flying explorers, including larger helicopters that could carry scientific instruments to places that rovers cannot reach."],[["What is the passage mainly about?",["How the Wright brothers built their airplane","Why Mars has very thin air","The first powered flight on another planet and what it achieved","How rovers collect rocks on Mars"],[],2,"主旨","首段寫機智號完成首次在其他行星上的動力飛行，第二、三段說明在火星飛行的困難與設計，第四段寫遠超預期的成果，末段談傳承與未來。主旨是 (C)。","(A) 萊特兄弟如何打造飛機　(B) 火星的空氣為何稀薄　(C) 首次在其他行星上的動力飛行及其成就　(D) 探測車如何在火星採集岩石"],["Why did Ingenuity's blades have to spin so fast?",["Mars's air is so thin that there is little for the blades to push against.","The helicopter was very heavy.","Strong winds on Mars blow the helicopter away.","The solar panel needed the blades to turn quickly."],[],0,"細節（原因）","第二段：火星空氣只有地球的約百分之一，<i>there is very little for a helicopter's blades to push against</i>，所以機身要很輕、旋翼要轉得很快，選 (A)。(B) 與「只有 1.8 公斤」相反。","(A) 火星空氣稀薄，旋翼幾乎推不到東西　(B) 直升機非常重　(C) 火星強風會把直升機吹走　(D) 太陽能板需要旋翼快速轉動"],["Which picture best shows Ingenuity?",[],["<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 132\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"126\" stroke=\"#C9B08F\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"88\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"68\" y2=\"122\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"112\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"132\" y2=\"122\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"88\" y2=\"124\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"112\" y2=\"124\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"72\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"42\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"3.6\" fill=\"#9C958E\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"86\" y=\"14\" width=\"28\" height=\"7\" fill=\"#4F5F78\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"84\" y=\"72\" width=\"32\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#D8CFC4\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 132\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"126\" stroke=\"#C9B08F\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"88\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"68\" y2=\"122\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"112\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"132\" y2=\"122\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"88\" y2=\"124\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"112\" y2=\"124\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"84\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"46\" y2=\"56\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"116\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"154\" y2=\"56\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"84\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"52\" y2=\"74\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"116\" y1=\"86\" x2=\"148\" y2=\"74\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"46\" cy=\"54\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"3.2\" fill=\"#9C958E\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><ellipse cx=\"154\" cy=\"54\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"3.2\" fill=\"#9C958E\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><ellipse cx=\"52\" cy=\"72\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"3.2\" fill=\"#9C958E\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><ellipse cx=\"148\" cy=\"72\" rx=\"20\" ry=\"3.2\" fill=\"#9C958E\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"84\" y=\"72\" width=\"32\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#D8CFC4\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><rect x=\"86\" y=\"64\" width=\"28\" height=\"7\" fill=\"#4F5F78\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 132\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"126\" stroke=\"#C9B08F\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"26\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"72\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"36\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"3.6\" fill=\"#9C958E\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"48\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"3.6\" fill=\"#9C958E\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"84\" y=\"72\" width=\"32\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#D8CFC4\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><rect x=\"86\" y=\"94\" width=\"28\" height=\"7\" fill=\"#4F5F78\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><circle cx=\"80\" cy=\"112\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#FCFAF7\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><circle cx=\"120\" cy=\"112\" r=\"12\" fill=\"#FCFAF7\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/></svg>","<svg viewBox=\"0 0 200 132\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" font-family=\"Avenir Next, Helvetica Neue, Arial, sans-serif\"><line x1=\"20\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"180\" y2=\"126\" stroke=\"#C9B08F\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><line x1=\"88\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"68\" y2=\"122\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"112\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"132\" y2=\"122\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.6\"/><line x1=\"95\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"88\" y2=\"124\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"105\" y1=\"92\" x2=\"112\" y2=\"124\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/><line x1=\"100\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"100\" y2=\"72\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"36\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"3.6\" fill=\"#9C958E\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><ellipse cx=\"100\" cy=\"50\" rx=\"62\" ry=\"3.6\" fill=\"#9C958E\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"86\" y=\"14\" width=\"28\" height=\"7\" fill=\"#4F5F78\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1\"/><rect x=\"84\" y=\"72\" width=\"32\" height=\"20\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#D8CFC4\" stroke=\"#3F3833\" stroke-width=\"1.4\"/></svg>"],3,"圖片題","第二段：兩組旋翼 <i>stacked one above the other on a central pole</i>，<i>On top of the pole sat a small solar panel</i>，還有 <i>four thin legs</i>。(D) 中央桿上有上下兩組旋翼、頂端有太陽能板、下方四支細腳，選 (D)。(A) 只有一組旋翼；(B) 四旋翼無人機；(C) 太陽能板在機身下方而且是輪子。","(A) 一組旋翼　(B) 四角旋翼無人機　(C) 太陽能板在下方、有輪子　(D) 兩組疊放旋翼、頂端太陽能板、四支腳"],["Which of the following shows that Ingenuity did better than expected?",["It carried a piece of cloth from the Wright brothers' airplane.","It flew 72 times instead of up to five.","Its blades were damaged in January 2024.","Its first flight lasted only 39 seconds."],[],1,"推論","第四段：原本設計 <i>to make up to five test flights over about a month</i>，結果飛了 72 次、將近三年，遠超預期，選 (B)。(A)(C)(D) 都是事實，但無法說明表現超出預期。","(A) 它帶著萊特兄弟飛機的一小塊布　(B) 它飛了 72 次，而非最多五次　(C) 它的旋翼在 2024 年 1 月受損　(D) 它的首航只持續 39 秒"]],["2021 年 4 月 19 日，一架小小的直升機在火星稀薄的空氣中升起約三公尺，盤旋了幾秒後再度降落。這次飛行只持續 39 秒，卻創造了歷史：美國太空總署的「機智號」成為第一架在其他行星上完成動力、受控飛行的飛行器。","在火星上飛行極為困難。火星的空氣密度只有地球的百分之一左右，直升機的旋翼幾乎推不到什麼東西。為了起飛，機智號必須非常輕──約 1.8 公斤──旋翼也必須轉得非常快，每分鐘約 2,400 轉，是地球上直升機的好幾倍。它有兩組旋翼，每組從一端到另一端約 1.2 公尺，上下疊放在一根中央桿上，朝相反方向旋轉。桿子頂端有一塊替電池充電的小太陽能板，四支細細的腳讓它能降落在多岩石的地面上。","由於無線電訊號在地球和火星之間往返要好幾分鐘，機智號無法用搖桿操控。工程師改為事先送出指令，由直升機自行飛行，利用攝影機和感應器辨識方向。","機智號原本的設計是在約一個月內進行最多五次試飛。結果它在將近三年間飛了 72 次，總距離約 17 公里，還幫助「毅力號」探測車找出安全路線和值得研究的地點。2024 年 1 月，它的一片旋翼在降落時受損，任務就此結束。","為了向歷史致敬，這架直升機帶著一小塊萊特兄弟飛機上的布料──那架飛機在 1903 年完成了地球上第一次動力飛行。機智號的成功為未來的飛行探險家開了一扇門，包括能把科學儀器帶到探測車到不了的地方的大型直升機。"],[["hover","v.","盤旋"],["aircraft","n.","飛行器"],["blade","n.","（旋翼的）葉片"],["stack","v.","堆疊"],["in advance","phr.","事先"],["rover","n.","探測車"]],320]]]],"B":[[1,"茶包的誕生",["Few inventions are as simple as the tea bag, and few were created so [[1]]. Around 1908, Thomas Sullivan, a tea merchant in New York, was looking for a cheaper way to send samples to his customers. Instead of packing the tea in [[2]] metal tins, he put small amounts into hand-sewn silk bags. He expected people to cut the bags open and [[3]] the leaves into a teapot as usual. To his surprise, many of them simply dropped the whole bag into hot water. They found this method quick and clean, and orders soon [[4]].","Sullivan's customers had only one [[5]]: the silk was too fine for the water to pass through easily. He therefore [[6]] silk with gauze, a loosely woven cotton cloth, and the first real tea bag was born. In the 1920s, machines began to produce tea bags in large numbers, and paper took the place of cloth. Bags with a string and a tag attached soon became a [[7]] sight in American kitchens.","The British, however, were slow to [[8]] the idea. Proud of their tea-making traditions, they looked down on tea bags as a lazy shortcut, and material shortages during World War II delayed things further. It was not until the 1950s that tea bags finally [[9]] in Britain. Today, they account for the vast [[10]] of the tea drunk there.","Tea lovers still argue about whether loose leaves taste better. Whatever the answer, the tea bag has made one of the world's oldest drinks far more convenient than ever before. Around the world, billions of them are used every year, in offices, on trains, and in kitchens where nobody has time to wash a teapot. Thomas Sullivan only wanted to save money on packaging, but he ended up changing the way the world drinks tea."],["majority","common","empty","replaced","accept","caught on","complaint","expensive","flooded in","accidentally"],[[9,"副詞","修飾 were created，茶包是「意外」發明的，後文 To his surprise 呼應。"],[7,"形容詞","他想找更便宜的方法，所以不用「昂貴的」金屬罐。"],[2,"動詞","和 cut 對稱用原形；empty A into B 是「把 A 倒進 B」。"],[8,"片語","顧客覺得方便，訂單「大量湧入」；flood in 不接受詞。"],[6,"名詞","only one 後面接單數名詞，冒號後面說明顧客「抱怨」的內容。"],[3,"動詞","replace A with B「用 B 取代 A」，過去式。"],[1,"形容詞","a common sight「常見的景象」。"],[4,"動詞","be slow to V；英國人很慢才「接受」這個點子。"],[5,"片語","catch on「流行起來」，主詞是 tea bags。"],[0,"名詞","the vast majority of「絕大多數的」。"]],["很少有發明像茶包這麼簡單，也很少有發明是這麼意外地誕生的。大約在 1908 年，紐約茶商 Thomas Sullivan 想找一個比較便宜的方法寄樣品給顧客。他不用昂貴的金屬罐裝茶葉，而是把少量茶葉放進手縫的絲質小袋。他以為大家會照常把袋子剪開，把茶葉倒進茶壺。出乎他意料，許多人直接把整個袋子丟進熱水裡。他們覺得這個方法又快又乾淨，訂單很快就大量湧入。","顧客只有一個抱怨：絲織得太密，水不容易通過。於是他用紗布（一種織得很鬆的棉布）取代絲，第一個真正的茶包就此誕生。1920 年代，機器開始大量生產茶包，紙也取代了布。附有棉線和標籤的茶包很快成為美國廚房裡常見的景象。","不過英國人很慢才接受這個點子。他們以自己的泡茶傳統為傲，瞧不起茶包，認為那是偷懶的捷徑，加上二戰期間物資短缺，又拖延了一段時間。直到 1950 年代，茶包才終於在英國流行起來。如今，那裡喝掉的茶絕大多數都是茶包。","愛茶的人仍在爭論散裝茶葉是不是比較好喝。不管答案是什麼，茶包已經讓世界上最古老的飲料之一變得前所未有地方便。全世界每年用掉數十億個茶包，在辦公室、火車上，還有沒人有空洗茶壺的廚房裡。Thomas Sullivan 原本只是想省包裝費，最後卻改變了全世界喝茶的方式。"],306],[2,"泡麵之父",["In postwar Japan, food was [[1]], and people would wait in long lines in the cold for a bowl of ramen. This sight left a deep [[2]] on Momofuku Ando, a businessman in Osaka, who decided to create noodles that could be prepared anywhere in minutes. Working alone in a small shed behind his house, he [[3]] for a whole year without success. The noodles he made either [[4]] or took too long to cook.","The breakthrough came when Ando watched his wife frying food in hot oil. He noticed that the oil drove the water out and left the food full of tiny holes. When noodles were fried in the same way, they dried [[5]] and could be kept for months; when boiling water was added, the holes let the water back in, and the noodles became soft again. In 1958, Ando's Chicken Ramen went on sale. It was at first [[6]] as a luxury, costing several times more than fresh noodles, but its convenience quickly won people over.","Ando's second great idea was [[7]] by a trip to the United States in 1966. There he saw supermarket managers break his noodles in half, put them in paper cups, and eat them with forks. Five years later, he [[8]] Cup Noodles, a product that served as its own package, cooking pot, and bowl.","Today, more than one hundred billion servings of instant noodles are [[9]] around the world every year. Ando, who ate his own noodles almost every day, lived to the age of 96. In his honor, a museum in Osaka lets visitors design their own cup of noodles. He believed that peace would come to the world when all people had [[10]] to eat."],["regarded","instantly","scarce","came up with","experimented","enough","impression","fell apart","inspired","consumed"],[[2,"形容詞","戰後日本食物「短缺」，所以大家排長隊等拉麵。"],[6,"名詞","leave a deep impression on「給…留下深刻印象」。"],[4,"動詞","for a whole year without success 說明他「實驗」了一整年；不接受詞。"],[7,"片語","either ... or ... 列出兩種失敗：麵「散掉」或煮太久。"],[1,"副詞","修飾 dried，油炸讓麵「立刻」變乾，也呼應 instant noodles。"],[0,"過去分詞","be regarded as「被視為」。"],[8,"過去分詞","be inspired by「受到…啟發」。"],[3,"片語","come up with「想出、推出」，受詞是 Cup Noodles。"],[9,"過去分詞","servings are consumed「被吃掉」。"],[5,"代名詞","have enough to eat「有足夠的東西吃」。"]],["戰後的日本食物短缺，人們會在寒冷中排長隊等一碗拉麵。這個景象讓大阪商人安藤百福留下深刻印象，他決定做出一種在任何地方、幾分鐘內就能煮好的麵。他獨自在屋後的小棚子裡實驗了一整年，都沒有成功。他做出的麵不是散掉，就是要煮太久。","突破出現在安藤看著妻子用熱油炸東西的時候。他注意到油把水分逼出來，讓食物布滿小孔。把麵用同樣的方法油炸，麵會立刻變乾，可以保存好幾個月；加入滾水時，小孔讓水重新進入，麵就恢復柔軟。1958 年，安藤的雞汁拉麵上市。起初它被視為奢侈品，價格是新鮮麵條的好幾倍，但它的方便很快贏得了人心。","安藤的第二個好點子，靈感來自 1966 年的一趟美國行。他在那裡看到超市的經理把他的麵折成兩半、放進紙杯、用叉子吃。五年後，他推出杯麵，這個產品本身就是包裝、鍋子和碗。","如今，全世界每年吃掉超過一千億份泡麵。幾乎每天都吃自家泡麵的安藤活到 96 歲。為了紀念他，大阪有一座博物館讓遊客設計自己的杯麵。他相信，當所有人都有足夠的東西吃，世界就會和平。"],290],[3,"氣泡紙原本是壁紙",["In 1957, two engineers, Alfred Fielding and Marc Chavannes, sealed two plastic shower curtains together in a garage in New Jersey, [[1]] air bubbles between them. What they had in mind was a new kind of wallpaper with a three-dimensional [[2]]. Unfortunately, nobody wanted plastic bubbles on their walls, and the product was a complete [[3]]. The pair then tried to sell it as a material for keeping greenhouses warm, but that idea did not [[4]] either.","The invention might have been forgotten if a clever salesman at their company had not heard that IBM was about to ship its new computers. These machines were [[5]] and costly, and at the time such goods were usually packed in crumpled newspaper, which was heavy, messy, and not very [[6]]. The salesman showed IBM how sheets of air bubbles could [[7]] the computers during transport. IBM was convinced, and Bubble Wrap finally found its true [[8]]. Orders from other manufacturers followed, and the two engineers' small company grew into a business worth billions of dollars.","More than sixty years later, the material is used to protect everything from eggs to electronics. Yet many people are [[9]] of it for quite another reason: the simple joy of popping the bubbles one by one. Some psychologists suggest that the activity helps [[10]] stress by keeping nervous fingers busy. There is even a Bubble Wrap Appreciation Day, celebrated on the last Monday of January. Recently, the company introduced a new version that is shipped flat and filled with air by the customer. It saves space on trucks, but to the disappointment of many fans, its bubbles cannot be popped. Not bad for a wallpaper that never made it onto a wall."],["cushion","purpose","failure","effective","take off","delicate","fond","relieve","trapping","texture"],[[8,"現在分詞","分詞構句，表示把兩片浴簾封在一起時「困住」了氣泡。"],[9,"名詞","a three-dimensional texture「立體的質感」，形容壁紙。"],[2,"名詞","沒有人想要，所以是 a complete failure「徹底的失敗」。"],[4,"片語","did not take off「沒有成功、沒有起飛」，either 表示又一次失敗。"],[5,"形容詞","電腦「精密易損」又昂貴，所以需要保護。"],[3,"形容詞","報紙又重又亂，而且保護效果不太「有效」。"],[0,"動詞","could 後面接原形；cushion 當動詞是「緩衝、保護」。"],[1,"名詞","find its true purpose「找到真正的用途」。"],[6,"形容詞","be fond of「喜歡」。"],[7,"動詞","help (to) V；relieve stress「紓解壓力」。"]],["1957 年，兩位工程師 Alfred Fielding 和 Marc Chavannes 在紐澤西的一間車庫裡，把兩片塑膠浴簾封在一起，中間困住了氣泡。他們想做的是一種有立體質感的新壁紙。可惜沒有人想在牆上貼塑膠泡泡，這個產品徹底失敗。兩人接著想把它當成溫室的保溫材料來賣，但這個點子也沒有成功。","要不是公司裡一位聰明的業務員聽說 IBM 即將運送新電腦，這項發明可能就被遺忘了。這些機器精密又昂貴，當時這類貨品通常用揉皺的報紙包裝，又重又亂，效果也不太好。這位業務員向 IBM 展示，一片片的氣泡可以在運送途中保護電腦。IBM 被說服了，氣泡紙終於找到真正的用途。其他製造商的訂單接踵而來，兩位工程師的小公司成長為價值數十億美元的企業。","六十多年後，這種材料被用來保護各種東西，從雞蛋到電子產品都有。不過許多人喜歡它是因為另一個原因：一顆一顆捏破泡泡的單純快樂。有些心理學家認為，這個動作讓緊張的手指有事做，有助於紓解壓力。甚至還有「氣泡紙感謝日」，訂在一月的最後一個星期一。近年這家公司推出一種新版本，出貨時是扁平的，由客戶自己充氣。它節省了卡車上的空間，但讓許多粉絲失望的是，它的泡泡捏不破。對一種從來沒貼上牆的壁紙來說，這成績不壞。"],287],[4,"紅綠燈的由來",["The world's first traffic signal was [[1]] outside the Houses of Parliament in London in 1868. Designed by a railway engineer, it used arms that moved up and down during the day and red and green gas lamps at night, and a police officer had to stand beside it to [[2]] it by hand. Less than a month later, a gas leak caused the signal to explode, badly [[3]] the officer. The experiment was [[4]], and London did without traffic signals for decades.","The idea was [[5]] in the United States in the early twentieth century, when cars began to crowd the streets and accidents became [[6]]. In 1914, the city of Cleveland set up the first electric traffic lights, which showed only red and green. Drivers had no [[7]] before the light changed, so cars often ran into each other in the middle of intersections. A Detroit police officer named William Potts solved the problem in 1920 by adding a third, yellow light, [[8]] drivers time to slow down.","Why red, yellow, and green? Railways had long used red for danger. Green [[9]] meant “caution,” and white meant “go,” but white signals were easily [[10]] for ordinary lamps or even stars, with disastrous results. Green was therefore moved to “go,” and yellow, the color most different from the other two, took over the job of caution.","Today's traffic lights are far smarter than their ancestors. Many are linked to sensors and cameras that adjust the timing of the signals to the flow of traffic. Some can even sense an ambulance or a fire engine coming and turn green to let it pass. The basic message, however, has not changed in a hundred years: red means stop."],["frequent","giving","revived","installed","injuring","originally","mistaken","abandoned","operate","warning"],[[3,"過去分詞","號誌「被裝設」在國會大廈外。"],[8,"動詞","to 後面接原形；operate it by hand「用手操作」。"],[4,"現在分詞","分詞構句，爆炸「使」警察受重傷。"],[7,"過去分詞","出了意外，這項試驗「被放棄」，後面說倫敦幾十年沒有號誌。"],[2,"過去分詞","幾十年後這個點子在美國「重新被採用」。"],[0,"形容詞","become 後面接形容詞；車多了，事故變得「頻繁」。"],[9,"名詞","have no warning「沒有預警」，所以需要黃燈。"],[1,"現在分詞","分詞構句，加上黃燈「給」駕駛時間減速。"],[5,"副詞","綠色「原本」代表小心，後來才改成通行。"],[6,"過去分詞","be mistaken for「被誤認成」。"]],["世界上第一座交通號誌於 1868 年裝設在倫敦國會大廈外。它由一位鐵路工程師設計，白天用上下擺動的臂桿，晚上用紅色和綠色的煤氣燈，還需要一名警察站在旁邊用手操作。不到一個月，煤氣外洩導致號誌爆炸，那名警察受了重傷。這項試驗被放棄，倫敦此後幾十年都沒有交通號誌。","二十世紀初，汽車開始擠滿街道，事故變得頻繁，這個點子在美國重新被採用。1914 年，克里夫蘭市設置了第一批電動紅綠燈，只有紅燈和綠燈。燈號改變之前駕駛沒有任何預警，所以車子常在路口中央相撞。1920 年，底特律一位名叫 William Potts 的警察加上第三個燈——黃燈，解決了這個問題，讓駕駛有時間減速。","為什麼是紅、黃、綠？鐵路長久以來用紅色代表危險。綠色原本代表「小心」，白色代表「通行」，但白色號誌很容易被誤認成普通的燈甚至星星，造成慘重的後果。於是綠色改為「通行」，而和另外兩色差別最大的黃色接下了「小心」的任務。","今天的紅綠燈比它們的祖先聰明得多。許多號誌連結感測器和攝影機，能依車流調整燈號的時間。有些甚至能感應到救護車或消防車接近，轉成綠燈讓它通過。不過基本的訊息一百年來沒有變：紅燈就是停。"],287],[5,"蜂蜜為什麼不會壞",["When archaeologists opened ancient Egyptian tombs, they found pots of honey thousands of years old, still perfectly [[1]]. Honey is one of the very few foods that can be stored [[2]]. What gives it this remarkable [[3]]?","The first secret is its low water content. Honey is [[4]] about 80 percent sugar and less than 20 percent water. Bacteria that land in it have the water sucked out of their own cells and die before they can [[5]]. In addition, honey is quite acidic, which makes it an even more [[6]] environment for germs.","The bees themselves deserve much of the credit. The nectar they collect from flowers is thin and watery; to dry it, they fan it with their wings for hours inside the hive. They also add a special substance produced in their own bodies. This substance [[7]] the sugar and, in the process, produces a small amount of hydrogen peroxide, a chemical often used to clean wounds. It is no [[8]] that people in many ancient cultures put honey on cuts and burns to prevent infection.","There is one condition, though. Honey must be kept in a sealed container. Left open in a damp place, it will [[9]] water from the air and eventually go bad. And if your honey turns cloudy and hard, don't throw it away. This change is completely [[10]]; simply place the jar in warm water, and the honey will become clear and smooth again. The same is true of honey that has been sitting in the cupboard for years. As long as the lid was closed tightly, there is no need to check the date on the label. The bees finished the job of preserving it long before it reached your kitchen."],["indefinitely","edible","made up of","natural","quality","hostile","multiply","absorb","breaks down","coincidence"],[[1,"形容詞","幾千年前的蜂蜜仍然完全「可以吃」。"],[0,"副詞","can be stored indefinitely「可以無限期保存」。"],[4,"名詞","this remarkable quality「這種驚人的特性」，指不會壞。"],[2,"片語","be made up of「由…組成」。"],[6,"動詞","細菌還來不及「繁殖」就死了；不接受詞。"],[5,"形容詞","酸性讓蜂蜜對細菌來說是更「不利的」環境。"],[8,"片語","break down「分解」，主詞 This substance 是單數，動詞加 s。"],[9,"名詞","It is no coincidence that ...「…並不是巧合」。"],[7,"動詞","will 後面接原形；absorb water「吸收水分」。"],[3,"形容詞","結晶變硬是完全「自然的」變化，不必丟掉。"]],["考古學家打開古埃及陵墓時，發現了幾千年前的一罐罐蜂蜜，仍然完全可以吃。蜂蜜是極少數可以無限期保存的食物之一。是什麼讓它有這種驚人的特性？","第一個祕密是含水量低。蜂蜜大約由百分之八十的糖和不到百分之二十的水組成。落在蜂蜜裡的細菌，自身細胞的水分會被吸走，還來不及繁殖就死了。此外，蜂蜜相當酸，對細菌來說是更不利的環境。","蜜蜂本身功不可沒。牠們從花朵採集的花蜜又稀又水；為了讓它變乾，牠們在蜂巢裡用翅膀搧好幾個小時。牠們還會加入自己體內分泌的一種特殊物質。這種物質會分解糖，過程中產生少量的過氧化氫，這是一種常用來清潔傷口的化學物質。許多古文明的人把蜂蜜塗在割傷和燒傷上預防感染，並不是巧合。","不過有一個條件：蜂蜜必須裝在密封的容器裡。如果在潮溼的地方敞開放著，它會吸收空氣中的水分，最後壞掉。如果你的蜂蜜變得混濁變硬，別丟掉。這種變化完全是自然的；只要把罐子放進溫水，蜂蜜就會恢復清澈滑順。在櫥櫃裡放了好幾年的蜂蜜也是一樣。只要蓋子蓋緊，就不必看標籤上的日期。早在蜂蜜到你家廚房之前，蜜蜂就已經完成保存的工作了。"],293],[6,"鉛筆裡沒有鉛",["Although we speak of “lead pencils,” pencils have never [[1]] any lead. The story began in the 1560s, when a large deposit of a soft, dark mineral was discovered in Borrowdale, England. Local shepherds [[2]] it to mark their sheep. Because the mineral looked like lead, people [[3]] called it “black lead.” It was actually graphite, a form of carbon, but the wrong name has [[4]] ever since.","Pure graphite left dark, clear lines, but it was so soft that it broke easily and stained the fingers. People first wrapped the sticks in string, and later slid them into hollow pieces of wood. The wooden pencil was born.","For two centuries, England controlled the pencil trade, since Borrowdale graphite was the only [[5]] pure enough to be used directly. When war cut France off from English supplies in the 1790s, a French army officer, Nicolas-Jacques Conté, was asked to find a [[6]] for it. He ground low-quality graphite into powder, mixed it with clay, and baked the mixture in an oven. By [[7]] the amount of clay, he could make a pencil harder or softer. This is the origin of grades such as HB and 2B printed on pencils today.","Another [[8]] came in 1858, when an American named Hymen Lipman attached a small eraser to the end of a pencil. In an age of screens, the humble pencil [[9]] popular, and billions are still made every year. It needs no battery, works at any angle, and forgives mistakes. Few tools are as cheap and [[10]]. Perhaps that is why, more than four centuries after those shepherds in Borrowdale, we still reach for one whenever we need to work out an idea on paper."],["source","reliable","adjusting","mistakenly","made use of","remains","improvement","stuck","contained","substitute"],[[8,"過去分詞","have never contained「從來不含有」鉛。"],[4,"片語","make use of「利用」，牧羊人利用它替羊做記號。"],[3,"副詞","修飾 called，後面說它其實是石墨，所以是「誤」稱。"],[7,"過去分詞","the name has stuck「這個名字就此沿用下來」，stick 的過去分詞。"],[0,"名詞","the only source「唯一的來源」。"],[9,"名詞","find a substitute for「找到…的替代品」。"],[2,"現在分詞","By + V-ing；adjust the amount「調整份量」。"],[6,"名詞","Another 後面接單數名詞；加上橡皮擦是另一項「改良」。"],[5,"動詞","remain popular「仍然受歡迎」，主詞 the pencil 是單數，動詞加 s。"],[1,"形容詞","和 cheap 並列，形容鉛筆「可靠」。"]],["雖然我們說「鉛筆」，鉛筆裡卻從來沒有鉛。故事開始於 1560 年代，當時在英格蘭的 Borrowdale 發現了大量柔軟的深色礦物。當地牧羊人利用它替羊做記號。因為這種礦物看起來像鉛，人們誤稱它為「黑鉛」。它其實是石墨，是碳的一種形態，但這個錯誤的名字就此沿用下來。","純石墨能畫出又黑又清楚的線條，但它太軟，容易斷，也會弄髒手指。人們起先用繩子把石墨條纏起來，後來改成塞進中空的木條。木頭鉛筆就這樣誕生了。","兩個世紀以來，英格蘭掌控了鉛筆貿易，因為 Borrowdale 的石墨是唯一純到可以直接使用的來源。1790 年代，戰爭切斷了法國從英格蘭取得的供應，法國軍官 Nicolas-Jacques Conté 受命尋找替代品。他把低品質的石墨磨成粉，和黏土混合，再放進爐子烘烤。藉由調整黏土的份量，他可以讓鉛筆變硬或變軟。今天印在鉛筆上的 HB、2B 等等級就是這樣來的。","另一項改良出現在 1858 年，美國人 Hymen Lipman 把一小塊橡皮擦裝在鉛筆尾端。在這個螢幕的時代，不起眼的鉛筆仍然受歡迎，每年還是生產數十億枝。它不需要電池、任何角度都能寫，還能容許犯錯。很少有工具這麼便宜又可靠。也許正因如此，在 Borrowdale 那些牧羊人之後四百多年，我們需要在紙上把想法理清楚時，還是會伸手拿一枝鉛筆。"],289],[7,"珊瑚白化",["Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they [[1]] about a quarter of all the species living in the sea. Corals themselves are animals. They live in [[2]] with tiny plant-like organisms called algae, which stay inside the corals' bodies. The algae use sunlight to make food and share it with the coral; in return, the coral gives them shelter. The algae are also what give corals their [[3]] colors.","This relationship, however, is highly [[4]] to heat. When the water stays just one or two degrees warmer than usual for several weeks, the stressed corals [[5]] the algae. Without them, the coral turns ghostly white, a condition known as bleaching. A bleached coral is not dead, but it is starving. If the water cools in time, the algae may return and the coral can [[6]]. If not, the coral dies, and the reef slowly [[7]].","Large-scale bleaching was almost unheard of before the 1980s. Since then, as the oceans have warmed, it has struck with increasing [[8]]. Australia's Great Barrier Reef alone has suffered several such events in the past ten years. Reefs elsewhere, from the Caribbean to the Indian Ocean, have fared no better. When a reef dies, the damage spreads to the people who depend on it for fishing, tourism, and protection from storm waves.","Scientists are now racing to help. In laboratories, some are growing corals that can stand higher temperatures and [[9]] them onto damaged reefs. Such efforts are [[10]], but they are far too small to save reefs around the world. In the long run, the only real solution is to slow down the warming of the planet."],["sensitive","promising","transplanting","frequency","partnership","crumbles","recover","drive out","brilliant","support"],[[9,"動詞","主詞 they（珊瑚礁）；support 在這裡是「供養、維持…的生存」。"],[4,"名詞","live in partnership with「和…共生、合作」。"],[8,"形容詞","brilliant colors「鮮豔的顏色」。"],[0,"形容詞","be sensitive to「對…敏感」。"],[7,"片語","drive out「趕走」，受詞是 the algae；主詞複數用原形。"],[6,"動詞","can 後面接原形；水溫及時下降，珊瑚可以「復原」。"],[5,"動詞","珊瑚死後，礁體慢慢「崩解」；主詞 the reef 是單數，動詞加 s。"],[3,"名詞","with increasing frequency「越來越頻繁地」。"],[2,"現在分詞","和 are growing 對稱；transplant「移植」到受損的礁上。"],[1,"形容詞","這些努力「有希望」，但規模太小。"]],["珊瑚礁覆蓋不到百分之一的海床，卻供養了海洋中大約四分之一的物種。珊瑚本身是動物，牠們和一種像植物的微小生物——藻類——共生，藻類就住在珊瑚體內。藻類利用陽光製造養分並分給珊瑚；珊瑚則提供牠們棲身之處。珊瑚鮮豔的顏色也是藻類帶來的。","不過，這種關係對高溫非常敏感。當水溫連續幾週只比平常高一兩度，受到壓力的珊瑚就會把藻類趕出去。沒有了藻類，珊瑚會變成慘白色，這種情況稱為白化。白化的珊瑚還沒死，但正在挨餓。如果水溫及時下降，藻類可能回來，珊瑚就能復原。否則珊瑚會死亡，礁體慢慢崩解。","1980 年代以前，幾乎沒聽過大規模白化。此後隨著海洋變暖，白化發生得越來越頻繁。光是澳洲的大堡礁，過去十年就遭遇了好幾次。其他地方的珊瑚礁，從加勒比海到印度洋，情況也沒有比較好。珊瑚礁一旦死亡，損害會擴及靠它捕魚、發展觀光和抵擋暴風巨浪的人們。","科學家正在和時間賽跑。有些人在實驗室培育能耐受較高溫度的珊瑚，再移植到受損的礁上。這些努力有希望，但規模太小，救不了全世界的珊瑚礁。長遠來看，唯一真正的解方是減緩地球暖化。"],281],[8,"上課塗鴉反而記得多",["Teachers have long [[1]] students for doodling in class, assuming that a wandering pen means a wandering mind. A well-known experiment, however, suggests the [[2]].","In 2009, psychologist Jackie Andrade asked forty volunteers to listen to a dull phone message about a party. Half of them were told to shade in small shapes on a piece of paper while listening; the others just listened. Afterwards, everyone was given a surprise memory test. The doodlers [[3]] 29 percent more names and places than the non-doodlers. The result surprised many people, since the doodlers had seemed to be paying less attention, not more.","Why should this be so? Andrade's [[4]] is that when a task is boring, the mind tends to [[5]] into daydreams, which use up a great deal of mental energy. Doodling is just [[6]] enough to keep the brain from daydreaming, yet not so much that it [[7]] listening. In other words, it keeps the mind in a state of [[8]] alertness.","There is a catch, though. The benefit seems to be limited to listening to boring material. When a task involves looking at pictures or slides, doodling competes for the same visual attention, and performance actually [[9]]. Besides, a detailed portrait of your teacher is no longer a doodle; it is a drawing that requires your full attention.","So the next time you sit through a long, dull speech, don't feel guilty about the little boxes and circles in the corner of your notebook. They may be a sign of [[10]], not laziness. Some companies have even begun to encourage employees to draw during meetings. As for students, it may be wise to explain the science to your teacher before you try it in class."],["opposite","explanation","recalled","drift off","interferes with","concentration","scolded","demanding","mild","declines"],[[6,"過去分詞","have long scolded A for B「長久以來因 B 責罵 A」。"],[0,"名詞","suggest the opposite「顯示情況恰好相反」。"],[2,"動詞","塗鴉的人多「回想起」29% 的人名和地名，過去式。"],[1,"名詞","所有格 Andrade's 後面接名詞；她的「解釋」。"],[3,"片語","drift off into daydreams「飄去做白日夢」。"],[7,"形容詞","just demanding enough「費力的程度剛好」足以防止分心。"],[4,"片語","interfere with「干擾」，主詞 it 是單數，動詞加 s。"],[8,"形容詞","a state of mild alertness「輕微的警覺狀態」。"],[9,"動詞","表現反而「下降」；不接受詞，主詞 performance 是單數。"],[5,"名詞","a sign of concentration「專心的徵兆」，和 laziness 對比。"]],["老師長久以來都會責罵上課塗鴉的學生，認為筆在亂畫就代表心不在焉。不過一項著名的實驗顯示，情況恰好相反。","2009 年，心理學家 Jackie Andrade 請四十名志願者聽一段關於派對的無聊電話留言。其中一半被要求邊聽邊在紙上把小圖形塗滿；另一半只是聽。之後，所有人接受了一場突如其來的記憶測驗。塗鴉的人比沒塗鴉的人多回想起百分之二十九的人名和地名。這個結果讓許多人驚訝，因為塗鴉的人看起來明明比較不專心。","為什麼會這樣？Andrade 的解釋是，當任務很無聊時，心思容易飄去做白日夢，而白日夢會耗掉大量的腦力。塗鴉費力的程度剛好足以讓大腦不做白日夢，卻又不至於干擾聆聽。換句話說，它讓頭腦維持在輕微的警覺狀態。","不過有個但書。這種好處似乎只限於聽無聊的內容。如果任務需要看圖片或投影片，塗鴉會搶走同樣的視覺注意力，表現反而下降。此外，仔細畫一幅老師的肖像已經不是塗鴉，而是需要全神貫注的繪畫。","所以下次你坐著聽冗長無聊的演講時，別為筆記本角落的小方塊和圓圈感到內疚。它們可能是專心的徵兆，而不是懶惰。有些公司甚至開始鼓勵員工開會時畫畫。至於學生，在課堂上嘗試之前，最好先向老師解釋這背後的科學。"],289],[9,"燈塔守護人",["For centuries, lighthouses guided ships past dangerous rocks, and every one of them depended on a keeper. The keeper's life was far from [[1]]. Several times a night, he had to climb hundreds of steps to refill the oil and wind the machine that [[2]] the lamp. Every day the windows had to be cleaned of salt, and every detail of the weather was [[3]] in a logbook. In thick fog, he might have to ring a bell by hand for hours. If the light went out even for a few minutes, a ship could be lost.","The job also demanded an unusual [[4]] for loneliness. Keepers on remote rocks might see no one but their fellow keepers for weeks, and storms could [[5]] their supplies of food and fuel. Yet many took great pride in their work, knowing that sailors' lives were [[6]]. Some even became heroes. In 1838, Grace Darling, the daughter of an English keeper, rowed out with her father through a storm to [[7]] nine survivors of a shipwreck.","Technology gradually made keepers [[8]]. Electric lamps, automatic timers, and later solar panels took over their duties one by one. Britain's last lighthouse keepers left their posts in 1998. In the United States, only one lighthouse, in Boston Harbor, kept its keeper into the twenty-first century, and only because a law demanded it.","Today, many old lighthouses have been [[9]] into museums and guesthouses. Visitors who spend a night in one can enjoy the sea view without having to climb the stairs at midnight. At the same time, they gain some [[10]] into the lives of those who once kept the lights burning. It was a hard life, but an honorable one."],["recorded","romantic","rescue","converted","at stake","unnecessary","rotated","insight","cut off","tolerance"],[[1,"形容詞","far from romantic「一點也不浪漫」，後面列出辛苦的工作。"],[6,"動詞","the machine that rotated the lamp「轉動燈的機器」，過去式。"],[0,"過去分詞","was recorded in a logbook「被記錄在日誌裡」。"],[9,"名詞","tolerance for loneliness「對孤獨的忍受力」。"],[8,"片語","cut off supplies「切斷補給」，could 後面接原形。"],[4,"片語","at stake「處於危險中、攸關存亡」。"],[2,"動詞","to 後面接原形；rescue survivors「救出生還者」。"],[5,"形容詞","make + 受詞 + 形容詞；科技讓守護人「不再必要」。"],[3,"過去分詞","be converted into「被改建成」。"],[7,"名詞","gain insight into「對…有所了解」。"]],["幾個世紀以來，燈塔引導船隻避開危險的礁石，而每一座燈塔都靠一位守護人。守護人的生活一點也不浪漫。他每晚要爬好幾次幾百級的階梯去添油，並替轉動燈的機器上發條。窗戶每天都得清掉鹽分，天氣的每個細節都要記錄在日誌裡。遇到濃霧，他可能得用手敲鐘好幾個小時。燈只要熄滅幾分鐘，就可能有船遇難。","這份工作還需要超乎常人的孤獨忍受力。偏遠礁岩上的守護人可能好幾週只見得到同事，暴風雨還可能切斷食物和燃料的補給。然而許多人非常以這份工作為榮，因為他們知道水手的性命攸關於此。有些人甚至成了英雄。1838 年，一位英格蘭守護人的女兒 Grace Darling 和父親在暴風雨中划船出海，救出一場船難的九名生還者。","科技逐漸讓守護人不再必要。電燈、自動計時器，以及後來的太陽能板，一項一項接手了他們的工作。英國最後一批燈塔守護人在 1998 年離開崗位。在美國，只有波士頓港的一座燈塔到二十一世紀還有守護人，而且只是因為法律這樣規定。","如今，許多老燈塔已被改建成博物館和民宿。在裡面過夜的遊客可以欣賞海景，不必半夜爬樓梯，同時也能對那些曾經讓燈火不滅的人的生活有所了解。那是辛苦的生活，卻也是光榮的生活。"],287],[10,"孩子們創造的語言：尼加拉瓜手語",["Until the late 1970s, deaf people in Nicaragua lived largely in [[1]]. There were no schools for them, and most stayed at home, communicating with their families through a handful of simple [[2]] of their own making. Things changed when the government opened the country's first schools for the deaf in the capital. For the first time, hundreds of deaf children were brought together. Most of them had never met another deaf person before.","The teachers tried to teach the children to read lips and speak Spanish, but with little [[3]]. On the playground and the school bus, however, something remarkable was happening. The children began [[4]] the signs each of them had brought from home, and within a few years a shared system had [[5]].","The real leap came with the younger children who entered the schools later. They did not simply copy the older pupils' rough signing. They made it faster, more regular, and far more [[6]], adding rules of grammar that the older children had never used. In 1986, the puzzled education officials [[7]] an American language expert, Judy Kegl. She soon realized that she was [[8]] the birth of a new language, something no scientist had ever observed directly. Languages usually take centuries to develop, and their beginnings are lost in time. Here was one that had appeared within a single generation, with its creators still alive to be studied.","Nicaraguan Sign Language is now used by thousands of people. For researchers, it offers rare [[9]] that the human brain, especially the young brain, is built to create language. Given company and a need to communicate, children will [[10]] a language where none exists."],["success","emerged","isolation","witnessing","complex","called in","evidence","gestures","combining","invent"],[[2,"名詞","live in isolation「過著與外界隔絕的生活」。"],[7,"名詞","a handful of simple gestures「幾個簡單的手勢」，要用複數。"],[0,"名詞","with little success「沒什麼成效」。"],[8,"現在分詞","began + V-ing；孩子們開始「結合」各自帶來的手勢。"],[1,"過去分詞","a shared system had emerged「一套共通的系統出現了」。"],[4,"形容詞","和 faster、more regular 並列；變得更「複雜」，後面說加入了文法。"],[5,"片語","call in「請來（專家）」，過去式。"],[3,"現在分詞","was witnessing「正在目睹」一種新語言的誕生。"],[6,"名詞","offer evidence that ...「提供…的證據」。"],[9,"動詞","will 後面接原形；孩子會「發明」一種語言。"]],["直到 1970 年代末期，尼加拉瓜的聾人大多過著與外界隔絕的生活。當時沒有他們能讀的學校，多數人待在家裡，靠自己發明的幾個簡單手勢和家人溝通。政府在首都開設全國第一批聾人學校之後，情況改變了。數百名聾童第一次聚在一起。他們大多數以前從來沒遇過別的聾人。","老師想教孩子讀唇語和說西班牙語，但沒什麼成效。然而在操場和校車上，一件驚人的事正在發生。孩子們開始把各自從家裡帶來的手勢結合起來，幾年之內，一套共通的系統出現了。","真正的躍進來自後來入學的年幼孩子。他們不只是模仿年長學生粗糙的手勢，還讓它變得更快、更有規則，也複雜得多，加入了年長孩子從未用過的文法規則。1986 年，困惑的教育官員請來美國語言專家 Judy Kegl。她很快意識到，自己正在目睹一種新語言的誕生，這是從來沒有科學家直接觀察到的事。語言通常要好幾個世紀才能發展出來，起源早已不可考。眼前這一種卻在一個世代之內出現，創造它的人都還在世，可以研究。","如今有數千人使用尼加拉瓜手語。對研究者來說，它提供了罕見的證據，證明人腦——尤其是年幼的腦——天生就會創造語言。只要有同伴、有溝通的需要，孩子就會在沒有語言的地方發明出一種語言。"],280],[11,"口香糖的故事",["People have chewed gum-like substances for thousands of years. The ancient Maya, for example, chewed chicle, the dried juice of a tropical tree, which they valued for its ability to clean the teeth and [[1]] hunger.","Modern chewing gum, however, owes its existence to a failed business plan. In the 1860s, a former Mexican president, Santa Anna, brought a supply of chicle to New York, hoping it could be turned into a cheap [[2]] to rubber. He [[3]] a local inventor, Thomas Adams, who experimented with the material for more than a year. But however Adams treated it, the chicle stayed too soft, and the rubber plan went [[4]].","Adams was about to throw the whole stock away when it [[5]] him that Santa Anna had enjoyed chewing the stuff. He boiled a small amount, rolled it into little balls, and placed them in a drugstore. They sold out within hours. Adams soon opened a factory, and by adding sugar and various [[6]], he created an entirely new industry.","The popularity of gum [[7]] in the twentieth century, helped by clever advertising and by American soldiers, who [[8]] sticks of gum to children wherever they were sent during World War II. Demand grew so great that natural chicle could no longer [[9]] it, and gum makers switched to man-made materials.","That switch has a cost. Modern gum does not break down; dropped on the street, it stays there for years. Some companies are therefore returning to chicle, advertising gum that is [[10]] to the environment. Whether chewers will accept the higher price remains to be seen. One thing is certain: after thousands of years, people show no sign of giving up the habit."],["handed out","harmless","teamed up with","meet","soared","alternative","nowhere","ease","occurred to","flavors"],[[7,"動詞","和 clean 對稱用原形；ease hunger「緩解飢餓」。"],[5,"名詞","a cheap alternative to rubber「橡膠的便宜替代品」。"],[2,"片語","team up with「和…合作」，過去式。"],[6,"副詞","go nowhere「毫無進展」。"],[8,"片語","it occurred to him that ...「他突然想到…」。"],[9,"名詞","various 後面接複數名詞；加糖和各種「口味」。"],[4,"動詞","popularity soared「人氣飆升」，不接受詞。"],[0,"片語","hand out「分發」，受詞是 sticks of gum。"],[3,"動詞","meet (the demand)「滿足需求」，could 後面接原形。"],[1,"形容詞","be harmless to「對…無害」。"]],["人類嚼類似口香糖的東西已經有幾千年了。例如古代馬雅人會嚼 chicle，那是一種熱帶樹木乾掉的汁液，他們看重它能清潔牙齒、緩解飢餓。","不過，現代口香糖的誕生要歸功於一個失敗的商業計畫。1860 年代，墨西哥前總統 Santa Anna 帶了一批 chicle 到紐約，希望能把它變成橡膠的便宜替代品。他和當地發明家 Thomas Adams 合作，Adams 用這種材料實驗了一年多。但不管怎麼處理，chicle 都太軟，橡膠計畫毫無進展。","Adams 正要把整批貨丟掉時，突然想到 Santa Anna 很喜歡嚼這東西。他煮了一小批，搓成小球，放在一家藥局賣，幾個小時就賣光了。Adams 很快開了工廠，並加入糖和各種口味，創造出一個全新的產業。","口香糖的人氣在二十世紀飆升，靠的是聰明的廣告，還有美國士兵——二戰期間他們不管被派到哪裡，都會分口香糖給孩子。需求大到天然 chicle 無法滿足，口香糖製造商於是改用人造材料。","這個改變是有代價的。現代口香糖不會分解；掉在街上，就會留在那裡好幾年。因此有些公司正回頭使用 chicle，宣傳他們的口香糖對環境無害。嚼口香糖的人會不會接受比較高的價格，還有待觀察。有一件事可以確定：幾千年過去了，人們完全沒有要放棄這個習慣的跡象。"],288],[12,"手扶梯該不該空一邊",["In many cities, there is an unwritten rule on escalators: stand on one side and leave the other side [[1]] for people in a hurry. The custom seems polite and efficient. Engineers, however, have their doubts.","In 2015, London's transport authority ran an unusual three-week [[2]] at Holborn, one of the busiest stations on the Underground. Staff asked passengers to stand on both sides of the “up” escalators instead of walking. Many commuters were annoyed, and some simply [[3]] the instructions. But the results were [[4]]: at the busiest hours, an escalator on which everyone stood carried about 30 percent more people than one with a walking lane. The lines at the bottom, which normally stretched across the hall, became much shorter.","The reason is simple. On a long escalator, few people are [[5]] to climb, so the walking side stays half empty while a long line [[6]] at the bottom on the standing side. Walkers also need more space between them. When everybody stands, both sides are filled, and the crowd below [[7]] more quickly.","There are other arguments, too. Escalator makers warn that when all the weight rests on one side, the machines wear out [[8]] and break down more often. In Japan, where falls on escalators cause hundreds of [[9]] every year, several cities now ask riders to stand still. Posters in stations remind passengers to hold the handrail and not to walk.","Old habits die hard, though. For those who have always walked, standing still feels like a waste of time, even if the [[10]] say otherwise. Changing the custom may take a generation. Until then, most of us will keep standing on one side, watching the empty half of the escalator roll by."],["disappears","unevenly","statistics","free","willing","trial","convincing","injuries","ignored","builds up"],[[3,"形容詞","leave + 受詞 + 形容詞；leave ... free 是把另一邊「空出來」。"],[5,"名詞","a three-week trial「為期三週的試驗」。"],[8,"動詞","有些人乾脆「不理會」指示，過去式。"],[6,"形容詞","結果「令人信服」，冒號後面是數據。"],[4,"形容詞","be willing to V「願意…」。"],[9,"片語","a long line builds up「排起長長的隊伍」，主詞單數，動詞加 s。"],[0,"動詞","下面的人潮更快「消失」；主詞 the crowd 是單數。"],[1,"副詞","wear out unevenly「磨損不平均」，修飾動詞。"],[7,"名詞","hundreds of 後面接複數名詞；造成數百起「受傷」。"],[2,"名詞","the statistics say otherwise「統計數字顯示並非如此」，動詞 say 是複數。"]],["在許多城市，搭手扶梯有個不成文的規定：站一邊，把另一邊空出來給趕時間的人。這個習慣看起來既有禮貌又有效率。不過工程師有疑慮。","2015 年，倫敦的交通主管機關在地鐵最繁忙的車站之一 Holborn 進行了一項為期三週的特別試驗。工作人員請乘客在「上行」手扶梯兩邊都站立，不要行走。許多通勤者很不高興，有些人乾脆不理會指示。但結果令人信服：在最繁忙的時段，所有人都站立的手扶梯，比留一條行走通道的手扶梯多載了大約百分之三十的人。底下的隊伍平常會排過整個大廳，試驗期間短了很多。","原因很簡單。在很長的手扶梯上，很少人願意爬，所以行走的那一邊有一半是空的，站立的那一邊卻在底下排起長長的隊伍。行走的人彼此之間也需要更大的空間。大家都站著時，兩邊都站滿，下面的人潮消失得更快。","還有其他理由。手扶梯製造商警告，重量全壓在一邊時，機器會磨損不平均，也更常故障。在日本，手扶梯上的跌倒每年造成數百起受傷，有幾個城市現在要求搭乘者站著不動。車站裡的海報提醒乘客握好扶手、不要行走。","不過習慣很難改。對一向用走的人來說，站著不動感覺是浪費時間，即使統計數字顯示並非如此。要改變這個習慣可能需要一個世代。在那之前，我們大多數人還是會站在一邊，看著手扶梯空著的那一半從眼前滑過。"],289],[13,"在南極過冬",["Every February, the last plane leaves the research station at the South Pole, and about forty people are left behind to face the [[1]] winter. For the next eight months, almost no aircraft can land, because temperatures fall below minus 60 degrees Celsius. The sun sets in March and does not [[2]] again until September. Outside, the wind is so cold that bare skin freezes in minutes.","Those who “winter over” are scientists and the support staff who keep the station [[3]]: cooks, mechanics, doctors, and electricians. Before being accepted, each of them must pass strict physical and mental tests, since hardly anyone can be [[4]] if something goes wrong.","Life inside the station is comfortable enough. A greenhouse supplies fresh vegetables, and there are a gym, a library, and a music room. Internet access, however, is limited to a few hours a day, when satellites pass overhead. The real challenge is in the mind. Months of darkness [[5]] sleep, and living with the same faces day after day can make small habits, such as loud chewing or a repeated joke, seem [[6]]. To keep their [[7]] up, the crew hold film festivals, costume parties, and a grand dinner in the middle of winter, a tradition that [[8]] to the earliest explorers.","There are rewards as well. On clear nights, colorful lights [[9]] across the sky, and the stars are brighter than anywhere else on Earth. And when the first plane returns in spring, the crew members step out with a friendship that few other experiences can [[10]]. Quite a few of them sign up again. They say that the silence, the darkness, and the people they shared them with are impossible to forget."],["disturb","spirits","match","approaching","evacuated","unbearable","dance","running","dates back","rise"],[[3,"現在分詞","the approaching winter「即將到來的冬天」，當形容詞用。"],[9,"動詞","太陽三月落下，到九月才再「升起」；does not 後面接原形。"],[7,"現在分詞","keep + 受詞 + V-ing；讓研究站持續「運作」。"],[4,"過去分詞","be evacuated「被撤離」；出事時幾乎沒有人能撤走。"],[0,"動詞","disturb sleep「擾亂睡眠」；主詞 Months 是複數。"],[5,"形容詞","seem unbearable「顯得難以忍受」。"],[1,"名詞","keep one's spirits up「保持士氣、打起精神」。"],[8,"片語","date back to「追溯到」；主詞 a tradition 是單數，動詞加 s。"],[6,"動詞","極光在天空「舞動」；主詞 lights 是複數。"],[2,"動詞","can match「比得上」；很少有經驗比得上這份情誼。"]],["每年二月，最後一班飛機離開南極點的研究站，大約四十個人留下來面對即將到來的冬天。接下來的八個月，飛機幾乎都無法降落，因為氣溫會降到攝氏零下 60 度以下。太陽在三月落下，要到九月才會再升起。外面的風冷到裸露的皮膚幾分鐘就會凍傷。","「留守過冬」的人是科學家，以及讓研究站持續運作的後勤人員：廚師、技師、醫生和電工。錄取之前，每個人都必須通過嚴格的身心檢測，因為一旦出事，幾乎沒有人能被撤離。","站內的生活算是舒適。溫室供應新鮮蔬菜，還有健身房、圖書室和音樂室。不過每天只有衛星經過上空的幾個小時能上網。真正的挑戰在心理。幾個月的黑暗會擾亂睡眠，而日復一日和同樣的面孔相處，會讓小習慣——像是咀嚼很大聲或重複的笑話——顯得難以忍受。為了保持士氣，隊員會舉辦影展、變裝派對，以及隆重的仲冬晚宴，這個傳統可以追溯到最早的探險家。","也有回報。晴朗的夜晚，彩色的光在天空舞動，星星比地球上任何地方都亮。當春天第一班飛機回來時，隊員帶著一份很少有其他經驗比得上的情誼走出來。其中不少人還會再報名。他們說，那份寂靜、那片黑暗，還有一起度過的人，都讓人無法忘懷。"],285],[14,"切洋蔥為什麼會流淚",["Chopping onions brings tears to almost every cook's eyes. No amount of experience seems to help. These tears are the result of a chemical [[1]] that the onion has developed to protect itself from hungry animals.","As it grows underground, an onion takes in certain minerals from the soil and stores them in its cells. As long as the cells remain whole, nothing happens. But once a knife [[2]] them, substances that were kept apart suddenly mix, [[3]] a rapid chain of reactions. The final product is a gas that floats up toward your face. When it reaches the water in your eyes, it turns into a mild acid. Nerves in the eye [[4]] the burning feeling and send a signal to the brain, which orders the eyes to produce tears to [[5]] the acid out.","Over the years, cooks have suggested countless tricks, from holding a piece of bread in the mouth to lighting a candle. Most of them are [[6]]. What really helps is using a sharp knife, which damages fewer cells, and [[7]] the onion in the refrigerator for half an hour beforehand, since cold slows down the reactions. Cutting the onion near an open window or a fan also helps, because the moving air carries the gas away from your face.","Farmers have even developed a “tearless” onion, which went on sale in the United States a few years ago. Yet some chefs are not [[8]], complaining that it lacks the sharp [[9]] of an ordinary onion. After all, the very substances that make us cry are also what make onions taste like onions. A few tears may be a small [[10]] to pay for a delicious dinner."],["slices through","useless","chilling","flush","impressed","price","triggering","detect","defense","flavor"],[[8,"名詞","a chemical defense「化學防禦」，用來保護自己。"],[0,"片語","slice through「切穿」；主詞 a knife 是單數，動詞加 s。"],[6,"現在分詞","分詞構句，混合後「引發」一連串反應。"],[7,"動詞","nerves detect「神經偵測到」；主詞複數。"],[3,"動詞","flush ... out「把…沖掉」，to 後面接原形。"],[1,"形容詞","大部分偏方都「沒用」，下一句才是真正有效的方法。"],[2,"現在分詞","和 using 對稱；chill the onion「把洋蔥冰一下」。"],[4,"過去分詞","be not impressed「不覺得厲害、不買帳」。"],[9,"名詞","the sharp flavor「嗆辣的味道」。"],[5,"名詞","a small price to pay「值得付出的小代價」。"]],["切洋蔥幾乎會讓每個下廚的人流淚。經驗再豐富似乎也沒用。這些眼淚來自洋蔥發展出來的一種化學防禦，用來保護自己不被飢餓的動物吃掉。","洋蔥在地下生長時，會從土壤吸收某些礦物質並儲存在細胞裡。只要細胞完好，什麼事都不會發生。但一旦刀子切穿細胞，原本被分開的物質突然混合，引發一連串快速的反應。最後的產物是一種氣體，會往上飄向你的臉。它碰到你眼睛裡的水分時，會變成一種弱酸。眼睛的神經偵測到灼熱感，傳送訊號給大腦，大腦便命令眼睛分泌淚水把酸沖掉。","多年來，下廚的人提出過無數偏方，從嘴裡含一塊麵包到點蠟燭都有，大部分都沒用。真正有幫助的是用鋒利的刀，這樣破壞的細胞比較少，以及事先把洋蔥放進冰箱冰半小時，因為低溫會減慢反應。在打開的窗戶或電風扇旁邊切也有幫助，因為流動的空氣會把氣體從你臉前帶走。","農民甚至培育出「不流淚」的洋蔥，幾年前已在美國上市。但有些廚師並不買帳，抱怨它少了一般洋蔥的嗆辣味。畢竟讓我們流淚的那些物質，也正是讓洋蔥有洋蔥味的東西。為了一頓美味的晚餐流幾滴淚，或許是值得付出的小代價。"],283],[15,"鬱金香狂熱",["Tulips are so closely [[1]] with the Netherlands that many people assume they have always grown there. In fact, the flowers came from Central Asia and did not reach Holland until the late sixteenth century. The Dutch, then among the richest people in Europe, fell in love with the [[2]] flower. Most prized of all were tulips with flame-like stripes of color. Nobody knew at the time that the stripes were caused by a disease, which also made such bulbs weak and [[3]].","By the 1630s, tulips had become a symbol of wealth, and prices climbed steadily. Soon, people with no interest in gardening began buying bulbs simply to [[4]] them at a profit. Much of the trade took place in winter, when the bulbs were still in the ground, so buyers signed contracts [[5]] to pay for flowers they had never seen. At the [[6]] of the craze, a single bulb of the most famous variety was reportedly worth more than a fine house in Amsterdam. Bulbs changed hands several times a day without ever leaving the soil.","Then, in February 1637, buyers failed to [[7]] at an ordinary sale. Confidence vanished overnight. Prices [[8]], and the contracts became worthless.","For centuries, the story has been told as a warning against human greed. Modern historians, however, believe it has been greatly [[9]]: relatively few people were involved, and hardly anyone was ruined. Still, “tulip mania” [[10]] in the language of finance. Whenever the price of something rises beyond reason, someone is sure to mention the tulips. As for the Dutch, they never lost their love of the flower. Today, the Netherlands grows billions of tulips a year and ships them all over the world."],["rare","exaggerated","lives on","associated","show up","exotic","promising","peak","collapsed","resell"],[[3,"過去分詞","be associated with「和…聯想在一起」。"],[5,"形容詞","the exotic flower「異國的花」，因為是從外地傳入的。"],[0,"形容詞","病害讓球莖又弱又「稀少」，所以更珍貴。"],[9,"動詞","to 後面接原形；買來只為了「轉賣」獲利。"],[6,"現在分詞","contracts promising to pay「承諾付款的合約」。"],[7,"名詞","at the peak of「在…的最高峰」。"],[4,"片語","fail to show up「沒有出現」。"],[8,"動詞","prices collapsed「價格崩盤」，過去式，不接受詞。"],[1,"過去分詞","has been greatly exaggerated「被大幅誇大」。"],[2,"片語","live on「繼續存在、流傳下來」；主詞單數，動詞加 s。"]],["鬱金香和荷蘭的關聯如此緊密，許多人以為它一直都長在那裡。其實這種花來自中亞，直到十六世紀末才傳到荷蘭。當時荷蘭人是歐洲最富有的人之一，他們愛上了這種異國的花。最受珍視的是帶有火焰般彩色條紋的鬱金香。當時沒有人知道條紋是一種病害造成的，這種病也讓這類球莖又弱又稀少。","到了 1630 年代，鬱金香成了財富的象徵，價格穩定攀升。很快地，對園藝毫無興趣的人也開始買球莖，只為了轉賣獲利。大部分交易在冬天進行，那時球莖還埋在土裡，所以買家簽下合約，承諾為自己從未見過的花付款。在狂熱的最高峰，最著名品種的一顆球莖據說比阿姆斯特丹的一棟好房子還值錢。球莖一天之內轉手好幾次，卻從來沒有離開過土壤。","接著在 1637 年 2 月，一場普通的拍賣會上買家沒有出現。信心一夕之間消失，價格崩盤，合約變得一文不值。","幾個世紀以來，這個故事一直被當成對人類貪婪的警告。不過現代歷史學家認為它被大幅誇大了：參與的人相對很少，幾乎沒有人因此破產。儘管如此，「鬱金香狂熱」仍然在金融用語中流傳下來。每當某樣東西的價格漲到不合理，一定會有人提起鬱金香。至於荷蘭人，他們對這種花的愛從未消失。如今荷蘭每年種植數十億株鬱金香，運往世界各地。"],288],[16,"拼圖的黃金年代",["The jigsaw puzzle began as a teaching tool. Around 1760, John Spilsbury, a London mapmaker, glued a map of Europe onto a thin board and cut it into pieces along the national [[1]]. Children who put the pieces back together learned geography while having fun. These “dissected maps,” as they were called, were an [[2]] hit among wealthy families, the only ones who could afford such handmade luxuries.","For the next century, puzzles remained expensive and mainly [[3]]. Puzzles for adults, which showed pictures instead of maps, did not appear until around 1900. These early puzzles were extremely difficult. There was no picture on the box to guide the player, and the pieces did not [[4]], so a careless sneeze could [[5]] a whole evening's work.","The golden age of the puzzle came, strangely enough, with the Great Depression of the 1930s. Manufacturers had learned to cut puzzles out of cardboard by machine, and prices [[6]] sharply. For millions of jobless Americans, a puzzle offered hours of cheap entertainment, as well as a small sense of [[7]] at a time when little else was going right. Some shops even rented puzzles out by the night, like books from a library.","History repeated itself in 2020, when the pandemic kept people at home. Sales rose so fast that puzzle makers could hardly [[8]] demand.","Why do puzzles comfort us in hard times? Psychologists point out that a puzzle is a problem that is [[9]] to have a solution, which real life seldom provides. In an anxious world, it is [[10]] to watch a pile of confusion slowly turn into order. Each piece that fits is a small victory, and the finished picture is proof that patience is rewarded."],["interlock","educational","guaranteed","borders","immediate","ruin","keep up with","soothing","accomplishment","dropped"],[[3,"名詞","along the national borders「沿著國界」，要用複數。"],[4,"形容詞","an immediate hit「立刻大受歡迎」。"],[1,"形容詞","拼圖仍然昂貴，而且主要是「教育用的」，呼應第一句 a teaching tool。"],[0,"動詞","did not 後面接原形；早期的拼圖片不會「互相卡住」。"],[5,"動詞","could 後面接原形；一個噴嚏就能「毀掉」整晚的成果。"],[9,"動詞","prices dropped sharply「價格大幅下降」，過去式。"],[8,"名詞","a sense of accomplishment「成就感」。"],[6,"片語","keep up with demand「趕上需求」。"],[2,"過去分詞","be guaranteed to V「保證一定會…」。"],[7,"形容詞","it is soothing to V「…讓人感到平靜」。"]],["拼圖一開始是教學工具。大約 1760 年，倫敦的地圖製作者 John Spilsbury 把一張歐洲地圖黏在薄木板上，沿著國界切成小塊。把碎片拼回去的孩子邊玩邊學地理。這些當時稱為「分割地圖」的東西在富裕家庭之間立刻大受歡迎，因為只有他們買得起這種手工奢侈品。","接下來的一個世紀，拼圖仍然昂貴，而且主要是教育用途。給成人的拼圖用的是圖畫而不是地圖，要到 1900 年左右才出現。這些早期的拼圖極為困難。盒子上沒有圖片可以參考，拼圖片也不會互相卡住，所以一個不小心的噴嚏就能毀掉一整晚的成果。","說來奇怪，拼圖的黃金年代是隨著 1930 年代的經濟大蕭條而來。製造商學會用機器從厚紙板切出拼圖，價格大幅下降。對數百萬失業的美國人來說，一盒拼圖提供了好幾個小時的便宜娛樂，也在諸事不順的時候帶來一點成就感。有些商店甚至像圖書館借書一樣，把拼圖按晚出租。","2020 年歷史重演，疫情讓人們待在家裡。銷量上升之快，拼圖製造商幾乎趕不上需求。","為什麼拼圖能在艱難的時候安慰我們？心理學家指出，拼圖是一個保證有解答的問題，而現實生活很少能如此。在令人焦慮的世界裡，看著一堆混亂慢慢變得有秩序，讓人感到平靜。每拼對一片都是一次小小的勝利，而完成的圖畫證明了耐心會有回報。"],290],[17,"棉花糖實驗的另一面",["In the late 1960s, psychologist Walter Mischel placed a marshmallow in front of young children and offered them a simple [[1]]: eat it now, or wait about fifteen minutes alone and get two. Some children ate the sweet the moment he left the room. Others [[2]] to wait, covering their eyes or singing to themselves in order to [[3]] the temptation. A few even turned their chairs around so that they could not see the sweet.","Years later, Mischel's team checked on the same children. Those who had waited longer seemed to do better at school and to handle stress more easily. The finding became famous, and the [[4]] most people drew from it was that self-control in childhood is the key to success. Parents and teachers everywhere began looking for ways to train children to wait.","The story, however, is not so simple. In 2018, other researchers repeated the experiment with more than 900 children from more [[5]] backgrounds. Once family income and the parents' education were [[6]], the link between waiting and later achievement almost disappeared. A child from a poor home, where promised treats do not always [[7]], may have learned that food in the hand is safer than food in the future. For such a child, eating the marshmallow at once is a [[8]] decision, not a sign of weakness.","Another study supports this view. Children who had just been let down by an adult waited for a much shorter time than those who had dealt with a [[9]] one.","The lesson may be this: if we want children to wait for better things, we must first give them a world in which waiting [[10]]. Patience, in other words, is not just a matter of character; it also depends on experience."],["pays off","struggled","taken into account","diverse","resist","deal","trustworthy","conclusion","arrive","sensible"],[[5,"名詞","offer a deal「提出一個交易（條件）」，冒號後面是內容。"],[1,"動詞","struggle to V「努力掙扎著…」，過去式。"],[4,"動詞","resist the temptation「抗拒誘惑」，to 後面接原形。"],[7,"名詞","draw a conclusion「得出結論」。"],[3,"形容詞","diverse backgrounds「多元的背景」。"],[2,"片語","be taken into account「被納入考量」。"],[8,"動詞","承諾的零食不一定會「到來」；do not 後面接原形，不接受詞。"],[9,"形容詞","a sensible decision「合理的決定」，和 a sign of weakness 對比。"],[6,"形容詞","a trustworthy one「值得信任的大人」，和前面 let down 的大人對比。"],[0,"片語","pay off「有回報」；主詞 waiting 是單數，動詞加 s。"]],["1960 年代末期，心理學家 Walter Mischel 在幼童面前放了一顆棉花糖，並提出一個簡單的條件：現在吃掉，或是獨自等大約十五分鐘，就能拿到兩顆。有些孩子在他離開房間的那一刻就把糖吃了。另一些孩子努力忍耐，遮住眼睛或自己唱歌，以抗拒誘惑。有幾個孩子甚至把椅子轉過去，讓自己看不到糖。","多年後，Mischel 的團隊追蹤了同一批孩子。當年等得比較久的人，學業表現似乎比較好，也比較能應付壓力。這項發現變得很有名，而多數人從中得出的結論是：童年時期的自制力是成功的關鍵。各地的父母和老師開始想辦法訓練孩子等待。","然而事情沒這麼簡單。2018 年，其他研究者用背景更多元的 900 多名兒童重做了這個實驗。把家庭收入和父母的教育程度納入考量之後，等待和日後成就之間的關聯幾乎消失了。來自貧窮家庭的孩子，大人答應的零食不一定會到來，他們可能已經學到：拿在手裡的食物比未來的食物可靠。對這樣的孩子來說，立刻吃掉棉花糖是合理的決定，而不是軟弱的表現。","另一項研究也支持這個看法。剛被大人放過鴿子的孩子，等待的時間比遇到值得信任的大人的孩子短得多。","這給我們的啟示或許是：如果希望孩子為了更好的東西而等待，我們必須先給他們一個等待會有回報的世界。換句話說，耐心不只是性格的問題，也取決於經驗。"],299],[18,"樹木的地下網路",["A forest may look like a collection of separate trees competing for sunlight. Beneath the soil, however, the trees are [[1]] by a vast network of fungi. Thread-like fungi wrap around the roots of trees and [[2]] far into the ground. Since the fungi cannot make their own food, they take sugar from the trees. In [[3]], they bring the trees water and minerals from places that roots cannot reach.","In the 1990s, Canadian scientist Suzanne Simard discovered something even more surprising. Using a form of carbon that she could [[4]], she showed that sugar traveled through the fungal threads from one tree to another. The journal that published her study [[5]] the network the “wood wide web.” Later studies suggested that the oldest and largest trees are the busiest centers of the network, and that trees attacked by insects may even send chemical [[6]] to warn their neighbors. A dying tree, some claimed, might even pass its remaining sugar on to younger trees nearby.","These ideas [[7]] the public imagination. Books and films began to describe the forest as a caring community rather than a battlefield. The idea that trees look after one another was simply too attractive to ignore.","Recently, though, some researchers have called for caution. A review published in 2023 argued that the evidence from real forests is thin and that popular writers have [[8]] what is actually known. It remains unclear whether the amount of sugar passing through the fungi is [[9]] to make any difference to a tree's survival.","The debate is a reminder that science is never finished. The underground network certainly exists. How much it matters is still an open [[10]]. Answering it will require many more years of patient work in the woods."],["sufficient","trace","linked","exchange","nicknamed","overstated","signals","extend","question","captured"],[[2,"過去分詞","be linked by「被…連結起來」。"],[7,"動詞","和 wrap 對稱用原形；extend into the ground「延伸到地下」。"],[3,"名詞","in exchange「作為交換」。"],[1,"動詞","could 後面接原形；trace「追蹤」這種碳的去向。"],[4,"動詞","nickname A B「把 A 暱稱為 B」，過去式。"],[6,"名詞","chemical signals「化學訊號」，要用複數。"],[9,"動詞","capture the public imagination「引起大眾的想像與興趣」，過去式。"],[5,"過去分詞","have overstated「誇大了」已知的事。"],[0,"形容詞","be sufficient to V「足以…」。"],[8,"名詞","an open question「尚無定論的問題」。"]],["森林看起來可能像一群各自爭奪陽光的樹。然而在土壤之下，樹木被一張巨大的真菌網路連結在一起。絲狀的真菌纏繞著樹根，並向地下延伸到很遠的地方。因為真菌無法自己製造養分，它們從樹木取得糖分。作為交換，它們從樹根到不了的地方替樹木帶來水和礦物質。","1990 年代，加拿大科學家 Suzanne Simard 發現了更令人驚訝的事。她利用一種可以追蹤的碳，證明糖分會經由真菌的細絲從一棵樹傳到另一棵樹。刊登她研究的期刊把這個網路暱稱為「樹聯網」。後來的研究指出，最老、最大的樹是網路中最繁忙的樞紐，而遭到昆蟲攻擊的樹甚至可能發出化學訊號警告鄰居。有人主張，垂死的樹甚至可能把剩下的糖分傳給附近較年輕的樹。","這些想法引起大眾的想像。書籍和影片開始把森林描述成一個互相照顧的社群，而不是戰場。樹木會彼此照顧，這個想法實在太吸引人，讓人難以忽視。","不過近來有些研究者呼籲要謹慎。2023 年發表的一篇回顧研究認為，來自真實森林的證據很薄弱，而通俗作品誇大了實際已知的內容。經由真菌傳遞的糖分是否足以影響一棵樹的存活，目前仍不清楚。","這場爭論提醒我們，科學永遠沒有完成的一天。地下網路確實存在，但它有多重要仍然是個尚無定論的問題。要回答它，還需要在森林裡耐心工作許多年。"],293],[19,"牛仔褲的誕生",["Jeans are worn by presidents and farmers alike, but they began as work clothes. In 1853, Levi Strauss, a young immigrant from Germany, moved to San Francisco to open a branch of his family's business, [[1]] cloth and clothing to small stores across the American West.","One of his customers was Jacob Davis, a tailor in Nevada. Around 1870, a woman asked Davis to make a pair of trousers for her husband, a woodcutter whose pants kept [[2]] at the pockets. Davis had an idea: he [[3]] the corners of the pockets with the small metal pins he normally used on horse blankets. The trousers proved so [[4]] that orders poured in. Davis feared that someone would steal his idea, but he lacked the money for a patent [[5]]. He therefore wrote to Strauss and proposed that they become partners. The patent was [[6]] on May 20, 1873, a date now regarded as the birthday of blue jeans. The two men began producing the trousers in San Francisco, and miners could not get enough of them.","For decades, jeans were worn mainly by miners, cowboys, and railway workers. Their image changed in the 1950s, when film stars wore them on screen as a sign of youthful [[7]]. Some schools [[8]] jeans from classrooms, which only made teenagers want them more. By the 1970s, jeans had [[9]] around the world as a symbol of freedom. In some countries where they were hard to find, a single pair could cost a month's salary.","Today, jeans come in countless styles and prices. Some are even [[10]] torn before they are sold, a fashion that would surely have puzzled the miners of the Old West."],["rebellion","reinforced","application","spread","banned","tearing","deliberately","durable","granted","supplying"],[[9,"現在分詞","分詞構句，說明這家店「供應」布料和衣服給各地小店。"],[5,"現在分詞","keep + V-ing；褲子的口袋老是「裂開」。"],[1,"動詞","reinforce A with B「用 B 加強 A」，過去式。"],[7,"形容詞","prove so durable「結果非常耐穿」。"],[2,"名詞","a patent application「專利申請」。"],[8,"過去分詞","the patent was granted「專利獲得核准」。"],[0,"名詞","youthful rebellion「年輕人的叛逆」。"],[4,"動詞","ban A from B「禁止 A 進入 B」，過去式。"],[3,"過去分詞","had spread around the world「已經傳遍全世界」，spread 三態同形。"],[6,"副詞","be deliberately torn「被故意弄破」，修飾 torn。"]],["從總統到農夫都穿牛仔褲，但它一開始是工作服。1853 年，來自德國的年輕移民 Levi Strauss 搬到舊金山，為家族生意開設分店，供應布料和衣服給美國西部各地的小商店。","他的顧客之一是內華達州的裁縫 Jacob Davis。1870 年左右，一位婦人請 Davis 替她丈夫做一條褲子，她丈夫是伐木工，褲子的口袋老是裂開。Davis 想到一個主意：他用平常釘馬毯的小金屬釘加強口袋的邊角。這條褲子非常耐穿，訂單大量湧入。Davis 擔心有人偷走他的點子，卻沒有錢申請專利，於是寫信給 Strauss，提議兩人合夥。專利在 1873 年 5 月 20 日獲得核准，這一天現在被視為牛仔褲的生日。兩人開始在舊金山生產這種褲子，礦工們搶著要買。","幾十年來，穿牛仔褲的主要是礦工、牛仔和鐵路工人。它的形象在 1950 年代改變，當時電影明星在銀幕上穿牛仔褲，作為年輕人叛逆的象徵。有些學校禁止學生穿牛仔褲進教室，結果只讓青少年更想穿。到了 1970 年代，牛仔褲已經作為自由的象徵傳遍全世界。在某些很難買到的國家，一條牛仔褲可能要價一個月的薪水。","如今牛仔褲有數不清的款式和價位。有些甚至在出售前就被故意弄破，這種時尚一定會讓昔日西部的礦工摸不著頭緒。"],283],[20,"第五種味道：鮮味",["For centuries, Western science recognized only four basic tastes: sweet, sour, salty, and bitter. Japanese cooks, however, had long felt that something was [[1]]. The clear soup made from dried seaweed, a foundation of Japanese cooking, had a rich taste that fit none of the four. They had no name for it, but they could not cook without it.","In 1907, a chemist named Kikunae Ikeda, enjoying a bowl of his wife's tofu soup, became [[2]] to find the source of that taste. He boiled down a large quantity of seaweed and, after many steps, [[3]] a small amount of a substance called glutamate. He named its taste umami, from the Japanese word for “delicious,” and developed a seasoning based on it, which is still [[4]] sold today.","Western scientists were [[5]] to accept a fifth taste. Many argued that umami was merely a [[6]] of the other four. The matter was not settled until around 2000, when researchers [[7]] tiny structures on the tongue that respond only to glutamate. Umami was at last accepted as the fifth basic taste, nearly a century after Ikeda's discovery.","Umami is by no means limited to Japanese food. Glutamate [[8]] naturally in tomatoes, aged cheese, mushrooms, and dried meat. It is even found in human breast milk, which may explain why we find the taste so [[9]]. Long before Ikeda, cooks around the world had discovered by experience that such ingredients [[10]] the flavor of a dish. Italians add cheese to pasta, and Chinese cooks put dried mushrooms in their soups. They were all using umami without knowing its name. The next time a simple bowl of soup tastes surprisingly rich, you will know what to thank."],["extracted","identified","satisfying","combination","occurs","missing","bring out","widely","reluctant","determined"],[[5,"形容詞","something was missing「少了什麼」。"],[9,"形容詞","become determined to V「下定決心要…」。"],[0,"動詞","extract「提取、萃取」出一種物質，過去式。"],[7,"副詞","be widely sold「被廣泛販售」，修飾 sold。"],[8,"形容詞","be reluctant to V「不願意…」，後面說他們提出反對理由。"],[3,"名詞","a combination of「…的組合」。"],[1,"動詞","identify「找出、辨識出」舌頭上的構造，過去式。"],[4,"動詞","occur naturally in「天然存在於」；主詞 Glutamate 是單數，動詞加 s。"],[2,"形容詞","find the taste so satisfying「覺得這種味道很令人滿足」。"],[6,"片語","bring out the flavor「帶出味道」；主詞複數用原形。"]],["幾個世紀以來，西方科學只承認四種基本味道：甜、酸、鹹、苦。然而日本的廚師早就覺得少了什麼。用乾燥海藻熬成的清湯是日本料理的基礎，它有一種濃郁的味道，不屬於四種中的任何一種。他們叫不出它的名字，做菜卻少不了它。","1907 年，化學家池田菊苗在享用妻子煮的豆腐湯時，下定決心要找出那種味道的來源。他熬煮了大量的海藻，經過許多步驟，提取出少量一種叫做麩胺酸鹽的物質。他把這種味道命名為 umami，源自日文的「美味」，並以它為基礎開發出一種調味料，至今仍被廣泛販售。","西方科學家不願意接受第五種味道。許多人認為鮮味只是另外四種味道的組合。這個問題直到 2000 年左右才有定論，當時研究者在舌頭上找出只對麩胺酸鹽有反應的微小構造。鮮味終於被接受為第五種基本味道，這時距離池田的發現已經將近一個世紀。","鮮味絕不限於日本食物。麩胺酸鹽天然存在於番茄、熟成乳酪、香菇和肉乾裡。甚至人類的母乳裡也有，這或許能解釋我們為什麼覺得這種味道如此令人滿足。早在池田之前，世界各地的廚師就已經從經驗中發現，這類食材能帶出一道菜的味道。義大利人在義大利麵上加乳酪，中國廚師在湯裡放乾香菇。他們都在使用鮮味，只是不知道它的名字。下次一碗簡單的湯喝起來出奇地濃郁時，你就知道該感謝什麼了。"],285]],"PS":[[1,"冰島的聖誕書潮",["Every Christmas Eve, people in Iceland unwrap their presents, and nearly everyone finds at least one book among them. [[1]] This cozy custom is part of what Icelanders call Jólabókaflóð, the “Christmas book flood.”","The tradition goes back to World War II. At that time, strict limits were placed on imported goods, and Icelanders had little to buy as gifts. [[2]] Books therefore became the nation's favorite present, and the habit outlived the war.","The “flood” begins in the autumn, when publishers release most of their new titles within a few weeks. [[3]] Families go through it eagerly, marking the titles they hope to receive. As a result, a large share of the year's book sales takes place in the last two months of the year.","For a country of fewer than 400,000 people, Iceland has a remarkably lively book culture. It publishes more books per person than almost any other nation. [[4]] So far, though, the book flood shows no sign of drying up."],["To help readers choose, a free catalog of all the new books is delivered to every household in the country.","Some worry that this love of printed books may fade as younger readers turn to screens.","Paper, however, was one of the few things that remained easy to obtain.","The rest of the evening is then spent reading in bed, often with a cup of hot chocolate.","Icelandic, the national language, has changed very little over the past thousand years."],[3,2,0,1],["前一句說幾乎每個人都會收到書，這一句用 then 接著說當晚就讀書；下一句的 This cozy custom 指的就是這件事。","前一句說進口商品受限、沒什麼可買，however 轉折說紙張仍容易取得；下一句 Books therefore 是結果。","前一句說出版社集中出新書，這一句說寄送新書目錄；下一句的 it 指的就是 catalog。","下一句 So far, though 表示「不過目前為止」，前面必須是一個擔憂；這一句正好說有人擔心年輕人改看螢幕。"],[4,"冰島語千年來變化很小，和聖誕書潮無關，放進任何一格都接不上前後文。"],["每年聖誕夜，冰島人拆開禮物，幾乎每個人都會在裡面找到至少一本書。接著，當晚剩下的時間就在床上讀書度過，常常還配一杯熱巧克力。這個溫馨的習俗是冰島人所說的 Jólabókaflóð，也就是「聖誕書潮」的一部分。","這項傳統可以追溯到第二次世界大戰。當時進口商品受到嚴格限制，冰島人沒什麼東西可以買來送禮。不過紙張是少數仍然容易取得的東西之一。書因此成為全國最受歡迎的禮物，這個習慣在戰後也延續下來。","「書潮」從秋天開始，出版社在幾週之內推出大部分的新書。為了幫讀者挑選，一本列出所有新書的免費目錄會送到全國每一戶人家。家家戶戶興致勃勃地翻閱，標出希望收到的書。因此，全年書籍銷量有很大一部分集中在最後兩個月。","以一個人口不到四十萬的國家來說，冰島的閱讀文化非常活躍。它的人均出版量幾乎比任何國家都高。有些人擔心，隨著年輕讀者轉向螢幕，這份對紙本書的熱愛可能會消退。不過到目前為止，書潮沒有任何乾涸的跡象。"],243],[2,"為什麼會起雞皮疙瘩",["Step out of a warm shower into a cold room, and tiny bumps will instantly appear on your arms. [[1]] What exactly are these “goosebumps,” and why do we get them?","Each hair on the human body grows out of a small pocket in the skin, and attached to each pocket is a tiny muscle. When we are cold or frightened, the brain sends out a signal without our being aware of it. [[2]] We cannot produce goosebumps at will, nor can we stop them.","For our distant ancestors, this reaction was truly useful. They were covered with thick hair, and raised hair trapped a layer of air that kept the body warm. [[3]] A frightened cat with its fur standing on end is doing exactly the same thing today.","Humans have since lost most of their body hair, so goosebumps no longer keep us warm or scare anyone away. [[4]] Recent research on mice, however, suggests that the muscles and nerves behind goosebumps may help the skin grow new hair. Perhaps this old reaction still has a job to do after all."],["The muscles tighten in response, pulling the hairs upright and pushing up the skin around them.","Many male birds, by contrast, puff up their feathers to attract a partner.","Scientists therefore long regarded them as a useless leftover from the past.","It also made them look larger and more threatening to their enemies.","The same thing may happen when you watch a horror movie or hear a moving piece of music."],[4,0,3,2],["前一句是冷的時候起雞皮疙瘩，The same thing 指同樣的現象，補充害怕和感動時也會發生。","前一句說大腦發出訊號，in response 說明肌肉的反應；The muscles 對應前面的 a tiny muscle。","前一句是豎起的毛可以保暖，also 補上第二個好處：看起來更大更嚇人；下一句的貓是例子。","前一句說雞皮疙瘩已經沒有保暖或嚇阻的作用，therefore 得出「被當成沒用的殘留」；下一句 however 再翻轉。"],[1,"許多雄鳥鼓起羽毛是為了求偶，by contrast 找不到可以對比的前文，和人類的雞皮疙瘩也接不上。"],["從溫暖的淋浴間走進寒冷的房間，手臂上立刻會冒出小顆粒。看恐怖片或聽到動人的音樂時，也可能發生同樣的事。這些「雞皮疙瘩」到底是什麼？我們為什麼會起雞皮疙瘩？","人體的每一根毛髮都從皮膚裡的一個小囊長出來，每個小囊都連著一條微小的肌肉。我們覺得冷或害怕時，大腦會在我們沒有意識到的情況下發出訊號。肌肉隨之收縮，把毛髮拉直，也把周圍的皮膚往上推。我們無法隨意製造雞皮疙瘩，也無法阻止它。","對我們遙遠的祖先來說，這個反應非常有用。他們全身長滿濃密的毛，豎起的毛能困住一層空氣，替身體保暖。這也讓他們在敵人眼中看起來更大、更有威脅性。今天受驚的貓全身的毛豎起來，做的正是同一件事。","人類後來失去了大部分的體毛，所以雞皮疙瘩不再能保暖，也嚇不走誰。科學家因此長期把它視為過去留下來的無用殘留。不過最近對老鼠的研究顯示，雞皮疙瘩背後的肌肉和神經可能有助於皮膚長出新的毛髮。也許這個古老的反應終究還有任務在身。"],250],[3,"酸種麵包",["Long before factories began selling yeast in neat little packets, bakers relied on sourdough. [[1]] Fed regularly with fresh flour, the mixture stays alive and can be used to make bread rise again and again.","Sourdough is probably the oldest form of raised bread. Historians believe that the ancient Egyptians were already baking it thousands of years ago. [[2]] Things changed in the nineteenth century, when cheap factory-made yeast allowed bakers to produce bread in a fraction of the time.","Sourdough never disappeared entirely, though. During the California Gold Rush, miners carried their precious mixtures with them from camp to camp. [[3]] San Francisco has been famous for its sour, chewy bread ever since, and one bakery there claims to have kept the same mixture alive since 1849.","The bread enjoyed a surprising comeback in 2020. Stuck at home during the pandemic, millions of people tried raising their own mixtures and proudly shared photos of their loaves online. [[4]] For them, sourdough was much more than something to eat."],["Rice, on the other hand, remains the main food in most parts of Asia.","On freezing mountain nights, some even slept beside them to keep them warm.","Many found the slow, daily routine of feeding and baking a welcome comfort during the long weeks of lockdown.","For most of history, in fact, it was the usual way to make a light, soft loaf.","This is a mixture of flour and water that is left to sit for days so that wild yeast and bacteria can grow in it."],[4,3,1,2],["前一句提到 sourdough，This is ... 是定義；下一句的 the mixture 指的就是這種麵糊。","前一句說古埃及人已經在烤，in fact 進一步說歷史上大多數時候這是最常用的方法；下一句 Things changed 接變化。","前一句說礦工帶著麵種在營地之間移動，even 更進一步：山上冰冷的夜晚還睡在旁邊；them 指 mixtures。","前一句說疫情期間很多人在家養麵種，Many 指這些人，lockdown（封城）呼應 Stuck at home；下一句 For them 也指同一群人。"],[0,"米飯是亞洲主食，和酸種麵包的歷史無關，on the other hand 也沒有對比的對象。"],["早在工廠開始販賣一小包一小包的酵母之前，麵包師傅靠的是酸種。那是一種麵粉和水的混合物，靜置幾天，讓野生酵母和細菌在裡面生長。只要定期餵新的麵粉，這團麵糊就能一直活著，反覆用來讓麵包發起來。","酸種麵包可能是最古老的發酵麵包。歷史學家認為，古埃及人幾千年前就已經在烤了。事實上，在歷史上大部分的時間，這是做出鬆軟麵包最常用的方法。情況在十九世紀改變，便宜的工廠酵母讓麵包師傅只花一小部分的時間就能做出麵包。","不過酸種從未完全消失。加州淘金熱期間，礦工帶著珍貴的麵種從一個營地到另一個營地。在山上冰冷的夜晚，有些人甚至睡在麵種旁邊替它保暖。舊金山從此以又酸又有嚼勁的麵包聞名，當地一家麵包店聲稱同一團麵種從 1849 年養到現在。","這種麵包在 2020 年意外地重新流行。疫情期間被困在家裡，數百萬人嘗試養自己的麵種，並驕傲地在網路上分享麵包的照片。許多人發現，每天餵養、烘焙的緩慢步調，在漫長的封城期間是一種難得的安慰。對他們來說，酸種遠遠不只是食物。"],253],[4,"偽裝成舞蹈的武術：卡波耶拉",["In a circle of clapping, singing people, two players spin, kick, and duck to the rhythm of drums. At first sight, they seem to be dancing. [[1]]","Capoeira was created by Africans who had been brought to Brazil as slaves to work on sugar farms. Forbidden to carry weapons or to train for combat, they needed a way to defend themselves. [[2]] What looked like harmless entertainment was actually preparation for escape.","After slavery ended in 1888, many former slaves moved to the cities, where life remained hard. Some used their skills in street fights or worked for criminal gangs. [[3]] For decades, it survived only in secret.","The turning point came in the 1930s, when a master known as Bimba opened the first capoeira school and presented the art as a respectable sport. The ban was soon lifted. [[4]] In 2014, it was added to UNESCO's list of cultural treasures. An art once practiced in chains has become a worldwide symbol of freedom."],["They therefore hid their fighting moves inside music and dance so that their masters would not become suspicious.","Since then, capoeira has spread far beyond Brazil and is now taught in more than a hundred countries.","As a result, the government made capoeira illegal in 1890, and anyone caught practicing it could be sent to prison.","In fact, they are practicing capoeira, a martial art that was born in Brazil several hundred years ago.","Sugar was then one of the most valuable products traded between Europe and the Americas."],[3,0,2,1],["前一句說看起來像跳舞，In fact 指出其實是武術卡波耶拉，並帶出主題。","前一句說他們不能帶武器也不能練武，therefore 接結果：把招式藏在音樂和舞蹈裡；下一句呼應「看似娛樂」。","前一句說有人用來打架或替幫派做事，As a result 帶出政府禁止；下一句「只能祕密流傳」是後續。","前一句說禁令解除，Since then 接之後傳到世界各地；下一句 2014 年列入 UNESCO 延續。"],[4,"這句講糖的貿易價值，雖然第二段提到糖廠，但和卡波耶拉的發展無關，放在任何一格都會打斷文意。"],["在一圈拍手唱歌的人中間，兩個人隨著鼓聲的節奏旋轉、踢腿、閃躲。乍看之下，他們像在跳舞。其實他們正在練卡波耶拉，一種幾百年前誕生在巴西的武術。","卡波耶拉是由被帶到巴西糖廠當奴隸的非洲人創造的。他們被禁止攜帶武器，也不准接受戰鬥訓練，需要一種保護自己的方法。因此他們把格鬥的招式藏在音樂和舞蹈裡，讓主人不會起疑。看起來無害的娛樂，其實是在為逃跑做準備。","1888 年奴隸制度結束後，許多從前的奴隸搬到城市，生活依然艱難。有些人把這身本領用在街頭打鬥，或替犯罪幫派做事。結果政府在 1890 年把卡波耶拉列為非法，任何被抓到練習的人都可能入獄。幾十年間，它只能在暗地裡流傳。","轉捩點出現在 1930 年代，一位人稱 Bimba 的師傅開設了第一所卡波耶拉學校，把這門技藝呈現為一項體面的運動。禁令不久就解除了。從那時起，卡波耶拉傳到巴西以外很遠的地方，如今有一百多個國家在教。2014 年，它被列入聯合國教科文組織的文化遺產名錄。一門曾經戴著鎖鏈練習的技藝，成了全世界自由的象徵。"],250],[5,"樹懶為什麼這麼慢",["Sloths, which live in the rainforests of Central and South America, are the slowest mammals on Earth. [[1]] Their very name is an old English word for laziness. Yet their slowness is not a weakness but a clever way of surviving.","Sloths feed mainly on leaves, which are hard to digest and provide very little energy. [[2]] With so little fuel, the animal simply cannot afford to move fast. Instead, it saves energy in every possible way, resting for most of the day and keeping its body temperature lower than that of most mammals.","Moving slowly also keeps sloths safe. Their main enemies, such as eagles and jaguars, hunt by sight and are quick to notice movement. A sloth hanging motionless among the branches is easy to miss. [[3]] To a passing hunter, a sloth looks like just another part of the tree.","There is one risk that sloths cannot avoid. About once a week, they climb all the way down to the ground to relieve themselves. [[4]] Whatever the reason, the sloth reminds us that in nature, being fast is not the only way to win."],["What is more, tiny green plants grow in its fur, helping it blend in with the leaves.","A single meal can take a sloth up to a month to process.","Jaguars are excellent swimmers and often hunt along rivers.","On the ground, they cover only about two meters a minute.","Scientists are still not sure why they take such a dangerous trip instead of staying safely in the trees."],[3,1,0,4],["前一句說樹懶是最慢的哺乳類，這一句用數字具體說明有多慢；下一句再說連牠們的名字本來就是「懶惰」的意思。","前一句說葉子難消化、熱量低，這一句說一餐要消化將近一個月；下一句 With so little fuel 呼應。","前一句說不動的樹懶很容易被忽略，What is more 再加一項：毛上長綠色小植物幫牠融入樹葉；下一句是總結。","前一句說牠們每週爬下地面排泄，such a dangerous trip 指的就是這件事；下一句 Whatever the reason 呼應 why。"],[2,"美洲豹會游泳、在河邊獵食，主詞換成美洲豹，和樹懶慢的原因無關。"],["樹懶生活在中南美洲的雨林裡，是地球上最慢的哺乳動物。在地面上，牠們一分鐘只能前進大約兩公尺。牠們的英文名字 sloth 本來就是「懶惰」的意思。然而牠們的慢不是弱點，而是一種聰明的生存方式。","樹懶主要吃樹葉，樹葉很難消化，提供的能量也非常少。一餐可能要花樹懶將近一個月才能消化完。燃料這麼少，牠根本負擔不起快速移動。於是牠用一切可能的方式節省能量，一天大部分的時間都在休息，體溫也維持得比多數哺乳動物低。","動作慢也讓樹懶安全。牠們的主要天敵，例如老鷹和美洲豹，靠視覺獵食，很快就會注意到動靜。一動也不動掛在樹枝間的樹懶很容易被忽略。此外，牠的毛上長著綠色的微小植物，幫助牠融入樹葉之中。對路過的獵食者來說，樹懶看起來只是樹的一部分。","有一個風險是樹懶躲不掉的。大約每週一次，牠們會一路爬到地面上排泄。科學家仍然不確定牠們為什麼要冒這麼危險的一趟，而不是安全地待在樹上。不管原因是什麼，樹懶提醒我們：在大自然裡，快不是唯一的致勝方式。"],252],[6,"沙子也會不夠用",["Sand seems to be everywhere, from beaches to deserts, so it is hard to imagine that the world could ever run short of it. [[1]] It goes into concrete, glass, roads, and even the chips inside our phones.","The demand has exploded along with the growth of cities. Every new apartment tower and highway requires enormous amounts of concrete, and concrete is mostly sand and small stones. [[2]]","One might think that deserts could supply all the sand we need. [[3]] Builders need the rough, sharp-edged sand found in rivers, lakes, and seas. This is why Dubai, a city surrounded by desert, has had to import sand from abroad for its tallest buildings.","Taking so much sand from rivers and coasts has serious consequences. Riverbanks collapse, fish lose their homes, and beaches wash away. [[4]] Researchers are now testing other materials, such as crushed glass and recycled concrete, to take the place of natural sand. Until these become widely available, however, the simplest answer is to build less wastefully and to treat sand as the limited resource it is."],["China alone is said to have used more cement between 2011 and 2013 than the United States did in the entire twentieth century.","Unfortunately, desert sand has been worn so smooth and round by the wind that its grains do not hold together well in concrete.","Yet after water, sand is the natural resource that humans use the most.","In some countries, the trade is so profitable that criminal groups have taken control of it.","Glass was first made in the Middle East more than four thousand years ago."],[2,0,1,3],["前一句說很難想像沙子會不夠，Yet 轉折指出沙子是用量僅次於水的自然資源；下一句 It 指 sand。","前一句說蓋房子和公路需要大量混凝土，這一句用中國的水泥用量當例子，說明需求有多大。","前一句說有人以為沙漠能供應沙子，Unfortunately 說明沙漠的沙太圓滑不能用；下一句說建築需要粗糙的沙。","前一句列出開採的後果，這一句再補一項：利潤太高，犯罪集團介入；the trade 指採沙買賣。"],[4,"玻璃的起源雖然和沙有關，但全文談的是沙的短缺，這句放進任何一格都偏離主題。"],["從海灘到沙漠，沙子似乎到處都是，所以很難想像這個世界有一天會缺沙。然而，沙子是人類用量僅次於水的自然資源。混凝土、玻璃、道路，甚至手機裡的晶片都要用到它。","隨著城市擴張，需求暴增。每一棟新的公寓大樓和每一條公路都需要大量的混凝土，而混凝土的主要成分就是沙子和小石頭。據說光是中國在 2011 到 2013 年間用掉的水泥，就比美國整個二十世紀用的還多。","有人可能會想，沙漠可以供應我們需要的所有沙子。可惜沙漠的沙被風磨得又滑又圓，在混凝土裡無法好好結合。建築需要的是河流、湖泊和海裡那種粗糙、稜角分明的沙。這就是為什麼被沙漠包圍的杜拜，蓋最高的建築時不得不從國外進口沙子。","從河流和海岸挖走這麼多沙，後果很嚴重。河岸崩塌，魚失去棲地，海灘被沖走。在某些國家，這門生意利潤太高，犯罪集團已經把持了它。研究者正在測試其他材料，例如碎玻璃和回收混凝土，來取代天然沙。不過在這些材料普及之前，最簡單的答案是蓋得不那麼浪費，並把沙子當成有限的資源來對待。"],264],[7,"摩斯電碼",["In 1825, Samuel Morse, an American painter, was working in Washington when a messenger on horseback brought him a letter saying that his wife was seriously ill. He rushed home at once. [[1]] Heartbroken, Morse began to dream of a way to send messages faster than any horse could run.","In the years that followed, Morse gradually put his brushes aside and experimented with electricity. Together with his partners, he built a machine that could send electric signals along a wire. [[2]] The most common letters were given the shortest codes; the letter E, for instance, is a single dot.","In 1844, Morse sent his first official message over a line between Washington and Baltimore. Within a few decades, telegraph wires crossed continents and ran under oceans. [[3]] Businesses, newspapers, and armies quickly came to depend on the new technology.","Morse code went to sea as well. The famous signal SOS, three dots, three dashes, and three dots, was chosen simply because it was easy to send and hard to mistake. [[4]] Although ships stopped using Morse code officially in 1999, radio lovers around the world still practice it today."],["Many of the survivors of the Titanic owed their lives to it.","News that had once taken weeks to arrive could now be delivered in minutes.","His paintings can still be seen in several American museums today.","By the time he arrived, however, she had already been buried.","To turn these signals into words, he created a code in which every letter was represented by a set of short and long signals, known as dots and dashes."],[3,4,1,0],["前一句說他立刻趕回家，By the time he arrived 接上抵達時的情況，however 帶出妻子已經下葬；下一句 Heartbroken 呼應。","前一句說機器能送出電的訊號，these signals 指這些訊號，這一句說明如何把訊號變成文字；下一句是代碼的例子。","前一句說電報線橫跨大陸和海洋，這一句說明結果：消息從幾週縮短到幾分鐘；下一句說各界開始依賴它。","前一句介紹求救訊號 SOS，it 指 SOS；鐵達尼號的生還者靠它獲救。"],[2,"這句講 Morse 的畫作，和電碼的發展無關；第二段以後主題都是電報，放不進去。"],["1825 年，美國畫家 Samuel Morse 正在華盛頓工作，一名騎馬的信差送來一封信，說他的妻子病重。他立刻趕回家。然而等他抵達時，她已經下葬了。傷心欲絕的 Morse 開始夢想找到一種比任何馬都快的傳訊方法。","接下來的幾年，Morse 逐漸放下畫筆，投入電的實驗。他和夥伴一起造出一臺能沿著電線傳送電訊號的機器。為了把這些訊號變成文字，他設計了一套代碼，每個字母由一組長短訊號（稱為點和劃）代表。最常用的字母代碼最短，例如字母 E 就只是一個點。","1844 年，Morse 透過華盛頓和巴爾的摩之間的線路發出第一則正式訊息。幾十年內，電報線橫跨各大陸，也鋪到海底。過去要花幾週才能送達的消息，現在幾分鐘就能傳到。企業、報社和軍隊很快就依賴起這項新技術。","摩斯電碼也用到了海上。著名的求救訊號 SOS——三點、三劃、三點——會被選中，只是因為它容易發送、不易弄錯。鐵達尼號的許多生還者就是靠它才活下來。雖然船隻在 1999 年正式停用摩斯電碼，世界各地的無線電愛好者至今仍在練習。"],263],[8,"同步發光的螢火蟲",["After dark along certain rivers in Southeast Asia, visitors can watch one of nature's strangest shows. Thousands of fireflies gather in the trees on the riverbank. [[1]]","For a long time, scientists refused to believe the reports of travelers who had seen the display. Some suggested that it was merely a trick of the observers' eyes. [[2]]","How do so many insects keep time without a leader? Researchers have found that each firefly has a kind of clock inside its body that tells it when to flash. When it sees a neighbor light up, it adjusts its own clock slightly. [[3]] The fireflies that flash together are all males, and flashing as one makes their signal far easier for females to see from a distance.","Sadly, the show is becoming harder to find. The trees where the fireflies gather are being cut down to make room for farms and houses. [[4]] Some villages have begun protecting their riverbanks and switching off lights at night, hoping that visitors will come and enjoy the display for years to come."],["Unlike most insects, fireflies taste bitter, so few birds are willing to eat them.","As thousands of fireflies keep making these small adjustments, the whole group gradually falls into the same rhythm.","Instead of blinking at random, they flash on and off at exactly the same moment, like lights on a Christmas tree.","Bright lights from nearby towns are another threat, since they prevent the insects from seeing one another's signals.","Only when the insects were filmed in the twentieth century was the phenomenon finally accepted as real."],[2,4,1,3],["前一句說成千上萬的螢火蟲聚在樹上，Instead of blinking at random 說明牠們不是亂閃，而是同時明滅。","前面說科學家長期不相信、以為是眼睛的錯覺，Only when ... 說明直到拍成影片才被接受。","前一句說每隻螢火蟲看到鄰居發光就微調自己的時鐘，these small adjustments 指這些微調，結果是整群同步。","前一句說樹被砍掉是一個威脅，another threat 補上第二個：城鎮的燈光；下一句的 switching off lights 呼應。"],[0,"螢火蟲味道苦、鳥不愛吃，和同步發光或面臨的威脅都無關。"],["入夜後，在東南亞某些河流沿岸，遊客可以看到大自然最奇特的表演之一。成千上萬的螢火蟲聚集在河岸的樹上。牠們不是隨意閃爍，而是在完全相同的瞬間一起亮、一起滅，就像聖誕樹上的燈。","很長一段時間，科學家不肯相信看過這幅景象的旅人所說的話。有些人認為那只是觀察者眼睛的錯覺。直到二十世紀這些昆蟲被拍成影片，這個現象才終於被承認是真的。","這麼多昆蟲沒有領袖，怎麼對得上拍子？研究者發現，每隻螢火蟲體內都有一種時鐘，告訴牠什麼時候發光。當牠看到鄰居亮起來，就會稍微調整自己的時鐘。成千上萬的螢火蟲不斷做這些微小的調整，整群就逐漸進入同一個節奏。一起發光的都是雄蟲，同步發光讓牠們的訊號更容易被遠處的雌蟲看見。","可惜這場表演越來越難見到。螢火蟲聚集的樹正被砍掉，騰出空間給農田和房屋。附近城鎮的明亮燈光是另一個威脅，因為燈光讓這些昆蟲看不見彼此的訊號。有些村莊已經開始保護河岸，並在夜間關燈，希望未來許多年遊客都能前來欣賞這幅景象。"],261],[9,"白噪音真的能助眠嗎",["Many people cannot fall asleep without the hum of an electric fan or an air conditioner. What they are enjoying is a form of “white noise.” [[1]] It sounds rather like rain falling or a radio that is not tuned to any station.","How can adding noise help a person sleep? The answer lies in the way the brain reacts to sound. [[2]] A door closing in a silent house can pull us out of sleep, while the same noise goes unnoticed in a room filled with a constant hum. White noise works like a blanket, covering the sharp sounds that would otherwise disturb us.","Parents have long put this idea to use. A crying baby often calms down at the sound of a hair dryer or a vacuum cleaner. [[3]] Today, machines and phone apps that play white noise are sold by the millions.","Doctors, however, advise a little caution. Played too loudly or too close to the ear, any noise can damage hearing, especially in young children. [[4]] For most of us, though, a quiet hum at low volume is a harmless way to a good night's rest."],["Sleep experts recommend that teenagers get at least eight hours of sleep a night.","The term refers to a steady sound that contains all the pitches the human ear can hear, mixed together at the same strength.","There is also a concern that people who use it every night may find it hard to sleep without it.","It is not loudness itself that wakes us, but a sudden change in the sounds around us.","Some experts believe such sounds remind the baby of the noises it heard before it was born."],[1,3,4,2],["前一句提出 white noise，The term refers to ... 是定義；下一句 It sounds like ... 接著描述。","前一句說答案在大腦對聲音的反應，這一句說明吵醒我們的是聲音的突然變化；下一句關門聲是例子。","前一句說嬰兒聽到吹風機的聲音會安靜下來，such sounds 指這些聲音，這一句解釋可能的原因。","前一句是第一個提醒：太大聲會傷聽力，also 帶出第二個擔憂：可能養成依賴；下一句 though 再回到正面。"],[0,"青少年該睡八小時和白噪音無關，放在任何一格都接不上。"],["很多人沒有電風扇或冷氣的嗡嗡聲就睡不著。他們享受的其實是一種「白噪音」。這個詞指的是一種穩定的聲音，包含人耳聽得到的所有音高，並以相同的強度混合在一起。它聽起來有點像下雨，或是沒有對準任何電臺的收音機。","增加噪音怎麼會幫助人入睡？答案在於大腦對聲音的反應方式。吵醒我們的不是音量本身，而是周圍聲音的突然變化。在寂靜的屋子裡，一扇門關上就能把我們吵醒；同樣的聲音在充滿持續嗡嗡聲的房間裡卻不會被注意到。白噪音就像一條毯子，蓋住那些原本會打擾我們的尖銳聲響。","父母早就把這個道理派上用場。哭鬧的嬰兒聽到吹風機或吸塵器的聲音常常就會安靜下來。有些專家認為，這類聲音讓嬰兒想起出生前聽到的聲響。如今，播放白噪音的機器和手機應用程式賣出了數百萬份。","不過醫生建議要稍微小心。任何噪音如果播得太大聲或離耳朵太近，都可能損害聽力，幼兒尤其如此。另外也有人擔心，每晚都使用的人可能會變得沒有它就睡不著。不過對我們大多數人來說，低音量的輕柔嗡嗡聲是通往一夜好眠的無害方法。"],277],[10,"左撇子的世界",["About one person in ten is left-handed, a proportion that has remained fairly stable throughout history. [[1]]","For centuries, being left-handed was treated as a fault that needed correcting. In many languages, the word for “left” is linked with bad luck or clumsiness, while “right” suggests what is proper and correct. [[2]] It was not until the second half of the twentieth century that schools in most countries gave up this practice.","Even today, daily life is full of small inconveniences for left-handers. Scissors, can openers, and school desks are generally designed for the right-handed majority. [[3]] Most left-handers simply learn to cope, often becoming skillful with both hands as a result.","In certain areas, however, being different pays off. [[4]] The explanation is simple. Since most athletes train mainly against right-handers, they have little experience in reading the moves of a left-handed opponent, who therefore enjoys the advantage of surprise. Being in the minority, it seems, is sometimes a strength."],["When a left-hander writes from left to right, the hand drags across the fresh ink and leaves a mess on the page.","Why some people prefer the left hand is still not fully understood, though both genes and early development seem to play a part.","Most people also have one eye that is stronger than the other.","Left-handed players are surprisingly common at the top level of sports such as tennis, boxing, and baseball.","Children who wrote with the left hand were often punished or forced to switch."],[1,4,0,3],["前一句說左撇子約占十分之一，這一句接著談成因仍不完全清楚，作為開頭段的收尾。","前一句說「左」在語言裡帶有負面意思，這一句說明實際的對待：被處罰或被迫改用右手；下一句 this practice 指這種做法。","前一句列出剪刀、開罐器等不便，這一句再舉寫字的例子；下一句說他們學會適應。","前一句說在某些領域「不一樣」反而有好處，這一句舉出運動的例子；下一句 The explanation 解釋原因。"],[2,"多數人有一隻眼睛較強，雖然看似相關，但全文談的是慣用手，這句沒有可以接的前後文。"],["大約每十個人就有一個是左撇子，這個比例在歷史上一直相當穩定。為什麼有些人偏好用左手，至今仍不完全清楚，不過基因和早期發育似乎都有影響。","幾個世紀以來，左撇子被當成需要矯正的毛病。在許多語言裡，「左」這個字和厄運或笨拙有關，「右」則代表正當和正確。用左手寫字的孩子常常被處罰，或被迫改用右手。直到二十世紀下半葉，多數國家的學校才放棄這種做法。","即使在今天，左撇子的日常生活仍充滿小小的不便。剪刀、開罐器和學校的課桌椅，通常是為占多數的右撇子設計的。由左往右寫字時，左撇子的手會拖過未乾的墨水，把紙面弄髒。大部分左撇子就是學著適應，往往因此兩隻手都很靈巧。","然而在某些領域，與眾不同反而有好處。在網球、拳擊和棒球這類運動的頂尖層級，左撇子選手出奇地常見。原因很簡單。多數運動員主要和右撇子對練，很少有機會判讀左撇子對手的動作，左撇子因此占了出其不意的優勢。看來身為少數，有時是一種力量。"],245],[11,"北投溫泉的興衰與重生",["Beitou, at the foot of the mountains north of Taipei, is one of Taiwan's best-known hot spring areas. [[1]] For the local indigenous people, the steaming valley was a mysterious place to be avoided.","The development of Beitou began under Japanese rule. In 1896, a Japanese businessman opened the first hot spring hotel there. [[2]] With a railway line linking it to Taipei, Beitou soon grew into a fashionable resort, where visitors bathed, dined, and listened to music.","After World War II, the hotels stayed busy, but the area's image suffered because of the nightlife businesses that grew up around them. [[3]] Many hotels closed their doors, and the old public bathhouse was abandoned and nearly forgotten.","Beitou's revival started with a group of schoolchildren. In the 1990s, a class and their teacher came across the ruined bathhouse during a field trip and began a campaign to save it. [[4]] The arrival of the MRT around the same time brought visitors back, and new hotels rose beside the old streets. Today, Beitou is once again a favorite escape for city residents and foreign tourists alike."],["When the government shut down these businesses in 1979, the number of visitors fell sharply.","Its hot water rises from an old volcano and carries a strong smell of sulfur.","Thanks to their efforts, the building was restored and reopened in 1998 as the Beitou Hot Spring Museum.","Many more followed, and in 1913 a large public bathhouse was built in the style of those in Japan.","Many of its hotels now offer private rooms where guests can bathe in hot spring water."],[1,3,0,2],["前一句介紹北投是溫泉區，Its hot water 說明泉水的來源和硫磺味；下一句 the steaming valley 延續描寫。","前一句說 1896 年開了第一家溫泉旅館，Many more followed 指更多旅館跟進，並帶出 1913 年的公共浴場。","前一句說形象因夜生活受損，these businesses 指那些行業；政府在 1979 年禁止後遊客大減，下一句說旅館關門。","前一句說師生發起搶救浴場的行動，Thanks to their efforts 接成果：1998 年修復成溫泉博物館。"],[4,"這句講現在的旅館提供私人湯屋，放進任何一格都會打斷前後文的時間順序。"],["北投位於臺北北邊的山腳下，是臺灣最知名的溫泉區之一。這裡的熱水來自一座古老的火山，帶著濃濃的硫磺味。對當地的原住民來說，這座冒著蒸氣的山谷是個應該避開的神祕地方。","北投的開發始於日治時期。1896 年，一位日本商人在這裡開了第一家溫泉旅館。更多旅館隨後跟進，1913 年還仿照日本的樣式蓋了一座大型公共浴場。有了連接臺北的鐵路，北投很快發展成時髦的度假勝地，遊客在這裡泡湯、用餐、聽音樂。","二戰之後，旅館生意依舊興隆，但這一帶的形象因為周邊興起的夜生活行業而受損。1979 年政府禁止這些行業之後，遊客人數大幅下滑。許多旅館關門，老公共浴場也遭到廢棄，幾乎被遺忘。","北投的重生始於一群小學生。1990 年代，一個班級和老師在校外教學時偶然發現了破敗的浴場，發起搶救行動。多虧他們的努力，這棟建築修復完成，並在 1998 年以北投溫泉博物館的身分重新開放。捷運差不多在同一時期通車，把遊客帶了回來，新的旅館也在老街旁蓋起。如今，北投再度成為都市居民和外國觀光客最愛的休憩去處。"],265],[12,"環法賽的黃衫",["The Tour de France is the most famous bicycle race in the world. Every July, about 180 riders cover some 3,500 kilometers in three weeks, climbing high mountains and racing through villages lined with cheering crowds. [[1]]","In late 1902, a struggling French sports paper called L'Auto was losing readers to its rival. One of its young reporters suggested organizing a bicycle race around the whole country, with daily reports appearing only in L'Auto. [[2]] The rival paper eventually went out of business, and the Tour became an annual event.","As the race grew, spectators complained that they could not tell which rider was in the lead. [[3]] They chose yellow, the color of the paper on which L'Auto was printed. The first rider to put it on, Eugène Christophe, was not pleased: other riders laughed and called him a canary.","No one laughs at the yellow jersey now. It has become the greatest prize in cycling, and riders will suffer almost anything to wear it for even a single day. [[4]] Yet none of them carries the same glory. More than a century later, the color of an old newspaper still decides who is king of the road."],["To solve this problem, the organizers decided in 1919 that the leader would wear a jersey of a special color.","Bicycles at that time were much heavier than the ones used by today's riders.","The plan worked even better than expected: during the first race in 1903, the paper's sales more than doubled.","Other jerseys have since been added, including a green one for the best sprinter and a spotted one for the best climber.","Surprisingly, this great sporting event began as a trick to sell newspapers."],[4,2,0,3],["前面介紹環法賽的規模，Surprisingly 轉到它的起源：為了賣報紙；下一段正是這個故事。","前一句是記者的提議，The plan 指這個計畫，這一句說結果銷量翻倍；下一句對手倒閉是後續。","前一句說觀眾分不出誰領先，this problem 指這個問題，解決方法是讓領先者穿特殊顏色；下一句 They chose yellow 接著說。","前面講黃衫的地位，Other jerseys 補充後來加的綠衫和圓點衫；下一句 none of them 指這些車衣。"],[1,"當時的腳踏車比較重，和黃衫的由來無關，放進任何一格都會偏離主題。"],["環法自由車賽是世界上最著名的自行車賽。每年七月，大約 180 名車手在三週內騎完約 3,500 公里，爬上高山，穿過兩旁站滿歡呼群眾的村莊。令人驚訝的是，這項體壇盛事一開始只是為了賣報紙的手段。","1902 年底，一家經營困難的法國體育報 L'Auto 的讀者不斷流失到對手那邊。報社一位年輕記者建議舉辦一場繞行全國的自行車賽，每日戰報只刊登在 L'Auto。這個計畫的效果比預期還好：1903 年第一屆比賽期間，報紙銷量增加了一倍以上。對手報紙最後關門大吉，環法賽則成為年度賽事。","隨著比賽規模變大，觀眾抱怨分不出哪位車手領先。為了解決這個問題，主辦單位在 1919 年決定讓領先者穿上一件特殊顏色的車衣。他們選了黃色，也就是 L'Auto 印報紙用的紙張顏色。第一位穿上它的車手 Eugène Christophe 並不高興：其他車手笑他，叫他金絲雀。","現在沒有人會嘲笑黃衫了。它已經成為自行車界最大的榮耀，車手願意忍受幾乎任何痛苦，只為了穿上它一天。後來又增加了其他車衣，包括頒給最佳衝刺車手的綠衫和頒給最佳爬坡車手的圓點衫。但沒有一件有同樣的榮光。一個多世紀之後，一份舊報紙的顏色依然決定誰是公路之王。"],283]],"MX":[[1,"F","校服存廢（論壇）","Most high schools in Taiwan have relaxed their uniform rules in recent years, and some students are now asking whether uniforms should be dropped altogether. The student council of one school has opened an online forum to collect opinions. Here are some of the posts.",[["A","Kevin","I'm all for getting rid of them. What I wear has nothing to do with how well I study. Let us dress the way we like. Nobody has ever proved that a uniform raises test scores."],["B","Mia","Uniforms save me time every morning. I don't have to stand in front of my closet wondering what to put on. Those extra ten minutes of sleep mean a lot to me."],["C","Leo","Without uniforms, clothes would turn into a competition. Not every family can afford brand-name outfits, and some kids would end up feeling left out."],["D","Sandy","Our uniform is made of thick fabric that doesn't breathe, and in summer it sticks to my skin. Before we argue about keeping it or not, could the school at least switch to a lighter material?"],["E","Ray","Uniforms make students easy to recognize. When a stranger wandered onto campus last year, the guards spotted him right away. I'd rather keep that protection."],["F","Tina","Expressing yourself is part of growing up. Forcing everyone to look identical kills creativity. At sixteen, we are old enough to decide what suits us."],["G","Oscar","Honestly, I don't care either way. But if we keep them, students should at least have a say in the design. The current one looks as if it was chosen fifty years ago."],["H","Joyce","I feel proud whenever I put on our school jacket at competitions. It gives me a sense of belonging that ordinary clothes never could."],["I","Daniel","I've worn the same white shirt and black trousers for five years. I can't wait for the day when I can finally pick my own clothes for school. Uniforms belong in the past."],["J","Ms. Fang","As a teacher, I've seen students laughed at for what they wear on casual-dress days. A uniform puts everyone on the same level, rich or poor, and lets students concentrate on what really matters."]],["Students who support uniforms say that they can take __47__ in their school when wearing them, while those who oppose uniforms believe that young people need room for creativity and personal __48__.",[[["pride"],"proud","take pride in 是固定用法，要把 Joyce 留言裡的形容詞 proud 改成名詞 pride。belonging 不能接在 take ... in 裡。"],[["expression"],"Expressing","personal 後面要接名詞，把 Tina 留言裡的 Expressing 改成 expression。"]]],["From (A) to (J) in the above forum, which ONES are in favor of keeping school uniforms?",[],"BCEHJ","B 省時間、C 避免比較、E 安全、H 歸屬感、J 讓學生不分貧富，都支持保留。D 只抱怨布料、沒有表態，G 表示沒意見，A、F、I 反對。"],["Which phrase in the forum means “not included in a group”?",["left out","feeling left out","feel left out"],"Leo 說買不起名牌的學生會 feel left out，就是「被排除在外」。"],["近年來臺灣多數高中已放寬制服規定，有些學生開始問：制服是不是乾脆取消？某校學生會開了線上論壇蒐集意見，以下是部分留言。","A. Kevin：我完全贊成取消。穿什麼和書讀得好不好無關，讓我們照自己喜歡的方式穿吧。從來沒有人證明過制服能提高考試成績。","B. Mia：制服讓我每天早上省下時間，不必站在衣櫃前煩惱要穿什麼。多睡的那十分鐘對我很重要。","C. Leo：沒有制服，穿著就會變成比賽。不是每個家庭都買得起名牌，有些孩子最後會覺得被排擠。","D. Sandy：我們的制服布料厚又不透氣，夏天會黏在皮膚上。在爭論要不要保留之前，學校能不能至少先換成比較輕薄的材質？","E. Ray：制服讓學生容易辨認。去年有陌生人闖進校園，警衛一眼就發現了。我寧可保留這層保護。","F. Tina：表達自我是成長的一部分。強迫每個人看起來一模一樣會扼殺創意。我們十六歲了，夠大了，可以決定什麼適合自己。","G. Oscar：老實說我都可以。但如果要保留，學生至少應該對設計有發言權。現在這套看起來像是五十年前選的。","H. Joyce：每次比賽穿上校隊外套我都覺得驕傲，那種歸屬感是便服給不了的。","I. Daniel：同樣的白襯衫和黑長褲我已經穿了五年。我等不及可以自己挑衣服上學的那一天。制服屬於過去。","J. Ms. Fang：身為老師，我看過學生在便服日因為穿著被嘲笑。制服讓每個人站在同一個水平上，不分貧富，也讓學生專心在真正重要的事情上。"]],[2,"P","意外的發明：微波爐與盤尼西林（雙文）","Some of the most useful inventions and discoveries were never planned. The following two stories show how a careful observer can turn an accident into a breakthrough.",[["A","The Microwave Oven","In 1945, Percy Spencer, an American engineer who had never finished elementary school, was testing a device that produces microwaves for radar. He suddenly noticed that the chocolate bar in his pocket had melted. Curious rather than annoyed, he placed some popcorn kernels near the device, and they popped. Spencer realized that microwaves could heat food quickly, and his company soon built the first microwave oven. It was nearly as tall as a refrigerator and weighed over 300 kilograms, so only restaurants and ships could use it. Smaller, cheaper models did not appear in ordinary kitchens until the late 1960s. Today, microwave ovens can be found in the vast majority of American homes."],["B","Penicillin","In 1928, Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming returned from a vacation to find that mold had grown on one of the dishes of bacteria he had left on his desk. Most people would have been annoyed by the mess. He was about to throw the dish away when he saw that the bacteria around the mold had been killed. Fleming studied the mold and named the substance it produced penicillin, but he could not make enough of it to treat patients. More than a decade later, a team at Oxford University found a way to purify the drug and produce it in large amounts. It went on to save countless soldiers in World War II. In 1945, Fleming and two of the Oxford scientists shared the Nobel Prize. Before penicillin, even a small cut could lead to a deadly infection. Doctors now warn, however, that using such drugs too often makes bacteria harder to kill."]],["Percy Spencer's __47__ about a melted chocolate bar led to a new way of cooking. As for penicillin, it did not become a common medicine until its mass __48__ was made possible by other scientists.",[[["curiosity"],"Curious","所有格 Spencer's 後面要接名詞，Curious 改成 curiosity。"],[["production"],"produce","its mass 後面要接名詞，動詞 produce 改成 production（mass production 大量生產）。"]]],["From (A) to (F) below, choose the ONES that are true for both Percy Spencer and Alexander Fleming.",["He made his finding by accident.","He was honored with a Nobel Prize.","It took many years before his finding benefited the general public.","He had received little education at school.","He alone turned the finding into a useful product.","He noticed something unexpected and looked into it."],"ACF","兩人的發現都出於意外（A），發現之後都過了很多年才普及（C），也都注意到意外現象並進一步查看（F）。B 只有 Fleming，D 只有 Spencer，E 兩人都不是。"],["Which word in the story of penicillin means “to remove unwanted substances from something”?",["purify","to purify"],"牛津團隊找到方法 purify the drug，purify 就是「提純、淨化」。"],["有些最實用的發明和發現從來不在計畫之內。下面兩個故事說明，細心的觀察者如何把意外變成突破。","微波爐：1945 年，從未讀完小學的美國工程師 Percy Spencer 正在測試一個為雷達產生微波的裝置，忽然發現口袋裡的巧克力棒融化了。他不覺得惱怒，反而感到好奇，於是把一些爆米花玉米粒放在裝置旁邊，結果玉米粒爆開了。Spencer 明白微波可以快速加熱食物，他的公司很快就造出第一臺微波爐。它幾乎和冰箱一樣高，重量超過 300 公斤，只有餐廳和船隻用得上。體積較小、價格較低的機型，要到 1960 年代末期才進入一般家庭的廚房。如今，絕大多數的美國家庭都有微波爐。","盤尼西林：1928 年，蘇格蘭科學家 Alexander Fleming 度假回來，發現留在桌上的一個細菌培養皿長了黴菌。大多數人看到這團髒亂只會覺得惱火。他正要把它丟掉時，注意到黴菌周圍的細菌都被殺死了。Fleming 研究了這種黴菌，把它產生的物質命名為盤尼西林，卻無法製造出足夠的量來治療病人。十多年後，牛津大學的一個團隊找到方法提純這種藥並大量生產，它後來在第二次世界大戰中救了無數士兵。1945 年，Fleming 和其中兩位牛津科學家共同獲得諾貝爾獎。在盤尼西林出現之前，連小小的割傷都可能引發致命的感染。不過醫生現在警告，太常使用這類藥物會讓細菌更難殺死。"]],[3,"L","社團博覽會（列舉）","Every September, Hillside High School holds a club fair in the gym so that new students can find a place to belong. With more than thirty clubs to choose from, making a decision is not easy. Students may visit as many booths as they like before signing up. The following six clubs are among the most talked-about this year.",[["A","Circuit Breakers","This robotics club meets twice a week to design, build, and program machines for national contests. No experience is needed. “We teach beginners everything from scratch,” says club president Wendy Kao. “All you need is patience.”"],["B","Green Thumbs","Members look after the vegetable garden on the school roof and sell what they grow at the school fair. “You get your hands dirty, but nothing tastes better than a tomato you planted yourself,” says member Howard Lin."],["C","Page Turners","Once a month, this book club picks a novel and invites its members to argue about it over tea. Last term's most heated debate was over a detective story whose ending nobody could agree on. It also runs a shelf in the library where students can exchange used books for free."],["D","Sound Lab","From recording a song to editing a podcast, Sound Lab teaches students how audio is produced. The club owns a small studio, and its weekly show is broadcast during lunch break. Members take turns hosting it."],["E","Helping Paws","Every other Saturday, members volunteer at a local animal shelter, where they walk dogs, clean cages, and help find homes for abandoned pets. “The work is tiring, but the animals are counting on us,” says founder Ruby Chen."],["F","Stage Door","The drama club puts on two plays a year. Besides acting, members can try their hand at costume design, lighting, and stage management. Tickets for last year's final show sold out in two days."]],["The clubs at Hillside High School suit students with different interests. Circuit Breakers, for example, welcomes those who are not __47__ in robotics, as long as they are __48__ enough to learn step by step.",[[["experienced"],"experience","be 動詞後面要接形容詞，把 Circuit Breakers 介紹裡的名詞 experience 改成 experienced（be experienced in 對…有經驗）。"],[["patient"],"patience","are ... enough 中間要放形容詞，把 patience 改成 patient。"]]],["Three new students are looking for a club. (1) Amber wants to learn how to make her own radio program. (2) Brian loves growing plants. (3) Cindy hopes to do something for homeless cats and dogs. Which clubs should they join? 請選出社團名稱前的英文大寫字母。",[],"BDE","(1) 做廣播節目 → Sound Lab（D）；(2) 種植物 → Green Thumbs（B）；(3) 幫助流浪貓狗 → Helping Paws（E）。"],["Which phrase is used by Wendy Kao to mean “from the very beginning”?",["from scratch"],"Wendy Kao 說 teach beginners everything from scratch，from scratch 就是「從頭開始」。"],["每年九月，Hillside 高中都會在體育館舉辦社團博覽會，讓新生找到自己的歸屬。社團超過三十個，要做決定並不容易。學生報名前可以盡量逛各個攤位。以下六個社團是今年討論度最高的。","A. Circuit Breakers：這個機器人社每週聚會兩次，為全國競賽設計、組裝機器並寫程式，不需要任何經驗。社長 Wendy Kao 說：「我們從頭教新手所有東西，你只需要有耐心。」","B. Green Thumbs：社員照顧學校屋頂的菜園，並在園遊會販賣自己種的蔬果。社員 Howard Lin 說：「手會弄髒，但沒有什麼比自己種的番茄更好吃。」","C. Page Turners：這個讀書會每個月選一本小說，邀社員邊喝茶邊辯論。上學期最激烈的一場辯論，是一本大家對結局各有看法的偵探小說。它也在圖書館經營一個書架，讓學生免費交換二手書。","D. Sound Lab：從錄一首歌到剪輯 podcast，Sound Lab 教學生聲音是怎麼製作出來的。社團有一間小錄音室，每週的節目在午休時間播出。節目由社員輪流主持。","E. Helping Paws：每隔一週的星期六，社員到當地動物收容所當志工，遛狗、清理籠子，並幫被遺棄的寵物找家。創辦人 Ruby Chen 說：「工作很累，但動物們都指望著我們。」","F. Stage Door：戲劇社每年演出兩齣戲。除了演戲，社員還能嘗試服裝設計、燈光和舞臺管理。去年最後一場演出的票兩天就賣完了。"]],[4,"F","共享電動滑板車（論壇）","Shared electric scooters have appeared on the streets of many cities around the world. The city of Riverton introduced them two years ago, and the city council must now decide whether to continue the program. Residents were invited to leave comments on the city's website. More than a thousand people responded in the first week.",[["A","Nora","I ride one every day from the train station to my office. It beats waiting twenty minutes for a bus. Without the scooters, I would probably go back to driving."],["B","Victor","They are scattered all over the sidewalks. My grandmother, who uses a wheelchair, often finds her way blocked. Last week she had to wait for a stranger to move three of them."],["C","Ella","A total ban goes too far. Set up parking zones and fine riders who leave scooters anywhere else, and I'd be happy to see them stay."],["D","Sam","Riders zip past pedestrians at 25 kilometers an hour, mostly without helmets. Two people on my street have ended up in the hospital. It simply isn't safe. Enough is enough."],["E","Grace","Every scooter trip that replaces a car ride means cleaner air. The environmental benefit is obvious. Isn't that what the city has been asking us to do for years?"],["F","Hugo","Cleaner air? Trucks drive around every night collecting the scooters for charging, and most scooters are thrown away within two years. I'm not convinced."],["G","Ivy","Fine by me, as long as riders must be over 18 and the speed is limited in crowded areas."],["H","Paul","Tourists love them. Shop owners along the riverside say business has picked up since the scooters arrived. Visitors can now reach the old town in five minutes instead of walking for half an hour."],["I","Mrs. Duncan","I own a small bike rental shop near the station. Since these scooters showed up, I've lost a third of my customers. The city is helping a big company at the expense of local businesses."],["J","Theo","I'd support the program if the company were made to pay for removing abandoned scooters and for accident insurance. Right now, taxpayers are covering those costs."]],["On the whole, the comments about shared scooters mainly focus on three issues: how convenient they are, the __47__ of riders and pedestrians, and their effect on the __48__.",[[["safety"],"safe","the ... of 中間要放名詞，把 Sam 留言裡的 safe 改成 safety。"],[["environment"],"environmental","the 後面要接名詞，把 Grace 留言裡的 environmental 改成 environment。"]]],["From (A) to (J) in the above comments, which ONES show that the writers can accept shared scooters only under certain conditions?",[],"CGJ","C 要求設停車區並開罰，G 要求限年齡和限速，J 要求業者負擔清運和保險，都是有條件接受。A、E、H 支持，B、D、F、I 反對或質疑。"],["Which phrase in the comments means “has improved”?",["has picked up","picked up"],"Paul 說 business has picked up，pick up 在這裡是「（生意）好轉」。"],["共享電動滑板車已經出現在世界各地許多城市的街頭。Riverton 市兩年前引進，市議會現在必須決定是否繼續這項計畫，並請居民到市府網站留言。第一週就有超過一千人回應。","A. Nora：我每天騎它從火車站到辦公室，比等二十分鐘的公車好多了。沒有滑板車的話，我大概會回去開車。","B. Victor：它們散落在人行道上到處都是。我奶奶坐輪椅，常常發現路被擋住。上星期她還得等陌生人幫忙搬開三臺。","C. Ella：全面禁止太過頭了。劃設停車區，對亂停的人開罰，我就樂見它們留下來。","D. Sam：騎的人以時速 25 公里從行人身邊呼嘯而過，大多沒戴安全帽。我家那條街已經有兩個人進了醫院。這根本不安全，夠了。","E. Grace：每一趟取代開車的滑板車行程，都代表更乾淨的空氣。對環境的好處很明顯。這不就是市政府多年來一直要我們做的事嗎？","F. Hugo：更乾淨的空氣？每天晚上都有卡車到處收滑板車回去充電，而且大多數滑板車不到兩年就被丟棄。我不相信。","G. Ivy：我可以接受，只要規定騎乘者必須滿 18 歲，並且在人多的地方限速。","H. Paul：觀光客很愛。河岸一帶的店家說，滑板車來了之後生意變好了。遊客現在五分鐘就能到舊城區，不必走半小時。","I. Mrs. Duncan：我在車站附近開一家小小的腳踏車出租店。這些滑板車出現之後，我少了三分之一的客人。市政府是在犧牲在地商家來幫一家大公司。","J. Theo：如果規定業者負擔清運棄置滑板車的費用和意外保險，我就支持這個計畫。現在這些成本是納稅人在付。"]],[5,"P","邊動邊睡的動物：海豚與軍艦鳥（雙文）","Sleep leaves an animal defenseless, yet no animal can go without it. For creatures that must keep moving to stay alive, this is a serious problem. Some species have evolved remarkable ways to rest without ever coming to a stop. Studies on dolphins and frigatebirds show how this is possible.",[["A","Bottlenose Dolphins","Dolphins must rise to the surface to breathe, and they have to watch out for sharks. If they slept as deeply as humans do, they would drown or be eaten. Their solution is to shut down only one half of the brain at a time. The other half stays alert, and the eye on the opposite side remains open. After about two hours, the two halves switch roles. In this way, a dolphin can swim slowly, come up for air, and keep watch around the clock. Baby dolphins and their mothers have even been observed to stay active day and night during the first month after birth."],["B","Great Frigatebirds","Frigatebirds can fly over the ocean for up to two months without landing. Because their feathers are not waterproof, they cannot rest on the water as other seabirds do. Scientists long suspected that the birds slept in the air, and a 2016 study finally proved it. Small devices attached to the birds' heads recorded their brain activity during flight. The birds did sleep, sometimes with one half of the brain and sometimes with both, but only in bursts of about twelve seconds. In total, they slept for less than an hour a day, mostly while gliding upward on rising air. Even so, they showed no sign of flying badly. Back on land, they slept for more than twelve hours a day."]],["As a result of __47__, dolphins and frigatebirds are able to rest while on the move. Sleeping with half of the brain allows a dolphin to keep __48__ and watching for danger at the same time.",[[["evolution"],"evolved","As a result of 後面要接名詞，把前言裡的動詞 evolved 改成 evolution。"],[["breathing","swimming","moving"],"breathe","keep 後面接 V-ing，而且要和 watching 對稱，breathe 改成 breathing；把 swim 改成 swimming，或用前言裡的 moving 也可以。"]]],["From (A) to (F) below, choose the ONES that are true for both dolphins and frigatebirds.",["They are able to rest one half of the brain at a time.","They sleep only during the day.","They are able to sleep without coming to a stop.","They sleep for only a few seconds each time.","They sometimes sleep with both halves of the brain at the same time.","They sleep much longer when they are on land."],"AC","兩種動物都能只讓半邊大腦休息（A），也都能不停下來就睡覺（C）。D、E、F 只符合軍艦鳥（海豚一次只關閉半邊大腦，所以 E 不符合），B 文章沒有說。"],["What makes it impossible for frigatebirds to rest on the surface of the ocean?",["Their feathers are not waterproof.","their feathers are not waterproof","because their feathers are not waterproof","their feathers aren't waterproof","because their feathers aren't waterproof","the feathers are not waterproof","feathers that are not waterproof","not waterproof feathers"],"原文：Because their feathers are not waterproof, they cannot rest on the water。"],["睡眠讓動物毫無防備，但沒有動物可以不睡覺。對必須不停移動才能活命的生物來說，這是個嚴重的問題。有些物種演化出驚人的方法，不必停下來也能休息。對海豚和軍艦鳥的研究說明了這如何辦到。","瓶鼻海豚：海豚必須浮上水面呼吸，還得提防鯊魚。如果牠們睡得像人類一樣沉，就會溺死或被吃掉。牠們的解決方法是一次只關閉半邊大腦，另一半保持警覺，對側的眼睛也睜著。大約兩小時後，兩邊交換。這樣一來，海豚可以全天候緩慢游動、浮上來換氣並保持警戒。研究者甚至觀察到，海豚寶寶和母親在出生後的第一個月日夜都保持活動。","大軍艦鳥：軍艦鳥可以在海上連續飛行長達兩個月不降落。因為羽毛不防水，牠們不能像其他海鳥那樣在水面上休息。科學家長久以來懷疑這種鳥在空中睡覺，2016 年的一項研究終於證實了。裝在鳥頭上的小型裝置記錄了牠們飛行時的腦部活動。牠們確實會睡，有時用半邊大腦，有時兩邊一起，但每次只有大約十二秒。牠們一天總共睡不到一小時，多半是在乘著上升氣流向上滑翔的時候。即使如此，牠們的飛行看不出受到影響。回到陸地後，牠們一天睡超過十二小時。"]],[6,"L","森林步道指南（列舉）","Pine Ridge Forest Park, two hours from the city, offers trails for every kind of walker. Before you set off, read the following guide and pick the trail that suits you best. All trails start from the visitor center, where maps are available for free.",[["A","Fern Loop","This 1.2-kilometer boardwalk is completely flat, so wheelchairs and baby strollers can pass easily. Signs along the way introduce the ferns and mosses that cover the forest floor. Allow about thirty minutes. Benches are placed every two hundred meters for those who need a rest."],["B","Mirror Lake Trail","A gentle four-kilometer walk leads to a lake that reflects the surrounding mountains like a mirror. There are picnic tables by the water, which makes this trail a favorite with families. It gets crowded on weekends, so arrive early. Lucky visitors may spot wild ducks on the water."],["C","Eagle Peak Trail","The most difficult trail in the park climbs nine kilometers over steep, rocky ground and takes about six hours. A permit is required, and beginners should not attempt it. The view above the clouds, however, is worth every drop of sweat."],["D","Old Railway Path","This five-kilometer path follows a railway that once carried logs down the mountain. Walkers pass through two tunnels and cross a wooden bridge before reaching the old station, now a small museum on the history of the logging industry. Bring a flashlight. The tunnels are dark and the ground inside can be wet."],["E","Firefly Valley","Open only on evenings from April to June, this short trail is visited on guided tours. Flashlights and phone screens must stay off so that the fireflies are not disturbed. Reservations are necessary. Each tour is limited to twenty people and lasts about an hour."]],["The trails of Pine Ridge Forest Park vary greatly in length and __47__. Besides enjoying the natural scenery, visitors can also explore a site of __48__ interest along the Old Railway Path.",[[["difficulty"],"difficult","和 length 對稱，要用名詞，把 Eagle Peak Trail 介紹裡的 difficult 改成 difficulty。"],[["historical","historic"],"history","of ... interest 中間要放形容詞，把 history 改成 historical（historic 也可以）。"]]],["Three visitors are planning a trip to the park. (1) Mr. Ho, who moves around in a wheelchair, wants an easy walk. (2) Peggy hopes to see insects glowing in the dark. (3) Tony is curious about how people used to make a living from the forest. Which trails should they choose? 請選出步道名稱前的英文大寫字母。",[],"ADE","(1) 輪椅可通行 → Fern Loop（A）；(2) 看螢火蟲 → Firefly Valley（E）；(3) 了解伐木業的歷史 → Old Railway Path（D）。"],["Which phrase in the guide means “deserving all the hard effort”?",["worth every drop of sweat","is worth every drop of sweat"],"Eagle Peak Trail 的介紹說山頂的景色 worth every drop of sweat，每一滴汗都值得。"],["Pine Ridge 森林公園距離市區兩小時，各種程度的人都能找到合適的步道。出發前請先讀下面的指南，挑一條最適合你的。所有步道都從遊客中心出發，那裡有免費地圖。","A. Fern Loop：這條 1.2 公里的木棧道完全平坦，輪椅和嬰兒車都能輕鬆通行。沿途的解說牌介紹覆蓋森林地面的蕨類和苔蘚。大約需要三十分鐘。每隔兩百公尺設有長椅，供需要休息的人使用。","B. Mirror Lake Trail：平緩的四公里步道通往一座湖，湖面像鏡子一樣映出四周的群山。水邊有野餐桌，所以很受家庭歡迎。週末人多，請早點到。運氣好的遊客可能會看到水面上的野鴨。","C. Eagle Peak Trail：園內最難的步道，在陡峭多石的地面上爬升九公里，約需六小時。需要申請許可，新手不宜嘗試。不過雲海之上的景色，每一滴汗都值得。","D. Old Railway Path：這條五公里的步道沿著昔日運送木材下山的鐵路前進。遊客會穿過兩座隧道、走過一座木橋，最後抵達舊車站，現在是介紹伐木業歷史的小博物館。請帶手電筒。隧道裡很暗，地面可能溼滑。","E. Firefly Valley：只在四月到六月的晚上開放，須參加導覽。手電筒和手機螢幕必須關閉，以免打擾螢火蟲。必須預約。每場導覽限二十人，約一小時。"]],[7,"F","夜市全面無現金（論壇）","More and more shops are going cashless. In the town of Greenfield, the night market committee has announced that its vendors will stop accepting cash next year. A local news website asked readers what they think of the plan.",[["A","Rita","About time! Paying with my phone takes two seconds. It's so convenient that I no longer dig through my bag for coins. The line at my favorite stall moves twice as fast now."],["B","Mr. Hsu","I've sold noodles here for thirty years. Half of my customers are elderly people who have never used a smartphone. I can't just turn them away."],["C","Dylan","I lost my wallet twice last year. With mobile payment there is nothing to steal, and if my phone goes missing, I can lock it from another device. Cash, once gone, is gone for good."],["D","Fiona","When I pay by phone, I don't feel the money leaving, so I end up spending far more than I planned. Cash keeps me under control."],["E","Marco","What happens when the network is down or my battery dies? During the last typhoon, the power was out for a whole day. Cash is simply more reliable."],["F","Jenny","Who knows how many hands those bills and coins have passed through? I'd rather not touch them, especially while I'm eating. Vendors who handle money and food with the same hands worry me even more."],["G","Owen","Every payment leaves a record. Companies know what I buy, where, and when. I'm not comfortable with that."],["H","Sara","Why not both? I use my phone most of the time, but I always keep a few bills in my pocket just in case."],["I","Leon","I run a fried chicken stall. Counting coins at midnight used to take me an hour, and I was always nervous carrying a bag of cash home. Now every sale goes straight into my account."],["J","Wendy","My ten-year-old gets his allowance in coins. How is he supposed to buy a snack if he has no phone and no bank account?"]],["Readers who welcome the plan stress the __47__ and security of mobile payment. Those who have doubts, on the other hand, are concerned about elderly customers, personal information, and the __48__ of the technology when there is no power or signal.",[[["convenience"],"convenient","the ... and security 要用名詞，把 Rita 留言裡的 convenient 改成 convenience。"],[["reliability"],"reliable","the ... of 中間要放名詞，把 Marco 留言裡的 reliable 改成 reliability。"]]],["From (A) to (J) in the above comments, which ONES point out a disadvantage of mobile payment?",[],"BDEGJ","B 年長顧客不會用，D 容易花太多，E 沒電沒網路就不能用，G 消費紀錄被掌握，J 小孩沒有手機和帳戶。A、C、F、I 講優點，H 主張兩種並用。"],["Which phrase in the comments means “refuse to serve them”?",["turn them away","turn away"],"Mr. Hsu 說他不能 turn them away，turn someone away 是「把人拒於門外」。"],["越來越多商店不收現金。在 Greenfield 鎮，夜市管理委員會宣布攤商明年起停收現金。當地一個新聞網站請讀者談談對這項計畫的看法。","A. Rita：早該這樣了！用手機付款只要兩秒，方便到我再也不用在包包裡翻零錢。我最愛的那一攤現在排隊快了一倍。","B. Mr. Hsu：我在這裡賣麵三十年了。我的客人有一半是從沒用過智慧型手機的長輩，我總不能把他們拒於門外。","C. Dylan：我去年掉了兩次皮夾。用行動支付就沒有東西可偷，手機不見了也能從別的裝置鎖住。現金一旦不見，就永遠找不回來了。","D. Fiona：用手機付款時感覺不到錢離開，結果花得比計畫多很多。現金讓我有所節制。","E. Marco：網路斷線或手機沒電怎麼辦？上次颱風停電一整天。現金就是比較可靠。","F. Jenny：誰知道那些鈔票和硬幣經過多少人的手？我寧可不碰，尤其是吃東西的時候。用同一雙手拿錢又拿食物的攤商更讓我擔心。","G. Owen：每一筆付款都會留下紀錄。企業知道我買了什麼、在哪裡買、什麼時候買，我覺得不自在。","H. Sara：為什麼不兩種都收？我大部分時間用手機，但口袋裡總會放幾張鈔票以防萬一。","I. Leon：我開炸雞攤。以前半夜數零錢要花一個小時，提著一袋現金回家也總是提心吊膽。現在每一筆收入都直接進我的帳戶。","J. Wendy：我十歲的兒子拿的零用錢是硬幣。他沒有手機也沒有銀行帳戶，要怎麼買點心？"]],[8,"P","南極點競賽：阿蒙森與史考特（雙文）","In the early twentieth century, the South Pole was one of the last places on Earth that no human had reached. In 1911, two expeditions set out to be the first. Both leaders were experienced and determined, and both had prepared for years. The different choices their leaders made led to very different endings.",[["A","Roald Amundsen","Amundsen, a Norwegian explorer, had learned from Arctic peoples how to survive in extreme cold. Following their example, his team of five wore loose fur clothing and traveled on skis, with 52 dogs pulling the sleds. Amundsen marked his food stores with long rows of flags so that they could be found even in a snowstorm. He also planned to feed some of the dogs to the others along the way, a cruel but practical decision. On December 14, 1911, the Norwegians reached the Pole and planted their flag. Ninety-nine days after setting out, all five men returned to their base in good health."],["B","Robert Falcon Scott","Scott, a British naval officer, placed his trust in motor sleds and ponies, but both failed. The engines broke down in the cold, and the ponies sank into the deep snow. For most of the journey, the men had to drag the heavy sleds themselves. Unlike Amundsen, Scott also stopped to collect rock samples for science along the way. When he and four companions finally arrived at the Pole on January 17, 1912, the Norwegian flag was already there. Exhausted and short of food, all five died on the way back. Months later, a search party found Scott's diary beside his body, only about 18 kilometers from a large store of supplies. In his last message, he wrote that he did not regret the journey."]],["Amundsen made good use of what he knew about __47__ in polar regions, and his journey went smoothly. Scott's team, by contrast, was greatly weakened by the __48__ of its motor sleds and ponies.",[[["survival"],"survive","介系詞 about 後面要接名詞，把 survive 改成 survival。"],[["failure"],"failed","the ... of 中間要放名詞，把 failed 改成 failure。"]]],["From (A) to (F) below, choose the ONES that are true for both Amundsen and Scott.",["He was one of five men who made it to the South Pole.","He used animals to help carry supplies.","He carried out scientific work during the journey.","He set out for the South Pole in 1911.","He learned his skills from the peoples of the Arctic.","He returned safely to his base."],"ABD","兩隊都是五個人抵達（A），都用了動物：狗和小馬（B），兩支探險隊都在 1911 年出發（D，見前言）。C 只有 Scott，E、F 只有 Amundsen。"],["What did Scott and his companions find when they arrived at the South Pole?",["The Norwegian flag.","the Norwegian flag","a Norwegian flag","Norwegian flag","Amundsen's flag","the flag of Norway","they found the Norwegian flag","the Norwegian flag was already there"],"原文：the Norwegian flag was already there。"],["二十世紀初，南極點是地球上少數還沒有人到過的地方。1911 年，兩支探險隊出發爭奪第一。兩位領隊都經驗豐富、意志堅定，也都準備了好幾年。兩位領隊的不同選擇，帶來截然不同的結局。","阿蒙森：挪威探險家阿蒙森曾向北極地區的民族學習如何在酷寒中生存。他的五人隊伍仿效他們，穿寬鬆的毛皮衣、滑雪前進，由 52 隻狗拉雪橇。阿蒙森用長長一排旗子標示糧食存放點，即使在暴風雪中也找得到。他還計畫沿途把一部分的狗餵給其他的狗吃，這個決定殘酷但實際。1911 年 12 月 14 日，挪威人抵達極點並插上國旗。出發九十九天後，五個人全都健康地回到基地。","史考特：英國海軍軍官史考特把希望放在機動雪橇和小馬上，但兩者都失敗了。引擎在低溫中故障，小馬陷進深雪。大部分路程，隊員必須自己拖著沉重的雪橇。和阿蒙森不同，史考特沿途還停下來為科學研究採集岩石樣本。1912 年 1 月 17 日，他和四名同伴終於抵達極點時，挪威國旗已經在那裡了。五人精疲力竭、糧食不足，全都死在回程途中。幾個月後，搜索隊在史考特的遺體旁找到他的日記，那裡距離一個大型補給站只有大約 18 公里。他在最後的留言中寫道，他不後悔這趟旅程。"]],[9,"L","探索博物館新展廳（列舉）","After two years of renovation, the National Museum of Discovery has finally reopened. Its six new halls are highly interactive: visitors are invited not just to look, but to touch, build, and play. Here is what you can expect. Tickets can be booked online, and children under six enter free.",[["A","Ocean Hall","A life-size model of a blue whale hangs from the ceiling. Below it, a shallow pool lets children gently touch starfish and sea cucumbers while guides explain how these animals live. Feeding time is at two o'clock every afternoon."],["B","Sky Dome","Lie back in a soft seat and watch the stars move across a huge curved screen. Shows start every hour and cover the night skies of both the northern and southern halves of the world. Seats fill up quickly, so pick up a free ticket at the entrance as soon as you arrive."],["C","Body Works","Walk through a giant heart, test how fast you react, and find out how much sugar is hidden in your favorite drinks. Many visitors leave this hall promising to eat better."],["D","Time Tunnel","This hall rebuilds a local street from the 1950s, complete with a barber shop, a grocery store, and an old cinema. Visitors can even try on the clothes of the time. For grandparents, it is a trip down memory lane. For children, it is a chance to see how people lived without smartphones."],["E","Tinker Lab","Design a paper plane, build a bridge, or connect a simple circuit, and then test whether it works. Staff members are always on hand to help. Things often fall apart here, and that is exactly the point. Visitors are encouraged to find out what went wrong and try again."],["F","Green Future","See how wind, sunlight, and moving water are turned into electricity. Ride a bicycle to find out how hard you must pedal to light a single lamp, and then design a house that wastes no energy."]],["The National Museum of Discovery has been completely __47__. Instead of simply looking at the exhibits, visitors now learn through __48__ with them.",[[["renovated"],"renovation","has been 後面接過去分詞，把名詞 renovation 改成 renovated。"],[["interaction"],"interactive","介系詞 through 後面要接名詞，把 interactive 改成 interaction。"]]],["Three visitors are at the museum. (1) Lucas wants to feel what a sea animal is like. (2) Mrs. Yeh hopes to see what her hometown looked like when she was a little girl. (3) Nina has to write a health report on sweet drinks. Which halls should they visit? 請選出展廳名稱前的英文大寫字母。",[],"ACD","(1) 摸海洋生物 → Ocean Hall（A）；(2) 看小時候的街景 → Time Tunnel（D）；(3) 飲料含糖量 → Body Works（C）。"],["Which phrase in the description of Time Tunnel means “an experience that brings back memories of the past”?",["a trip down memory lane","trip down memory lane"],"原文：For grandparents, it is a trip down memory lane。"],["經過兩年整修，國立探索博物館終於重新開放。六個新展廳都高度互動：參觀者不只是看，還能摸、動手做和玩。以下是你可以期待的內容。門票可以線上預訂，六歲以下兒童免費入場。","A. Ocean Hall：天花板上懸掛著實際大小的藍鯨模型。下方的淺水池讓孩子輕輕觸摸海星和海參，導覽員同時解說這些動物如何生活。每天下午兩點是餵食時間。","B. Sky Dome：躺在柔軟的座椅上，看星星劃過巨大的弧形螢幕。每小時一場，內容涵蓋南北半球的夜空。座位很快就滿，一到館請先到入口領取免費票券。","C. Body Works：走進一顆巨大的心臟、測試你的反應速度，看看你最愛的飲料裡藏了多少糖。很多人離開這個展廳時都發誓要吃得更健康。","D. Time Tunnel：這個展廳重建了 1950 年代的在地街道，有理髮店、雜貨店和老戲院。參觀者甚至可以試穿當時的衣服。對祖父母來說，這是一趟懷舊之旅。對孩子來說，則是看看沒有智慧型手機的年代人們怎麼生活的機會。","E. Tinker Lab：設計紙飛機、搭一座橋或接一個簡單的電路，再測試它能不能用。工作人員隨時在旁協助。東西在這裡常常會垮掉，而這正是重點。館方鼓勵參觀者找出哪裡出錯，然後再試一次。","F. Green Future：看看風、陽光和流水如何變成電。騎上腳踏車，體驗要踩多用力才能點亮一盞燈，然後設計一棟不浪費能源的房子。"]],[10,"F","高中生該不該打工（論壇）","According to a recent survey at Lakeview High School, nearly one third of its students have had a part-time job. The school newspaper invited students, teachers, and parents to share their views on the issue. Here is a selection of the replies.",[["A","Zoe","Working at a breakfast shop made me more responsible. I learned to show up on time and to deal with difficult customers. No textbook ever taught me that."],["B","Mr. Lai","I see students falling asleep in first period after working late the night before. Their grades slip first, and then their health is harmed."],["C","Eric","My family lives on a tight budget. Thanks to my wages, I can pay my own phone bill and cram school fees without asking my parents for money. I'd rather be at basketball practice, believe me, but it's worth it."],["D","Mrs. Wu","A teenager's job is to study. There will be forty years to work after graduation. Why rush into it?"],["E","Hannah","I'm fine with it during the summer and winter breaks. During the semester? No way. I tried it once in tenth grade and failed two exams."],["F","Jay","Once I started earning my own money, I realized how hard my parents work. Now I think twice before I spend anything."],["G","Coach Tseng","Some employers take advantage of young workers: unpaid overtime, no insurance, sudden schedule changes. Students rarely know their rights. Schools should teach basic labor law before anyone signs a contract."],["H","Lily","Up to eight hours a week sounds reasonable. Beyond that, schoolwork is bound to suffer. My older sister worked twenty hours a week and had to repeat a year."],["I","Sean","My weekend job at a pet shop convinced me to become a vet. Before that, I had no idea what I wanted to do with my life."],["J","Nurse Kuo","Teenagers need eight to nine hours of sleep. Those who work in the evening rarely get more than six, and they often come to my office complaining of headaches."]],["Some people believe that a part-time job gives teenagers a sense of __47__ and helps them understand the value of money. Others argue that working long hours can be __48__ to students' studies and health.",[[["responsibility"],"responsible","a sense of 後面要接名詞，把 Zoe 留言裡的 responsible 改成 responsibility。"],[["harmful"],"harmed","can be ... to 中間要放形容詞，把 Mr. Lai 留言裡的 harmed 改成 harmful。"]]],["From (A) to (J) in the above posts, which ONES mention a benefit of having a part-time job?",[],"ACFI","A 學到負責任，C 能負擔自己的費用，F 懂得父母辛苦、花錢更謹慎，I 找到未來的方向。B、D、G、J 講壞處或反對，E、H 談條件。"],["Which phrase in the posts means “treat someone unfairly for one's own benefit”?",["take advantage of","take advantage of young workers"],"Coach Tseng 說有些雇主 take advantage of young workers，占年輕員工的便宜。"],["根據 Lakeview 高中最近的一項調查，將近三分之一的學生打過工。校刊邀請學生、老師和家長分享對這個議題的看法。以下是部分回覆。","A. Zoe：在早餐店工作讓我更有責任感。我學會準時到，也學會應付難纏的客人，這些課本從來沒教過。","B. Mr. Lai：我看到學生前一晚工作到很晚，第一節課就睡著。先是成績下滑，接著健康也受損。","C. Eric：我家手頭很緊。多虧這份薪水，我可以自己付電話費和補習費，不必跟爸媽拿錢。相信我，我寧可去練籃球，但這樣很值得。","D. Mrs. Wu：青少年的工作就是讀書。畢業之後有四十年可以工作。何必急著開始？","E. Hannah：寒暑假我覺得可以。學期中？不行。我高一試過一次，結果兩科考試不及格。","F. Jay：開始自己賺錢之後，我才明白爸媽工作多辛苦。現在我花任何錢之前都會多想一下。","G. Coach Tseng：有些雇主占年輕員工的便宜：加班不給錢、沒有保險、臨時改班表。學生很少知道自己的權益。學校應該在學生簽約之前先教基本的勞動法規。","H. Lily：一週最多八小時聽起來合理。超過的話，課業一定會受影響。我姊姊一週工作二十小時，結果留級一年。","I. Sean：週末在寵物店打工讓我決定要當獸醫。在那之前，我完全不知道自己這輩子想做什麼。","J. Nurse Kuo：青少年需要八到九小時的睡眠。晚上打工的學生很少睡超過六小時，常常因為頭痛來保健室找我。"]],[11,"P","光的節慶：排燈節與水燈節（雙文）","In many cultures, light stands for hope and a fresh start. Two of Asia's best-loved festivals, Diwali and Loy Krathong, both fill the night with small flames. In recent years, however, both have had to face a modern problem.",[["A","Diwali","Diwali is the biggest festival of the year in India. It lasts five days and falls in October or November; the exact dates change every year because they follow a lunar calendar. The festival celebrates the victory of light over darkness. Markets are crowded for weeks beforehand as families shop for gifts and decorations. Families clean their homes, draw colorful patterns on the floor by the door, and light rows of small clay oil lamps to welcome Lakshmi, the goddess of wealth. People also put on new clothes, exchange sweets, and set off fireworks. Unfortunately, the heavy smoke adds to air pollution, and some cities now restrict the use of fireworks."],["B","Loy Krathong","Loy Krathong takes place in Thailand on the night of the full moon in the twelfth month of the Thai lunar calendar, usually in November. A krathong is a small floating basket, traditionally made from the trunk and leaves of a banana tree and decorated with flowers and a candle. After making a wish, people release their krathongs onto rivers and ponds to thank the goddess of water and to let go of their bad luck. Thousands of tiny lights drifting downstream make a magical sight. In the northern city of Chiang Mai, people also send paper lanterns up into the sky at the same time of year. The next morning, though, workers must remove tons of waste from the water, so people are urged to use only natural materials."]],["Beautiful as they are, Diwali and Loy Krathong have been criticized for leaving the air or the water __47__. As a result, there are now strict __48__ on fireworks in some Indian cities.",[[["polluted"],"pollution","leave + 受詞 + 形容詞，把名詞 pollution 改成過去分詞 polluted。"],[["restrictions"],"restrict","there are 後面要接複數名詞，把動詞 restrict 改成 restrictions。"]]],["From (A) to (F) below, choose the ONES that are true for both Diwali and Loy Krathong.",["Setting off fireworks is a major activity.","Its date is decided by a lunar calendar.","It lasts for several days.","People light lamps or candles during the festival.","A goddess is honored during the festival.","People use parts of the banana tree to celebrate."],"BDE","兩個節日都依農曆（陰曆）訂日期（B）、都點燈或點蠟燭（D）、都敬拜女神（E）。A、C 只有排燈節，F 只有水燈節。"],["Which phrase in the passage about Loy Krathong means “stop holding on to”?",["let go of","to let go of"],"原文：to let go of their bad luck，放下厄運。"],["在許多文化裡，光代表希望和新的開始。亞洲最受喜愛的兩個節慶——排燈節和水燈節——都用點點火光照亮夜晚。不過近年來，兩者都必須面對一個現代的問題。","排燈節：排燈節是印度一年中最盛大的節日，為期五天，落在十月或十一月；因為依照陰曆，確切日期每年不同。這個節日慶祝光明戰勝黑暗。節日前幾週市場就擠滿了人，家家戶戶採買禮物和裝飾品。家家戶戶打掃房屋、在門口的地板上畫出彩色圖案，並點亮一排排小陶土油燈，迎接財富女神 Lakshmi。人們也穿上新衣、互贈甜點、施放煙火。可惜濃煙加重了空氣污染，有些城市現在限制施放煙火。","水燈節：水燈節在泰國陰曆十二月的月圓之夜舉行，通常在十一月。krathong 是一種漂浮的小籃子，傳統上用香蕉樹的樹幹和葉子製成，再用花和蠟燭裝飾。許願之後，人們把水燈放到河流和池塘上，感謝水神，也放下自己的厄運。成千上萬的小光點順流而下，景象如夢似幻。在北部城市清邁，人們在同一時節還會把紙天燈放上天空。但隔天早上，工作人員必須從水裡清出好幾噸垃圾，因此人們被呼籲只使用天然材料。"]],[12,"L","社區暑期志工招募（列舉）","The Greenfield Community Center is looking for teenage volunteers this summer. Whatever your interests are, there is a way for you to make a difference. Read about the six programs below and sign up at the front desk. Most programs take only a few hours a week, and every volunteer will receive a certificate at the end of the summer.",[["A","Story Time","Read picture books to children aged four to seven at the public library on Saturday mornings. You need to be patient and not afraid of using funny voices."],["B","Silver Tech","Many seniors own a smartphone but have no idea how to make a video call or book a doctor's appointment online. Sit down with them one-on-one on Wednesday afternoons and show them how. It takes time, but their smiles when they finally see their grandchildren on the screen make it all worthwhile."],["C","River Guardians","On the first Sunday of each month, we pick up trash along the riverbank. Gloves and bags are provided. Volunteers also record what they find for a university research project. Last year, the records helped persuade the town to ban plastic bags at the riverside market."],["D","Second Bite","Every evening, bakeries and markets throw away food that is still perfectly good. Our volunteers collect unsold bread and vegetables and deliver them to families in need. A bicycle is required."],["E","Homework Buddies","Help elementary school students with their math and English homework after school. As the saying goes, it takes a village to raise a child. Each volunteer is matched with one child for the whole summer."],["F","Trail Keepers","Join a team repairing mountain paths damaged by typhoons. The work involves heavy lifting and two nights of camping, so volunteers must be at least sixteen."]],["The programs call for different qualities and skills. Reading to small children, for example, requires a great deal of __47__. Second Bite, meanwhile, fights food waste through the collection and __48__ of unsold food.",[[["patience"],"patient","a great deal of 後面要接不可數名詞，把 patient 改成 patience。"],[["delivery"],"deliver","和 collection 對稱，要用名詞，把 deliver 改成 delivery。"]]],["Three students want to volunteer. (1) Emily is worried about pollution in local waters. (2) Frank does well in English and enjoys teaching younger kids. (3) Gina wishes her grandmother could use a phone to chat with her, and wants to help other elderly people do the same. Which programs should they sign up for? 請選出活動名稱前的英文大寫字母。",[],"BCE","(1) 河岸淨灘 → River Guardians（C）；(2) 教小學生英文 → Homework Buddies（E）；(3) 教長輩用手機 → Silver Tech（B）。"],["Which phrase in the description of Second Bite means “not having enough food or money”?",["in need","families in need"],"原文：deliver them to families in need，有需要（生活困難）的家庭。"],["Greenfield 社區中心今年夏天要招募青少年志工。不管你的興趣是什麼，都有方法讓你盡一份心力。請閱讀以下六個計畫，並到櫃檯報名。大部分計畫每週只需要幾個小時，暑假結束時每位志工都會拿到證書。","A. Story Time：星期六早上在公共圖書館為四到七歲的孩子讀繪本。你需要有耐心，而且不怕用搞笑的聲音說話。","B. Silver Tech：很多長輩有智慧型手機，卻不知道怎麼打視訊電話或上網掛號。星期三下午坐下來一對一教他們。這需要時間，但他們終於在螢幕上看到孫子孫女時的笑容，會讓一切都值得。","C. River Guardians：每個月第一個星期日，我們沿著河岸撿垃圾，提供手套和袋子。志工也要記錄撿到的東西，供一項大學研究計畫使用。去年，這些紀錄協助說服鎮上禁止河濱市場使用塑膠袋。","D. Second Bite：每天晚上，麵包店和市場都會丟掉還完好的食物。我們的志工收集沒賣完的麵包和蔬菜，送到有需要的家庭。必須自備腳踏車。","E. Homework Buddies：放學後協助小學生寫數學和英文作業。俗話說，養大一個孩子需要全村的力量。每位志工整個暑假固定陪伴一個孩子。","F. Trail Keepers：加入團隊修復被颱風損壞的山徑。工作需要搬重物，還要露營兩晚，所以志工必須年滿十六歲。"]],[13,"F","空檔年值不值得（論壇）","In some countries, many students take a “gap year,” a year off between high school and university, to work, travel, or volunteer. The idea is slowly catching on in Taiwan. An education magazine asked its readers whether a gap year is a good idea.",[["A","Peter","I spent mine working on farms in Hualien and Taitung. I came back knowing that I wanted to study agriculture. No exam could have told me that. My classmates who went straight to college changed majors twice."],["B","Ms. Chang","Students lose their study habits quickly. After a year away from books, many find it hard to keep up in college. Math and English suffer the most."],["C","Andy","A year of travel costs a fortune. It's a luxury for rich kids, not for ordinary families like mine."],["D","Wei","My cousin took a gap year and wasted it playing video games in his bedroom. Without a clear plan, it's just a long vacation. His parents were furious."],["E","Dr. Lin","In my experience as a professor, students who have taken a gap year tend to be more mature. They know why they are in my classroom."],["F","Kate","All my friends would be a year ahead of me. I'd feel left behind. They would graduate and find jobs while I was still in school."],["G","Mrs. Ho","I'd support my daughter if she showed me a detailed plan and paid part of the costs herself. A gap year should be earned, not given."],["H","Ben","Volunteering abroad opened my eyes to other ways of living, and my English improved far more than it ever did in class."],["I","Nina","I worked at the front desk of a hospital for a year before college. Seeing patients every day made me certain that nursing was the right path for me."],["J","Mr. Tsai","Universities here rarely let students delay their entry. If you give up your place, you may have to take the entrance exam all over again."]],["Supporters of the gap year point to benefits such as a clearer goal in life and great __47__ in language skills. Critics, however, warn that without careful planning, the year may turn out to be a __48__ of time and money.",[[["improvement"],"improved","great ... in 中間要放名詞，把 Ben 留言裡的 improved 改成 improvement。"],[["waste"],"wasted","a ... of time 要用名詞，把 Wei 留言裡的 wasted 改成 waste。"]]],["From (A) to (J) in the above posts, which ONES express doubts or worries about taking a gap year?",[],"BCDFJ","B 擔心跟不上課業，C 嫌太貴，D 認為沒計畫就是浪費，F 怕落後同學，J 擔心失去入學名額。A、E、H、I 肯定空檔年，G 是有條件支持。"],["Which phrase in the posts means “is extremely expensive”?",["costs a fortune","cost a fortune"],"Andy 說 A year of travel costs a fortune，cost a fortune 是「花一大筆錢」。"],["在某些國家，許多學生會在高中和大學之間休息一年，稱為「空檔年」，用來工作、旅行或當志工。這個想法在臺灣慢慢流行起來。一份教育雜誌問讀者：空檔年是不是個好主意？","A. Peter：我的空檔年在花蓮和臺東的農場工作。回來之後我知道自己想讀農業，這是任何考試都沒辦法告訴我的。直接上大學的同學反而轉了兩次系。","B. Ms. Chang：學生很快就會失去讀書習慣。離開書本一年後，很多人上大學會跟不上。數學和英文受的影響最大。","C. Andy：旅行一年要花一大筆錢。那是有錢人家小孩的奢侈品，不是我們這種普通家庭負擔得起的。","D. Wei：我表哥休了空檔年，結果都窩在房間打電動浪費掉了。沒有明確的計畫，那只是一個長假。他爸媽氣炸了。","E. Dr. Lin：以我當教授的經驗，休過空檔年的學生通常比較成熟，他們知道自己為什麼坐在我的教室裡。","F. Kate：我所有的朋友都會比我早一年，我會覺得自己落後了。我還在讀書的時候，他們就畢業找到工作了。","G. Mrs. Ho：如果女兒拿出詳細的計畫，並且自己負擔一部分費用，我會支持她。空檔年應該是自己爭取來的，不是別人給的。","H. Ben：到國外當志工讓我看見其他的生活方式，英文進步的幅度也遠超過在課堂上。","I. Nina：上大學之前，我在醫院櫃檯工作了一年。每天看到病人，讓我確定護理是適合我的路。","J. Mr. Tsai：這裡的大學很少讓學生延後入學。如果放棄名額，你可能得從頭再考一次入學考試。"]],[14,"P","會耍心機的植物：捕蠅草與泰坦魔芋（雙文）","Plants cannot run after their food or travel to find a partner. Some of them, however, have developed surprising tricks to make insects serve their needs. The Venus flytrap and the titan arum are two famous examples.",[["A","The Venus Flytrap","The Venus flytrap grows wild only in the wetlands of North and South Carolina in the United States. Because the soil there is poor, the plant gets extra nutrients by catching insects. Each trap is a leaf with tiny hairs on its inner surface. One touch is not enough to set it off: the trap closes only if the hairs are touched twice within about twenty seconds. This saves the plant from wasting energy on raindrops. When the second touch comes, the leaf snaps shut in about a tenth of a second. Very small insects can still slip out through the gaps along its edge, since they are not worth the effort. As a larger insect struggles, the trap seals tightly and releases digestive juices. About a week later, it opens again, and only an empty shell is left."],["B","The Titan Arum","The titan arum grows wild only in the rainforests of Sumatra, Indonesia. Its bloom can reach three meters in height, but it appears only once every several years and lasts just a day or two. To make the most of this brief chance, the plant gives off a powerful smell of rotting meat and even heats itself up so that the smell travels farther. Flies and beetles that normally lay their eggs on dead animals are fooled. They crawl inside, pick up pollen, and carry it to another bloom, getting nothing in return. Whenever one blooms in a botanical garden, thousands of visitors line up, holding their noses, to see it."]],["The Venus flytrap catches insects and __47__ them to make up for the poor soil it lives in. The titan arum, on the other hand, uses its terrible smell to __48__ flies and beetles into carrying its pollen.",[[["digests"],"digestive","和 catches 對稱，要用第三人稱單數動詞，把 digestive 改成 digests。"],[["fool","trick"],"fooled","to 後面接原形動詞，把 fooled 改成 fool（fool A into V-ing 騙 A 去做某事）；把前言裡的 tricks 改成動詞 trick 也可以。"]]],["From (A) to (F) below, choose the ONES that are true for both the Venus flytrap and the titan arum.",["It can be found in the wild in only one limited area.","It feeds on the insects it attracts.","It reacts when it is touched.","It produces a strong smell.","It makes use of insects, which gain nothing from it.","It is able to raise its own temperature."],"AE","兩種植物都只在一個地區野生（A），都利用昆蟲而昆蟲得不到好處（E）。B、C 只有捕蠅草，D、F 只有泰坦魔芋。"],["What does the bloom of the titan arum smell like?",["Rotting meat.","rotting meat","like rotting meat","It smells like rotting meat","the smell of rotting meat","a powerful smell of rotting meat","rotten meat","dead animals"],"原文：gives off a powerful smell of rotting meat。"],["植物不能追著食物跑，也不能出遠門找伴侶。不過有些植物發展出令人驚訝的手段，讓昆蟲為它們服務。捕蠅草和泰坦魔芋是兩個著名的例子。","捕蠅草：捕蠅草只野生於美國北卡羅來納州和南卡羅來納州的溼地。因為那裡土壤貧瘠，它靠捕捉昆蟲取得額外的養分。每個捕蟲夾是一片葉子，內側表面有細毛。碰一下不足以觸發：細毛必須在大約二十秒內被碰到兩次，夾子才會關上，這讓它不會把能量浪費在雨滴上。第二次碰觸一出現，葉片就在大約十分之一秒內猛然闔上。非常小的昆蟲還是可以從邊緣的縫隙溜走，因為牠們不值得花這個力氣。較大的昆蟲掙扎時，夾子會緊緊密合並釋出消化液。大約一週後它重新打開，只剩下一個空殼。","泰坦魔芋：泰坦魔芋只野生於印尼蘇門答臘的雨林。它的花可以高達三公尺，但好幾年才開一次，而且只開一兩天。為了善用這短暫的機會，它會散發強烈的腐肉氣味，甚至讓自己升溫，好讓氣味傳得更遠。平常在動物屍體上產卵的蒼蠅和甲蟲因此上當，爬進去沾上花粉，再帶到另一朵花，卻什麼回報也沒有。每當植物園裡有一株開花，總有成千上萬的遊客捏著鼻子排隊來看。"]],[15,"L","港口美食節餐車（列舉）","The Harbor Food Festival returns this weekend with more than forty food trucks lined up along the waterfront. If you cannot decide where to start, here are seven trucks that regular visitors recommend. Entry is free, and most trucks stay open until ten at night.",[["A","Mama Lu's Kitchen","Braised pork rice and three-cup chicken are cooked from a family recipe handed down for three generations. One bite will remind you of dinner at your grandmother's house. Portions are generous, so come hungry."],["B","Veggie Voyage","Everything on the menu is made from plants: no meat, no eggs, no milk. The mushroom burger is so juicy that even meat lovers come back for seconds. The owner, who used to plan meals for a hospital, promises that every dish is as healthy as it is tasty."],["C","Seoul Bites","Korean fried chicken and spicy rice cakes are served at five levels of heat. Be warned: level five is not for the faint of heart."],["D","Sweet Cloud","This truck makes ice cream by hand with fruit from nearby farms. Mango, lychee, guava: the flavors depend on what is in season. Come early, because the most popular ones are gone by mid-afternoon."],["E","The Daily Catch","The owner buys seafood at the harbor every morning, so the grilled squid and fish soup are as fresh as they can be. When everything is sold out, the truck closes for the day."],["F","Bean There","Coffee beans are roasted right on the truck. While you wait, the owner gives free ten-minute lessons on how to brew a perfect cup at home. Iced drinks are available for those who cannot stand the heat."],["G","Taco Loco","Soft corn tortillas are filled with grilled beef, fresh lime, and a sauce made on the spot. The owner learned the recipes during the three years she spent in Mexico."]],["The food trucks at the festival serve everything from traditional local dishes to foreign flavors. Several of them take pride in the __47__ of their ingredients, and Sweet Cloud even offers a __48__ menu that changes throughout the year.",[[["freshness"],"fresh","the ... of 中間要放名詞，把 The Daily Catch 介紹裡的 fresh 改成 freshness。"],[["seasonal"],"season","a ... menu 中間要放形容詞，把 Sweet Cloud 介紹裡的 season 改成 seasonal。"]]],["Three friends are visiting the festival. (1) Helen never eats any animal products. (2) Ian wants to learn how to make good coffee. (3) Judy misses the taste of home-style Taiwanese cooking. Which trucks should they go to? 請選出餐車名稱前的英文大寫字母。",[],"ABF","(1) 全植物餐點 → Veggie Voyage（B）；(2) 學沖咖啡 → Bean There（F）；(3) 臺式家常菜 → Mama Lu's Kitchen（A）。"],["Which phrase in the description of Seoul Bites refers to “people who are easily frightened”?",["the faint of heart","faint of heart"],"原文：level five is not for the faint of heart，膽小的人不適合。"],["港口美食節這個週末回來了，四十多輛餐車沿著水岸排開。如果你不知道從哪裡開始，以下是常客推薦的七輛餐車。入場免費，大部分餐車營業到晚上十點。","A. Mama Lu's Kitchen：滷肉飯和三杯雞用的是傳了三代的家傳食譜。吃一口就會想起在奶奶家吃晚餐的味道。份量很大，記得空著肚子來。","B. Veggie Voyage：菜單上的所有東西都是植物做的：沒有肉、沒有蛋、沒有牛奶。香菇漢堡多汁到連愛吃肉的人都會回來再點一份。老闆以前在醫院規劃餐點，她保證每道菜都既健康又好吃。","C. Seoul Bites：韓式炸雞和辣炒年糕有五種辣度。先提醒你：第五級不適合膽小的人。","D. Sweet Cloud：這輛餐車用附近農場的水果手工製作冰淇淋。芒果、荔枝、芭樂，口味要看當季產什麼。請早點來，最受歡迎的口味下午過半就賣完了。","E. The Daily Catch：老闆每天早上到港口買海鮮，所以烤魷魚和魚湯新鮮得不能再新鮮。全部賣完，餐車當天就收攤。","F. Bean There：咖啡豆就在餐車上烘。等待的時候，老闆會免費教你十分鐘，如何在家沖出一杯完美的咖啡。怕熱的人也可以點冰飲。","G. Taco Loco：柔軟的玉米餅包著烤牛肉、新鮮萊姆和現做的醬汁。老闆在墨西哥住了三年，食譜就是那時學來的。"]],[16,"F","你會買二手衣嗎（論壇）","Fast fashion has made clothes cheaper than ever, but mountains of unwanted clothing end up as waste every year. Buying second-hand is often suggested as a better option. A lifestyle website asked its readers: “Would you buy used clothes?”",[["A","Anna","Absolutely. I once found a wool coat for a tenth of its original price. Shopping at vintage stores feels like a treasure hunt. You never know what you will find."],["B","Tom","I can't get past the idea that a stranger has worn it. Even after it's been washed, it gives me the creeps. I feel the same way about used shoes."],["C","Vicky","Only if it's something special, like a designer jacket I could never afford new. For everyday T-shirts and socks, I'd rather buy new ones."],["D","Ken","Making a single pair of jeans takes thousands of liters of water. Giving clothes a second life is the least we can do for the planet. I haven't bought anything new in two years."],["E","Mei","The sizes are a headache. You find the perfect shirt, but you can't choose your size, because there is only one, and it's never mine."],["F","Rosa","Honestly, a lot of what people donate is worn out or stained. Sometimes “second-hand” is just a polite word for garbage. At the charity shop where I volunteer, we throw away half of what we receive."],["G","Carl","My kids outgrow their clothes in six months. Swapping with other parents has saved us a lot of money. The children don't seem to notice the difference."],["H","Lena","Every piece in a second-hand shop is unique. Nobody at school will ever show up wearing the same thing as me."],["I","Bella","Clothes made twenty years ago were built to last. My mother's old denim jacket still looks better than anything I've bought this year."],["J","Sunny","Buying used clothes online is risky. The color in the photo never matches what arrives, and sellers usually refuse to take anything back."]],["Fans of second-hand clothing are attracted by the low prices and the __47__ of each piece, and some also see it as a way to protect the environment. Those who avoid it complain about poor quality and the lack of __48__ when it comes to sizes.",[[["uniqueness"],"unique","the ... of 中間要放名詞，把 Lena 留言裡的 unique 改成 uniqueness。"],[["choice","choices"],"choose","the lack of 後面要接名詞，把 Mei 留言裡的 choose 改成 choice（複數 choices 也可以）。"]]],["From (A) to (J) in the above posts, which ONES show a positive attitude toward second-hand clothes?",[],"ADGHI","A 便宜又有尋寶的樂趣，D 環保，G 省錢，H 獨一無二，I 舊衣品質好。B、E、F、J 是負面看法，C 只在特殊情況才買。"],["Which phrase in the posts means “makes me feel uncomfortable or disgusted”?",["gives me the creeps","give me the creeps","it gives me the creeps"],"Tom 說別人穿過的衣服 gives me the creeps，讓他渾身不舒服。"],["快時尚讓衣服比以往都便宜，但每年有堆積如山的衣物變成垃圾。買二手常被認為是更好的選擇。一個生活風格網站問讀者：「你會買二手衣嗎？」","A. Anna：當然會。我曾經用原價的十分之一買到一件羊毛大衣。逛古著店就像在尋寶。你永遠不知道會找到什麼。","B. Tom：我過不了「陌生人穿過」這一關。就算洗過，還是讓我渾身不舒服。二手鞋也給我同樣的感覺。","C. Vicky：只有特別的東西才會，例如我永遠買不起全新的設計師外套。日常的 T 恤和襪子，我寧可買新的。","D. Ken：做一條牛仔褲要用掉幾千公升的水。讓衣服有第二次生命，是我們至少能為地球做的事。我已經兩年沒買新衣服了。","E. Mei：尺寸很讓人頭痛。你找到一件完美的襯衫，卻不能選尺寸，因為只有一件，而且永遠不是我的尺寸。","F. Rosa：老實說，很多人捐出來的東西不是穿壞了就是有污漬。有時候「二手」只是垃圾的客氣說法。在我當志工的慈善商店，收到的東西有一半得丟掉。","G. Carl：我的小孩六個月就穿不下了。和其他家長交換衣服幫我們省了很多錢。孩子們似乎也沒注意到有什麼不同。","H. Lena：二手店裡的每一件都獨一無二。學校裡不會有人和我撞衫。","I. Bella：二十年前做的衣服很耐穿。我媽媽的舊牛仔外套，看起來還是比我今年買的任何衣服都好。","J. Sunny：在網路上買二手衣很冒險。照片裡的顏色和收到的永遠不一樣，賣家通常也不接受退貨。"]],[17,"P","單車城市：波哥大與哥本哈根（雙文）","Traffic jams and dirty air trouble big cities everywhere. To deal with these problems, two cities on opposite sides of the world have turned to the same simple machine: the bicycle.",[["A","Bogotá","Every Sunday and public holiday, from seven in the morning to two in the afternoon, the capital of Colombia closes more than 120 kilometers of its main roads to cars and hands them over to cyclists, runners, and skaters. The event, known as the Ciclovía, began in 1974 as a small protest organized by citizens. Today, it draws more than a million people every week. Free exercise classes are held in parks along the route, and hundreds of cities around the world have copied the idea. For many families in poorer districts, it is the only chance to enjoy so much open space. On weekdays, however, the same roads are once again packed with cars."],["B","Copenhagen","In the Danish capital, cycling is a way of life every day of the week. The city has built hundreds of kilometers of bike lanes separated from car traffic, as well as bridges for bicycles only. On some main streets, traffic lights are timed so that cyclists traveling at 20 kilometers an hour get green lights all the way. As a result, nearly half of all trips to work or school in the city are made by bike, even in winter, when snow is cleared from bike lanes before car lanes. In the city center, bicycles now outnumber cars. Most residents say they ride not to save the planet but because it is the fastest way to get around."]],["Bogotá's Ciclovía is a __47__ event that gives citizens a break from car traffic. Copenhagen, on the other hand, makes daily cycling safe by __48__ bike lanes from the roads used by cars.",[[["weekly"],"week","a ... event 中間要放形容詞，把 week 改成 weekly（每週一次的）。"],[["separating"],"separated","介系詞 by 後面接 V-ing，把 separated 改成 separating。"]]],["From (A) to (F) below, choose the ONES that are true for both Bogotá and Copenhagen.",["Cycling there started as a form of protest.","The city offers cyclists road space that they do not have to share with cars.","Main roads are closed to cars every day.","A large number of people ride bikes in the city.","Its car-free event has been copied by cities around the world.","People there keep cycling even when it snows."],"BD","兩個城市都給單車不必和汽車共用的空間（B），也都有大量的人騎車（D）。A、E 只有波哥大的段落提到，F 只有哥本哈根，C 兩者都不是。"],["Which phrase in the passage about Copenhagen means “a normal part of everyday living”?",["a way of life","way of life"],"原文：cycling is a way of life，騎單車是一種生活方式。"],["塞車和骯髒的空氣困擾著各地的大城市。為了處理這些問題，地球兩端的兩座城市都求助於同一種簡單的機器：腳踏車。","波哥大：每個星期日和國定假日，從早上七點到下午兩點，哥倫比亞首都會封閉超過 120 公里的主要道路，不讓汽車進入，交給騎車、跑步和溜冰的人。這項活動稱為 Ciclovía，1974 年從市民發起的小型抗議開始，如今每週吸引超過一百萬人。沿線的公園有免費的運動課程，全世界有數百個城市仿效這個點子。對較貧窮地區的許多家庭來說，這是唯一能享受這麼大片開放空間的機會。不過到了平日，同樣的道路又擠滿了汽車。","哥本哈根：在丹麥首都，騎單車是每天的生活方式。這座城市建了數百公里和汽車分隔的自行車道，還有單車專用橋。在某些主要街道，紅綠燈的時間經過設計，讓時速 20 公里的騎士一路綠燈。因此，市內將近一半的通勤通學是騎單車，連冬天也一樣，下雪時自行車道比汽車道先除雪。在市中心，腳踏車的數量現在比汽車還多。多數居民說，他們騎車不是為了拯救地球，而是因為這是最快的移動方式。"]],[18,"L","暑期營隊簡介（列舉）","School is almost out, and the Sunrise Youth Center has prepared six camps to keep teenagers busy this summer. Each camp lasts one to two weeks. Take a look and find the one that is right for you. Places are limited, and those who sign up before June 1 get a ten percent discount.",[["A","Code Camp","Design and build your own mobile game in two weeks. Laptops are provided, and you don't need to know anything about programming before you come. The best games will be shown at the center's open day."],["B","Wild Week","Spend five days in the mountains learning to set up a tent, read a map, and cook over a fire. Phones are collected on the first day. It is a rare chance to unplug. At night, campers sleep under the stars and learn to find their way without a compass."],["C","Speak Up","Does your mind go blank in front of a crowd? This camp will build your confidence through games, debates, and daily practice. On the last day, every camper gives a speech to an audience of parents."],["D","Kitchen Science","Why does bread rise? Why does chocolate melt in your hand? Find out by doing experiments you can eat. Campers take the recipes home and are encouraged to cook for their families."],["E","Ocean Explorers","Go snorkeling over coral reefs, learn to identify fish, and join a beach cleanup. For safety reasons, campers are required to swim fifty meters without help. Last year's campers removed over two hundred kilograms of trash from the shore."],["F","Young Reporters","Interview local shop owners, take photos, and write your own stories. At the end of the camp, your work will be published in a community newspaper. Several past campers have gone on to write for their school magazines."]],["The camps welcome most teenagers, although Ocean Explorers has a swimming __47__ for those who wish to join. Speak Up, meanwhile, is designed for students who hope to become more __48__ speakers.",[[["requirement"],"required","a swimming 後面要接名詞，把 required 改成 requirement。"],[["confident"],"confidence","more ... speakers 中間要放形容詞，把 confidence 改成 confident。"]]],["Three students are choosing a camp. (1) Karen dreams of becoming a journalist. (2) Leo's parents think he spends too much time on his phone and want him to enjoy nature. (3) Molly wonders why a cake gets bigger in the oven. Which camps should they join? 請選出營隊名稱前的英文大寫字母。",[],"BDF","(1) 採訪寫作 → Young Reporters（F）；(2) 上山且收手機 → Wild Week（B）；(3) 食物裡的科學 → Kitchen Science（D）。"],["Which word in the description of Wild Week means “to stop using electronic devices for a period of time”?",["unplug","to unplug"],"原文：It is a rare chance to unplug，unplug 原意是拔掉插頭，這裡指暫時不用 3C。"],["學期快結束了，Sunrise 青少年中心準備了六個營隊，讓青少年這個暑假過得充實。每個營隊為期一到兩週。看一看，找出適合你的那一個。名額有限，6 月 1 日前報名可享九折。","A. Code Camp：用兩週設計並做出自己的手機遊戲。提供筆電，來之前不需要懂任何程式。最好的遊戲會在中心的開放日展出。","B. Wild Week：在山上待五天，學搭帳篷、看地圖、生火煮飯。第一天就會收手機，這是難得可以遠離 3C 的機會。晚上學員睡在星空下，並學習不靠指南針辨認方向。","C. Speak Up：站在人群前腦袋就一片空白嗎？這個營隊透過遊戲、辯論和每天的練習建立你的自信。最後一天，每位學員都要對家長發表演說。","D. Kitchen Science：麵包為什麼會膨脹？巧克力為什麼會在手裡融化？做可以吃的實驗來找答案。學員會把食譜帶回家，中心也鼓勵他們煮給家人吃。","E. Ocean Explorers：在珊瑚礁上浮潛、學認魚，並參加淨灘。基於安全，學員必須能獨力游五十公尺。去年的學員從岸邊清走了超過兩百公斤的垃圾。","F. Young Reporters：採訪在地店家、拍照，寫自己的報導。營隊結束時，你的作品會刊登在社區報上。有幾位過去的學員後來為校刊寫稿。"]],[19,"F","太空旅遊值得嗎（論壇）","Private companies have begun flying paying passengers to the edge of space. A ticket can cost hundreds of thousands of US dollars, and the flight may last only minutes. A science magazine opened a forum on the question: Is space tourism worth it?",[["A","Leo","Seeing Earth from above changes people. Astronauts say that borders disappear and you realize how fragile our planet is. More people should have that experience. If world leaders took the trip, they might make wiser decisions."],["B","Dana","Each rocket launch sends tons of pollution into the upper atmosphere. All that so a billionaire can float around for four minutes? The planet can't afford such expensive toys."],["C","Raj","Every new technology starts out as a toy for the rich. Air travel was once only for the wealthy, and now students fly abroad on a budget. Give it time."],["D","Mina","When so many people still go hungry, spending a fortune on a joyride is hard to defend. Imagine how many school lunches one ticket could buy."],["E","Felix","The money comes from private pockets, not from taxes. Besides, the reusable rockets built for tourists are making it cheaper to launch satellites, which benefits science."],["F","Ruth","I'd go in a heartbeat, if it ever costs about the same as a trip to Europe. Until then, I'll just watch the videos."],["G","Joe","Space is dangerous, and tourists get only a few days of training. One deadly accident could finish the whole industry. Do passengers really know what could go wrong?"],["H","Elsa","Kids who watch these launches grow up wanting to be engineers and scientists. You can't put a price on inspiration. I became an engineer because of a launch I watched at the age of eight."],["I","Omar","A rocket that carries six tourists could be carrying a telescope or a weather satellite instead. It is a waste of a limited resource."],["J","Nell","Let people spend their own money as they like. Nobody complains when the rich buy yachts, and at least space flights teach us something new."]],["People who defend space tourism believe that it will advance technology and __47__ the next generation. Those against it stress the damage done to the atmosphere and the __48__ that passengers have to face.",[[["inspire"],"inspiration","will advance ... and 後面要接原形動詞，把 Elsa 留言裡的 inspiration 改成 inspire。"],[["danger","dangers","risk","risks"],"dangerous","the 後面要接名詞，把 Joe 留言裡的 dangerous 改成 danger（複數 dangers 也可以；寫 risk、risks 意思也通）。"]]],["From (A) to (J) in the above forum, which ONES speak in defense of space tourism?",[],"ACEHJ","A 改變人的視野，C 新科技都先從有錢人開始，E 用的是私人的錢而且有助科學，H 啟發下一代，J 花自己的錢無妨。B、D、G、I 反對，F 只說自己便宜了才去。"],["Which phrase in the forum means “immediately, without stopping to think”?",["in a heartbeat"],"Ruth 說 I'd go in a heartbeat，in a heartbeat 是「毫不猶豫、立刻」。"],["私人公司已經開始載付費乘客飛到太空邊緣。一張票可能要價數十萬美元，而飛行可能只有幾分鐘。一份科學雜誌開了論壇討論：太空旅遊值得嗎？","A. Leo：從上方看地球會改變一個人。太空人說，國界消失了，你會明白我們的星球有多脆弱。應該讓更多人有這種體驗。如果各國領袖也去一趟，也許會做出更明智的決定。","B. Dana：每一次火箭發射都把好幾噸污染物送進高層大氣。就為了讓一個億萬富翁飄四分鐘？地球負擔不起這麼昂貴的玩具。","C. Raj：每一種新科技一開始都是有錢人的玩具。搭飛機曾經只屬於富人，現在學生也能用小預算飛出國。給它一點時間。","D. Mina：還有這麼多人挨餓，花一大筆錢去兜風實在很難說得過去。想想一張票可以買多少份學校午餐。","E. Felix：錢是私人口袋出的，不是稅金。而且為觀光客打造的可重複使用火箭，正讓發射衛星變得更便宜，對科學有好處。","F. Ruth：我會毫不猶豫地去——如果哪天價格和去一趟歐洲差不多的話。在那之前，我看影片就好。","G. Joe：太空很危險，而觀光客只受幾天訓練。一次致命的意外就可能讓整個產業完蛋。乘客真的知道可能出什麼事嗎？","H. Elsa：看著這些發射長大的孩子，會想當工程師和科學家。啟發是無價的。我就是因為八歲時看了一場發射才成為工程師的。","I. Omar：載六名觀光客的火箭，本來可以載一架望遠鏡或一顆氣象衛星。這是在浪費有限的資源。","J. Nell：讓人們隨自己的意思花自己的錢吧。有錢人買遊艇沒人抱怨，太空飛行至少還能讓我們學到新東西。"]],[20,"P","在體育館誕生的運動：籃球與排球（雙文）","Two of the world's most popular sports were born only four years apart in two neighboring towns in Massachusetts, USA. Both were invented by young teachers who simply wanted to solve a problem in their gym classes.",[["A","Basketball","In December 1891, James Naismith, a physical education teacher at a YMCA training school in Springfield, needed an indoor game to keep a restless class busy during the harsh winter. He nailed a peach basket to the balcony at each end of the gym, about three meters above the floor, and wrote down thirteen rules. Players could not run with the ball; they had to pass it. Every time someone scored, the game stopped so that the ball could be taken out of the basket, until someone finally cut out the bottoms. Naismith's students loved the game at once, and within weeks crowds were gathering to watch. The game spread rapidly through YMCAs across the country and became an Olympic sport in 1936. Naismith himself was there to watch the first Olympic game."],["B","Volleyball","In 1895, William Morgan, a YMCA director in the nearby town of Holyoke, noticed that basketball was too rough for the older businessmen in his classes. He wanted a gentler game with no physical contact. Borrowing a net from tennis, he raised it to about two meters and had two teams hit a ball back and forth over it. Morgan called his game “Mintonette.” A teacher watching an early match suggested a better name, since the players seemed to be volleying the ball. Unlike basketball, the new game could be enjoyed by people of almost any age. Volleyball joined the Olympic Games in 1964. Today, it is played by hundreds of millions of people, on beaches as well as in gyms."]],["Both basketball and volleyball were created to meet the needs of gym classes, and both soon gained great __47__. Interestingly, the name “volleyball” was not Morgan's own idea but the __48__ of another teacher.",[[["popularity"],"popular","gained great 後面要接名詞，把 popular 改成 popularity。"],[["suggestion"],"suggested","the ... of 中間要放名詞，把 suggested 改成 suggestion。"]]],["From (A) to (F) below, choose the ONES that are true for both basketball and volleyball.",["It was invented by someone working for the YMCA.","It was designed for older players.","Its first equipment was made from objects meant for other uses.","It is still known by the name its inventor gave it.","It later became an Olympic sport.","It was first played outdoors."],"ACE","兩位發明者都在 YMCA 工作（A），最早的器材都借用別的東西：桃子籃和網球網（C），後來都成為奧運項目（E）。B 只有排球；D：排球後來改了名字，不符合；F 兩者都不是。"],["What was volleyball called when it was first invented?",["Mintonette.","Mintonette","It was called Mintonette"],"原文：Morgan called his game “Mintonette.”"],["世界上最受歡迎的兩種運動，誕生在美國麻薩諸塞州兩個相鄰的城鎮，前後只差四年。發明者都是年輕的老師，他們只是想解決體育課上的一個問題。","籃球：1891 年 12 月，在春田市一所 YMCA 訓練學校教體育的 James Naismith，需要一種室內遊戲，讓一班坐不住的學生在嚴冬裡有事可做。他在體育館兩端的看臺上各釘了一個桃子籃，離地約三公尺，並寫下十三條規則。球員不能拿著球跑，必須傳球。每次有人得分，比賽就得暫停把球從籃子裡拿出來，直到終於有人把籃底挖掉。Naismith 的學生馬上愛上這個遊戲，幾週之內就有人群聚集觀看。這項運動很快透過全國各地的 YMCA 傳開，並在 1936 年成為奧運項目。Naismith 本人也到場觀看了第一場奧運籃球賽。","排球：1895 年，在附近 Holyoke 鎮擔任 YMCA 主任的 William Morgan 發現，籃球對他班上年紀較大的商人來說太激烈了。他想要一種比較溫和、沒有身體碰撞的運動。他借來網球的網子，升高到大約兩公尺，讓兩隊把球在網子上方來回拍擊。Morgan 把這個運動叫做「Mintonette」。一位觀看早期比賽的老師建議了一個更好的名字，因為球員看起來像在截擊（volley）球。和籃球不同，這項新運動幾乎任何年齡的人都能享受。排球在 1964 年加入奧運。如今有數億人打排球，在體育館裡，也在沙灘上。"]]]};
